      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Resignation of Mr, T. J. Garnett.</head>
        <p>Resignation of Mr, T. J. Garnett.</p>
        <p>The fortnight's,- meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians ssas held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, Rev. T. R. Matthews presiding. There were also present :—Mrs. Simpson, Dr. Murray, Messrs. G. Mather, J. Billing., A. J. Jolley, A. Brooks, W. Furness W. C. Brooks, It. Lee, R. Bolton, M. %V. Seton, J. Cook, T. Ritchie A. Bottomley (clerk). R. W. Peacock (assistant clerk), and A. Wright (superintendent of the Cottage Homes). ORGANIST'S RF.SIGNATION. At a meeting of the House Committee held on May 14th a letter was read from Miss Mary Crampton. 58, Parker-street, stating that owing to her approaching marriagii and leaving Warrington to reside at Colwyn Bay, she had resigned her position as organist to the Free Church Council at the Workhonse. It was recom- mended that the best thanks of the Guardians be con- veyed to Miss Crampton for the honorary services rendered by her as organist at the Workhouse when services had been held by members of the Free Church Council. It was recommended that Mrs. Daman, the -Vicarage, Walton, be appointed a member of the Ladies Visiting Committee and that Mrs. Dutton Fairelough, of Kellett, Grappenhall, and Mrs. N. Davis, of Vine Cottage, Grappenhall, be appointed members of the Brabazon Ladies Committee. The minutes were appointed. - N aINTWISNCE ARREARS. Mr. Brnmsact's said that when the collectors! returns were submitted at the last meeting he wes impressed with the enormous amount of arrears. He would like to know how they had accumulated because he under- stood that under the new arrangement it would not be possible for any contributor to be more than a month in arrears. Mr. A. BROOKS: Without the committee knowing'. 'There are some who are six months in arrears. The CLERK said the return included, all old arrears. 'Under the new arrangement any contributor who was a month in arrears must be reported by the collector to the Guardians and in every case that had been carried out. The Guardiaes were therefore in a position to know how tbe cases actually stood and to make any inquiries as to the circumstances of the contributors. PUBLIC VACCINATORS. The CLERK read a letter from the Cliorlion Guardians asking the Board to support a resolution making repre- sentations to the Local Governmont Board in favour of all medical men being allowed to act as district. :medical officers and public vaccinators and to be paid according to a scale fined by the Guardians. It wasbdecided to refer the matter to the Association of Poor Law Unions. Mr. A. BROOKS asked how such an alteration would affect their present medical officers. Would it be a case of compensating them for loss of office or of continuing their services at, the usual salary while the other medical men in the town did their work? The CLERK: There would have to be legislation to alter it first, of all. If they decided that the present I%Tedical Officers should vacate their position, they would have to be compensated and • any medical man who came to reside within the Union would lie a public vaccinator and district medical officer. Mr. A. BROOKS: There would be a scramble amongst them for the work. RESIGNATION OF MR. T. J. G ARNETT. The following letter was received from Mr. T. J. Garnett, the relieving, officer for No. 1 Relief District of the Union :—" Tt is with feelings of regret that I tender you my resignation of the office of Relieving Officer for the No. 1 District. As no doubt you are well aware, during the past two years. owing to slack- ness of trade in the town, the work devolving noon me has been very heavy, and although I have conscien- tiously tried to carry out my duties faithfullyany health is such that I am unable to bear the strain any longer, and I reluctantly place my resignation in your hands. A sregards the superannuation allowance to be made to me, I hope the Guardians when fixing same will kindly take into consideration the large amount of extra work I have had to pbrform both in regard to relief duties and the work put upon, me by the Old Age Pensions Act. If you can see your way to add a few years to my present service I shall be ever grateful, and I feel that .1 am deserving of some consideration. I cannot sever my connection without expressing my appreciation of the kindness and courtesy shown to me at all time by the Guardians and officers during my term of office extending aver a pesiod of 293-, years. I remain, ladies and gentlemen, your obedient servants T. J. Garnett, relieving officer.' On the motion of Mr. W. C. Brooks, seconded by Mr. Billing°, the resignation was accepted, and the con- sideration of the question of superannuation and the appointment of a successor was referred to the Finance Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Maurin's fountains, which were billed a week ago but did not appear, form a great attraction this week. Falling water forms one of nature's most lovely spectacles, but Mons Maurin has improved on nature. His fountains spring from the stage, and spool high in the air, whilst electric lights in all colours play from above on the sparkling water. The effect is surpassingly beautiful, and with constant variety in shape and colour, the fountains seem incapable.of tiring the eye. At the close, real live "water nymphs" are introduced with pleasing effect. Carney and Armstrong are comedians with ideas •and ability, Their turn is one of the most amusing and original submitted for some time, and well deserved the spirited recalls of a gratified audience, Miss Norman Grant appears in a role seldom attempted by ladies, viz.: that of a society entertainer at the piano. She puts plenty of "go " into her work but it is getting time such a song as "It did go" was shelved. Fortunately Miss Grant's repertoire is fairly extensive and contains other good numbers. Lola Patey sings very sweetly as a character comedienne, her impersonations of the Dutch and Irish girl being full of merit. Amos Howard is a coloured sand dancer, who can step it with the best. Ward and Davies produce an amusing sketch "Wanted a Typist," which winds up .with some smart dancing. Jessie Wilton, chorous , comedienne, has some good songs, which appeal to the "house." The orchestra and the biograph play their accustomed parts and,, as usual, play them well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</p>
        <p>Special Whitsuntide attractions have been secure:l for next week. Patrons of the Palace will be glad of the opportunity of again hearing Bella and Bijou, the famous society entertainers, who are great favourites with Warrington audiences. Other artistes will be : —Brothers Ashley, in their comedy boxing sketch Next Please" Sira, marvellous One-legged acrobat; Fred Ward, comedian; Toff Noel, comedian and eccentric dancer; Daisy Hurdle, character comedienne; Society Quartette, vocalists and speciality dancers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The swimming season has again commenced and Warrington and Runcorn water polo teams met in a friendly match on Tuesday evening. Many new players hav,e been secured by Warrington, and Pepper, lace, Dunn and Wolstencroft, bid fair to prove very useful acquisitions. The teams were :—Warrington: Woods, goal; Beaman and lace, backs; James, half-back; Dunn, Pepper and Wolstencrott, forwards. Runcorn: Williams, goal; Millington and Chamberlain, backs; R. Allen, half- back; Lunt, Brookes and Riley, forwards. Warrington won the toss and • Runcorn were called upon tri defend the deep end. Warrington were soon on the aggressive and after two futile attempts at goal by Wolstencroft, Pepper from a pass scored the first goal of the season. After a few minutes further play James added another. Warrington played in fine style and two more coals were registered by Welsten- croft and Pepper. R. Allen, who played for Runcorn, now came into piorainence and narrowly missed scor- ing, the ball going over the cross-bar, Pepper again scored for Warrington and the interval arrived with the score Warrington 5. Runcorn nil. The second half saw the Warrington team still on the attack. Allen made a long shot hut was unsuccessful the goalkeeper stopping the leather in a acientific manner. A stiuggle now took place between Dunn and the Runcorn goalkeeper in the goal mouth, which resulted in Williams gaining the better of the situation. At 'this point he excelled, hut was finally defeated by Turner, who scored from a few yards in front of coal. Wolstencroft twain evaded the vigilance of Williams and brought the, 66:ire up to seven. Pepper again sent in a successful shot from half-way. Runcorn, nothing daunted, tackled splendidly. Time was called, the score being :—Warrington,B ; Runcorn, nil. SQUADRON RACE.</p>
        <p>A squadron race took place between Runcorn and Warrington, the latter winning by a length and a half. R. Pepper, H. Woolstencroft, S. Ince and R. Beaman swam for Warrington and Brooks, Lunt, Chamberlain and Allen for Runcorn. SCHOOL CHILDREN'S HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>A schohl children's handicap was also decided, the distance being two breadths (30 yards). 'The winners were :-1, A. Dean; 2, F. Payne; 3, T. Galvin. Prizes were offered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>•:..'ric"4-,i,'..r+c.;4. 44,,t.t1fig,,.5f0i41; PON-4,44 'SI'. • " eA44 ',"!. Ite 1 r'4' • ' '` ', r 4.E • •V'nt 'j' ~ ; "C '' _4"" .;WM:*., 4.'.. 4 s Ari .91. t. . . . ~V *,..m," '." ''' • c,,,..,4 ,' f.f,-,,, ~ :.) .6i% % .... ,K, ~, 4-Mi• r . " ".-: . ,z.z.,) , i, •</head>
        <p>•:..'ric"4-,i,'..r+c.;4. 44,,t.t1fig,,.5f0i41; PON-4,44 'SI'. • " eA44 ',"!. Ite 1 r'4' • ' '` ', r 4.E • •V'nt 'j' ~ ; "C '' _4"" .;WM:*., 4.'.. 4 s Ari .91. t. . . . ~V *,..m," '." ''' • c,,,..,4 ,' f.f,-,,, ~ :.) .6i% % .... ,K, ~, 4-Mi• r . " ".-: . ,z.z.,) , i, •</p>
        <p>UNITED IRISH LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Alderman Boyle IP., on Home Rule</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening last a large and representative gathering of Irishmen listened to an inspiring address ny Alderman Boyle J.P., in the rooms of the United Irish League Winwick-street. Dr. Liston presided and was supported amongst others by Messrs, W.J. Loughrey (U.I.L. Organiser for Lancashire), Charles Roddv,James Fay, P. blaginn,A. M.Crowe and E. Kelly. The Chairman in a short speech extended a welcome to the visitors.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOYLE in a lengthy and masterly address informed his hearers that it was about 16 years since he had the pleasure of addressing an Irish audience in Warrington. Since that time the Horne Rule cause had made great strides in the English Constituencies. Reviewing the present political position, the speaker said that as far as the great masses of Englishmen were concerned Home Rule was not looked upon as a boeeY as Unionists had represented it to be in the past. The speaker referred to the arguments that were used against the demand for National self-govern- ment and closely analized the religious aspect of the situation and in a convincing manner disposed of any ground for fear that anything but toleration would be received at the hands of Irishmen under the regime of self-government. With regard to the much used statement that Irishmen were unfit to manage their own affairs, he would refer them to the immense success that the County Councils of Ireland had attained. There was no country better governed by its local bodies than Ireland was So-day. Surely no other proof of their ability was necessary. Whilst Eng,lishmen were now keenly studying the effect of the recent Budget upon those concerned whilst to Irishmen it was also of interest, yet the question of Home Rule was of Predominating impottancei He could tell his hearers that at the next general election no candidate for Parliamentary honours either Liberal, Conservative or Labour, would be entrusted with the Irish vote unless be was pledged to a measure of Home Rule and also that it would be made a real living issue— the same as Free Trade and Protection. As far as Irish constituencies were concerned there would be no doubt that a huge majority of Nationalist members would be again returned to Parliame'nt at the Dissolution, but it was in the English constituencies that a real fight would take place. At the present time the Irish vote in many divisions was of supreme importance and very often a balancing power. It was for that reason that he made a special appeal to Irishmen to he loyal to their local branches, as those were the centres that would play such an important part in next general election. He was pleased to see that the Leagce in Warrington was making good progress, but still there was a good deal more to do. Their cause was a just cause—the demand of a nation for self-government. Their demand consisted simply elan Irish Parliament to control purely Irish domestic affairs with an executive responsible to it. That did not mean Repara- tion, and he was certainly of the opinion that English- men would not long continue their objection on the score. (Applause.) The weat majority of Englishmen were very badly informeol upon the question of Home Rule, and it was party rancour that had been the cause of it ;in the past. He looked forward to the speedy settlement of the ques- tion, and in conclusion he asked every Irishman to trust the Irish party as the Irish party had trusted them. (Applause.) To Catholics he would say, that whether it was for the defence of their schools or in endeavour- ing to secure the removal of those Disabilities which were still imposed upon them, they could depend upon the Nationalist party on all occasions to endeavour to see that justice was obtained. (Applause). Mr. W. J. LOUGHTLEY also addressed the meeting. Mr. A. M. CROWE in proposing a vote of thanks to the speakers observed that the Irish vote in Warrington was of great importance and probably atthe next election. would be a balancing power. There were nearly 2,000 Irish votes in Warrington, and properly organised it would be readily seen what a force they were. He appealed for generous support to their local organisation Mr. CHARLES RODDY seconded and the motion was carried with acciammation. _</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Alderman BOYLE, seconded by Mr. W.. 1. LOUGHEEY, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>FIRST AND BEsT.—Readers should not forget that when purchasing at shops the well-know Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People they should see this full title appears on the package. Unfortunately, some dealers worry the public with substitutes described as "just the same "or "just as good" (because of the greater profits they bring), but haying effected so many cures Dr. Williams' Pink Pills must always remain the best,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>DERBY.</head>
        <p>THE DERBY.</p>
        <p>This annual event resulted as follows Minoru ; 2, Leuviera ; 3, William the Fouih. Time as taken by Benson's Chronograph, 2 mins. 42 2-5 sees. Time last year 2 mina. 29 4-5 se's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>kel)t Amm, 1,-;1; 17:1'4141.'4 • . " 4,1 – • 4 •, • tn • . ••;-64 :444: ,354-fh, ( • ..,101,` —,; 4. ‘O,l, 4!;., 4 „ , . 1.1;, Tr, Z. 4:••• -447 4076-R ,dt 4,41, v o,t • , • . • A ,••• • •,i • 1/..)•••47 • 2 7,;;A: – 4,1 •YZ-te, , 4 4, 4 -*P'. ^ .`2l 'l4 41V'' (.4"/ • • *P. 500 " • •t 4— .4 4 • , , • ' • ''':l;4 . • – ":0 • "FA, • • „, 4 :IN , 4`, r• -b• r 440,4„ft, 4 • • • • – wa, . .</head>
        <p>kel)t Amm, 1,-;1; 17:1'4141.'4 • . " 4,1 - • 4 •, • tn • . ••;-64 :444: ,354-fh, ( • ..,101,` —,; 4. ‘O,l, 4!;., 4 „ , . 1.1;, Tr, Z. 4:••• -447 4076-R ,dt 4,41, v o,t • , • . • A ,••• • •,i • 1/..)•••47 • 2 7,;;A: - 4,1 •YZ-te, , 4 4, 4 -*P'. ^ .`2l 'l4 41V'' (.4"/ • • *P. 500 " • •t 4— .4 4 • , , • ' • ''':l;4 . • - ":0 • "FA, • • „, 4 :IN , 4`, r• -b• r 440,4„ft, 4 • • • • - wa, . .</p>
        <p>vvirAft ~ ; , 0 z !'• :4 ~</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>LZy Recap.]</p>
        <p>POULTRY FOR EXMBITION. (Cmdinued).</p>
        <p>It will be fresh in the minds of my readers that ast week I gave a general outline of breeding ?oultry for exhibition, mentioning some of the many lifferent breeds. Special requirements in treatment ire necessary with each different breed, and I what Wherefore to take my readers into a closer study of general management. The early hatched stock will</p>
        <p>&gt;y, now have— got into well grown birds, and to a :ertain extent you will be able to judge of their .elative value as show specimens. Therefore a</p>
        <p>SELECTION</p>
        <p>of the best should be made and separated into different pens. Be careful not to overcrowd, for there is nothing more disastrous than overcrowding, nothing which encourages disease more quickly, and failure as a result is inevitable. The novice may ask, what points are to guide one in making the selection of stock? It cannot be done by simply taking a bird's-eye view of the flock. First of all then prepare the extra pens intended for use. Then when the birds have gone to roost take a hamper and pick out all the cockerels and place them in their new quarters. Thus you-have taken the first step. After this • has been done you are better able to go through them, selecting only the healthiest and best coloured ones. Do not be merely guided in colour from outward appearance, you must carefully handle every individual bird selecting those only which have the best under-colour as well as a good top colour. This is a decided factor in show specimens. For instance, supposing you are going in for Buff-Orpingtons and you think that you have a good lot of birds simply because they are uniform in colour from &amp;I outward appearances. But on a closer exami- nation after handling them you find that the. under :..olour is very light and sometimes even</p>
        <p>. white. This should tell you at once that such birds must not only to be discarded from an exhibition standpoint but also as unfit specimens for stock purposes. In these days of keen competition on the show-bench such birds would not stand the ghost of a chance. The plumage of all self-coloured birds should be uniform right down to the root of the feather. Whether the shade in colour is on the light or dark side does not much matter providing that it is, as pointed out, even all through. Again in making a selection decide on those that are straight. What I mean is that they should not have crooked breast bones and twisted feet (common occurences when allowed to perch at too early an age). All these are serious faults and count against success in show specimens. The best guide I can offer to the novice after having decided on the breed is to make himself acquainted with the variety by visiting a few good shows and comparing notes between the winning birds and the ones lower down in the honours list. Study the type knd character as well as the colour of the birds. Thus by degrees you will gradually educate yourself. Stick to one variety until you have mastered all that there is to know about it, and while you are doing so your general knowledge of other varieties will grow, and your capacity in the selection and weeding out of the stock -will be more reliable as a consequence. Therefore, I would advise you after choosing the best, put all the discarded pullets into the utility pen and feed on for an egg supply. The cockerels not intended or not qualified for show pur- poses must either be sold or fatted up for table. Give all the space and time available to your best birds, for it is only by constant and careful attention that you can hope to be successful as a breeder and exhibitor of exhibition poultry.</p>
        <p>PRESERVATION'</p>
        <p>of colour is maintained ,in our stock either by natural or artificial shadirg from the hot sun and inclement weather, especially in birds of a white or light-coloured plumage. If your runs are not naturally shaded you should erect covered runs about 3 feet high from the ground so that when the rays of .the aim are powerful the birds can find shelter. Moveable cockerel pens should be used in preparing the male birds for show. These are small houses about 7or 8 ' feet in length and 3feet in height,, with handles at each end so that they are-easily moved from place to place. This should be done about every other day when the runs would be kept sweet and clean. Cockerels thus housed are speedily got into show condition, being kept free from the damaging effects of the weather, as is the case when allowed to run out in the open. However good a bird may be in show poirts it would stand a very poor chance of success if sent to compete in an untrained condition. Many a good bird has had to be passed by the judge, because it was too vough in the pen when approached. It is therefore essential that .a proper system of</p>
        <p>ti t "21</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TRAINING</head>
        <p>TRAINING</p>
        <p>should be given so that when faced by the judge the birds do not resent being handled,because to correctly adjudicate upon the merits of a bird it is necessary to take them out of the show-pen and carefully examine. A well-trained bird against an untrained one of even a better quality stands a better chance of winning the prize. Fanciers in a large way usually have a place specially erected for per ling and training their birds for show. It is no uncommon sight when visiting such a place to see twenty or thirty birds thus penned off receiving a disciplined course of training. But to the fancier in a small way a couple or more of wire training pens could be fixed up in any old shed or stable suitable for the purpose. I have seen more than one cup-winner emanate from very crude dwelling places. Keep a good supply of clean litt3r on the floor of the pen and Place the birds in such a position that you have often to pass and so get them used to being closely noticed.and occasionally take them out and so also get them used to being handled. The proper training of birds for exhibition is not a difficult matter at all if carried out on the lines suggested. You will be surprised how soon they become perfectly docile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>FEEDING</head>
        <p>FEEDING</p>
        <p>for exhibition varies according to variety and colour You must not. feed light-coloured birds on\ Indian corn or maize as the tendency by so doing is that you would get a yellowish tint, and this is very undesirable especially in birds of an absolutely white feather. Then there is the question of the special feeding of hard and soft-feathered varieties. It would never do to feed any of the different classes of game on the same lines as other varieties of the soft-feathered tribe. The feather of the Game is short and what is called hard. On the other hand, the feather of the Cochin China and such like is soft and long. Thus you will often see them described in show reports as follows:—" Old English Game, 'lst and special prize, a grand specimen, good condition, as hard as nails.'" Again :—Cochin class, Ist prize, a good one, lovely condition, abund- ance of feather." So you see how necessary it is to feed, judiciously according to the constitution of the bree-1. Hard-feathered birds should not be fed too liberally on soft food (except when moulting:), but all soft-feathered birds should be given soft food at least once a day. A very essential article of diet for show- birds is animal food. I prefer cut green bone (recently recommended for utility stock and described in these columns). It has a wonderful effect in pro- ducing bone and helping to keep the birds in fine condition. There are many other hints which could be recommended in the special feeding for exhibition and which I intend to give. So I shall ask my readers to follow these articles on exhibition poultry very closely, and next week I shall again continue my remarks on this interesting branch of poultry farm- ing. The show season will soon be upon us, and I want if possible to assist you in adding to your laurels tinny a first and special prize. (To be continued).</p>
        <p>CommEncrAL FAlLllRES.—According to Kenips Mercanti/e Gazette, the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, May 21st, was 164 viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, S 4; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 80. The respective numbers in tbc corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 81; Deeds of Arrange- ment, 79—Total 160; being an increase of 4. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 21 weeks of the present year is 3,310 the total number recorded in the corresponding 21 'Pleas of last year was 3,489, showing a decrease of 179.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>rul J. HAVELOCK WILSON, M.P. DEFENDS FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>Tim Policy For the Workeirs.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Havelock Wilson, M.P., the seamen's repre- sentative in Parliament, was the principal speaker at the second of a series of open-air meetings, promoted by the Warrington Progressive Labour League, which was held at Bridge Foot (neaf Arpley Cannons) on Monday evening. There was again a large attendance and the simple yet convincing way in which Mr. Wilson put the case for Free 'Trade and Reform not only held the deep interest of his hearers but frequently aroused their enthusiasm. Mr. J. Elwell, jun.' presided and was supported on the platform by Mr. J. Havelock Wilson, M.P., Mr. C. E. Parker, Mr. Joseph Howes, Mr. S. Wright, Messrs. J. A. J. Morton and 3. Howes, 'jun. (hon. secretaries of the League).</p>
        <p>MR. ELWELL AND COUNCILLOR BROADHURST The CHAIRMAN said he noticed that their tariff reform friends on the Thursday evening following the debatecharged him with making mis-statements on that occasion. Mr. Henry Taylor said the statement he (Mr. Elwell) made with regard to wages was altogether wrong and added that he had worked in 1873 when wages were higher than at the present time. He (Mr. Elwell) admitted it, but he did not make the statement attri- buted to him that wages were higher at the present time than they had been at any period in the history of the trade. The statement he made was that taking the last ten years, the average wages were higher than in any other ten years. In support of that he held in his band a statement of the wages paid in the iron trade from 1851 to 1871, signed by E. Barker, secretary of the Iron Masters' Association. He had also a statement of the wages paid from 1871 up to the present time, con- firmed and signed by the vice-president of the Midland Wages Board, not only of the wages paid in the districts ruled by the Midland Board but in those ruled by the Northern and Scotch Boards. Mr. Taylor also stated that in 1873 he got 13s. 6d. per ton for puddling. Never in the history of the iron trade, how- ever, had that amount been paid as a basis price to peddlers. Higher sums had been paid for special iron, but 13s. 6d. had never been paid for common iron which was the basis on Which the wages were taken. In 1873 gave an award in arbitration wee given which brought wages up to 13s. 3d. per ton, but whereas a ton to-day was 2,2401b5., in 1873 it was 2,4001b5. which brought the average wages at that period down to 12s. 9d. instead of 13s. 6d. Mr. Broadhurst was very fond of quoting figures, but where he got them froin he (Mr. Elwell) did not know, because they certainly did not correspond with the Board of Trade returns. But allowing him to have his figures ; he stated that in the three years 1906-7-8 there was imported into this country £67,000,C00 worth of finished iron and steel. Of that, however, £19,000,000 represented ore and scrap, and yet Mr. Broadhurst quoted it as being finiched material which we could manufacture in this country. Something like £9,000,000 was accounted for by re-exports and a vast amount of the imported material was made up of ferro-manganese, blooms and billets, which Mr. Broadhurst said were not to be taxed by the tariff reformers. That brought down the amount of finished material coming into this country to about ' £40,000,0( ). But what did we send out? During the same period the exports in our iron and steel trade amounted to £286,898,851. (Applause). Councillor Broadhurst drew a dismal picture with regard to the trade at Barrow. but the condition of things operating there a few months back was not due to the question of Free Trade or Tariff Reform. Anyone who followed the trade journals would know that some twelve months ago the Barrow directorate decided to close down their works in order to bring them up to date. They were being handicapped to the extent of 6s. or Bs. per ton in the manufacture of steel. They had spent about £250,00 on improvements and re-started the works on May 3rd, and in the Barrow Press the statement appeared that as a • result of the first week's working 1,000 tons of feel had been saved. The tariff reformers said that unemployment was more severeiu this country than it was in Protected countries. He had in his hand a White Paper issued o the order of the House of Commons which stated that the production of iron and steel in the United Kingdom during . the pas year fell to the extent of 834,000 tons ; Germany 1,000,000 tons ; United States. 10.000,000 tens. That meant that 38 per cent. of the employees in the iron and steel trade of America were out of employment. The Government returns showed that there was far more unemployment in Protected countries than there was in Free Trade England. Mr. Elwell dealt with other arguments raised by Councillor Broadhurst and contended that instead of hindering the trade of this country Frce Trade had given it a great impetus. Mr. Stephen Jeans, speaking of the year 1830, said we imported more iron and steel than we exported. What was the condition of things under Free Trade? We imported £60,000,000 as against £287,000,000 of exports. (Applause.) Protected countries were ruled by trusts and cartels and no man was safe. Half the iron and steel trade of America was in the hands of one gigantic trust and if a man made a mistake he had either to leave his trade or his country. It would be an easy matter in a small country like ours for a trust of capitalists to get the whole of the industry into their bands and that would be greatly to the detri- ment of the workingmen. (Applause). MR. HAVELOCK WILSON, M.P., AND FREE TRADE.</p>
        <p>Mi. HAVELOCK WILSON, M.P., said tariff reformers preached them a beautiful story. They were always ready to magnify the fact that in this country there were thousands of working men and women unable to obtain employment. They said: Did it not appear nonsense that while so much unemployment existed, we should be bringing into the country goods that we could manufacture ourselves? The tariff reformer, however, always omitted to call attention to the state - of affairs existing in foreign countries. He Mr. Wilson) was in a fortunate position. He had not any need to go to Blue Books for information; what he was going to speak of that night he bad seen with his own eyes. He had been travelling since he was thirteen Years of ago and he was turned fifty-one. He had been five times round the world ; he had travelled over 40,000 miles of the United States, and thousands of miles of the Australian coast; he had spent weeks and months in almost every Continental couutry—Gernany, France, Denmark, Sweden, Norway, Russia, Italy. Spain, Portugal—in fact there was scarcely a part of the world that he had not touched, and he had had an exceptional opportunity of tanking inquiries on the spot, because his travels abroad had generally beck in connection with the organizing of labour. The first time he went to the United States was in 1876, when he visited California. At that time the ordinary labouring man would turn up his nose with considerable contempt if anybody offered him a job at less than 15s, a day. In those days the country was being opened up, the gold mines of California were being worked, and there was a scarcity of men and consequently wages were good. At the same time, buying clothes was quite another experience. fie had se.n a snit of clothes purchased in San Francisco for £5 which he believed they could have bought in any town in England for about 225. The next time he was in the United States was in 1885 when he worked as a scaffold rigger on the Brooklyn Bridge at 123. a day. Food and clothing were at least 25 per cent. dearer than they were in the United Kingdom. A more recent visit was in 1897, when he went to the States as one of the delegates representing the whole of tbe trade unionists of the United Kingdom and remained there over six months organizing labour. He found that thousands of labourers were earning only 5s a day,whereas the first time be went to America the same class of labour would earn three or four times as much. The McKinley tariff, which bad not been erected on the occasion of his first visit, had now come into operation. In New York be had seen thousands of workmen down at the docks, struggling for employment in the same manner as the same class of workmen struggled for employment in Liverpool or London. His experience had been that the, percentage of unemployed workmen in America. was far in excess of the unemployed in this country. The tariff reformers said Protection would put this country in a better position. In 1860 the shipping trade of the United States was • almost equal to ours, but after that year the United Stateg Congress declared that no ship could be owned in America unless sbe was built in the United States. From the time that that Protective policy was adopted, the shipping trade of America had steadily declined year by year, whereas outs had gone forward by leaps and bounds. No foreign' ship was allowed to carry cargo from one American port to another, the whole of the const'ng trade being reserved absolutely for American ships. In ' spite of that Protection ships were tied up with nothing to do and there was a much largerpercentage of unemployed seamen in America than there was in this country at the present time. Mr. Havelock Wilson then contradicted the assertion or inference that our imports were paid for in cash, and pointed out that we received them in exchange for other goods which we</p>
        <p>f the exported. Great Britain owned more than hal shipping of the world, and the reason was 'that -- could build shops more cheaply than they could other nation because we had I ree imports. More than be`. our ships were employed in carrying cargoes from foreign country to another and we received payment 13 those services in the form of food or materials wh,l°,,k we extracted -from the foreigners. Mr. Have 0.. Wilson also showed how Free Lade enabled us to but •as ships so cheaply that we built a great many for foreleo countries. Many of the materials require the construction of vessels were imported froei Germany and the reason we got them 5cP cheaply was that the German industry b e Protected, the German 'manufactorer's market vi t limited and in order to dispose of his surplus be se°, the goods over here at a price below what it had 61:50 him to produce them. We used the cheap material the construction of ships which we sold to the foreiglf at an enormous profit to this country. (Applause). taxes were imposed and £lO,OOO were put en the Priceld a big ship, the Germans would be in a position to bin 0 the vessel as cheaply as we could and we should les, the order. The building of ships also mrant te,°% employment for people in many other industries. 'Jibed speaker showed how the supply of cheap, bountSl, sugar from abroad enabled us to beat our Contenf competitors in the jam and biscuit trades, in spite of the tariffs. Tariff reformers were always reminding le. that we were getting poorer. Mr. Lloyd-George nnl,„c, a speech in the House of Commons, when he was he',7 told that the capital of this country was putting ',I, wings and flying abroad, in which he gave some ye',•' remarkable figures. He showed that whilst there wtati, all this clap-trap about capital going abroad, the wetuft, of the country was steadily increasing year by year. was perfectly true that as far as the workingman concerned, he (the speaker) could not honestly seY his position as a capitalist was improving, but who "o.j-e5 to blame for that? Not Free Trade. It was larg',Z, due to the wcricingman himself failing to organize insist that he should at least have a fair share of ;;r, -, wealth he produced. He (Mr. Wilson) was not a Social" He did not want anybody else's wealth. A VOICE: Neither do the Socialists. Mr. WILSON: No, they do not. It would be wr°r)g. of me to say they do. fyit The Interrupter: But you are trying to &amp;go impression. to Mr. WiLsoe : You don't know what T was tryine do, unless you are a thought reader. I don't vvant . have any argument with you. I suppose I can montiood the word Socialism without the world coming to an eue It is not such a sacred cause that we can't mention t't name of it. Continuing Mr. Wilson said it %vas Ir 4 possible for all people to be equal, because bralcd would always come out on top, even if they bi'eb Socialism to-morrow. But he did advocate and 10°,0 to the workingmen that it was possible for theta have a fairer share of the wealth they produced ths, they were getting at the present time. When he se°7ol mite commencing at the bottom rune of the ladder se, in twenty or twenty-five Years time being able to Proe clainrhiniself a millionaire, he said that man had don somebody down, or he would not be in that positioce, He did not accuse the man of robbery. He might achieved his position in the commercial way that Rockefeller had done. He had crnshed out all o°2; petitors and was novv able to accumulate his wealth it millions. In his (Mr. Wilson's) opinion Mr. Rockefelllo did not earn those millions. It was the people in .bng employ who were earning wealth and he was get°-to a larger share of it than he was entitled receive. So in this country wealth accarnulete' in certain directions to the positive disadvantsg: of the vvorkiegpeople. But the working people tbev't selves were to blame for it He (the speaker) was nr°l: one of those who was always advocating " more %coo for the workingmen." He did not believe in it. would like them all to be doing less work but gettins; more money for it. (Hear, hear). What wan the 0 of talking about mere work for working°, when some were working fourteen hours a d'illa The taxes imposed tinder tariff reform increase the cost of living to the extent of 25 per cenrir Mr: Wilson contended that what handicapped us in °if; trade was the fact that there were a few parasitese this country who were eating into our vitals and e, could not see them on the surface. He'meant the ipho "who toil not, .neither do they spin," the men Nv'to extorted from the industry of this country from se*"„.o re 6s'. per ton on royalty rents and way-leaves. I3elo could sell our iron and steel we had to pay those t°oe, a, who did no toil three times as much in royalties way-leaves as •the foreigner had to pay. In al,nlo°o every foreign country the royalties went to tho ?tllel hut in this country they went to the private indiYld to who would have a difficulty in proving his right ti them. Foreign countries had the aivantage nve,rher with regard to railway rates. Germrinrenoourage",to industries by carriage of goods from inland tow,e'oo the sea ports at very small charges, but in Englana policy was reversed. A ton of goods which had to forwarded from Glasgow to Liverpool could he soer;- cheaper via New York than direct. The railwaa,' jc panies also gave the foreigner preferential rates, rife did not think we would have heard one thing of tt' reform had it not been for the South African war.„try was necessary to place before the people of this CO the some conflicting issue so that they would forget' ~Te great crime of all crimes, that caused blood and treasmg to flow, and brought eternal disgrace on the nan3,1,0 our great country. Thank God we had repaired ',,ta wrong by granting a constitution to the two Souute, African Republics, and as a result of that wise meres;cie of Liberal reform we had now a united South Airs He ventured to say that in a few years it would of the most prosperous parts of the Empire. (APPle° di. Questions having been invited, a member of the price asked whether the workers who created Mr. “°° feller's wealth were not entitled to the whole of it. big Mr. WILSON said that if Mr. Rockefeller with be brains had been able to organize his great concelli, should have his share as well as the workingoic Capital was also entitled to some interest. Mr. C. E. PARKER, in proposing a vote of thanls 0, Mr. Havelock Wilson, said he had seldom heard slic„°,ith excellent exposition of the Free Traie side. regard to sugar, however, it should be borne in rhed that a few years ago the bounty system was abolle" it; by the Tories—a most foolish procedure becanee,ero practically threw away about £5,000,000 that we.° getting yearly in cheap sugar. a tO Mr. HOWES seconded and said it was intend° of hold meetings throughout the summer. Thea' w°otit follow the tariff reformers in their campaign ° there was nothing left to follow. (Laughter.) The vote was heartily accorded and the me ati4 closed.</p>
