        <p>LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE ASSOCIATION CLUB CHAMPIONSHIP. SECOND ROUND.</p>
        <p>Warrington Bull's Head v. George Hotel. Prestwich.- Played on the green of the Nags Head Hotel. Croft's Bank on Saturday. Details :-Warrington Bull's Head (W, Daniels 21, H. Williams 21, H. Fogg 6. HAlbrook 21, Gleave (captain) 12' J. Bedworth 21, .1. Jolley 12, .1. Swanton 21, E. Shaw, 19, J. Hackett 13, .1. Gleave 21, NV. Andrews 10), 198 ; George Hotel, Prestwich (A. Chappell 16, J. Rothwell 20, R. Spencer 21, .1. Codling 14 ; H. Fielding 21, W. Fielding, jun., 11. J. Paulden 21, P. Harting 16, J. R. Natal! 21, H. Clarke (captain) 21. J. F. Kay 6, A. Booth 21), 209 ; majority for George, 11. Railway lintel, Cadishead, v. Roebuck, Flixton.- Played at Stretford Angel on Saturday. Cadishead 196 ; Roebuck 220; majority for Roebuck, 24. 'Warrington Cemetery v. Radcliffe With ins.-Played O n the green of the Lord Nelson Hotel, Urmston' on Saturday. Details :-Warrington Cemetery (G. Ball, jun., 21, J. Bolton 21, G. Ball, sen., 21, J. Wood 16, R. Rot hwell 11, J. Harrison 16, A. Scott 9, J. Gleave 21, J. Allison 21. J. Ashton 21, J. Humphreys 21. A. G. Jones 14), 213 ; Radcliffe Withins (J. !Wort 10, j.lsberwood 8, H. Gardiner 18, A. Fish 21. W. E. Walker 21, R. Daven- port 21, W. Davenport 21, P. Harupson 10. A. Greenbalgh 11, .1, Howarth 9, H. Robinson P, W. Mort 21), 1811-; majority for Cemetery, 33.</p>
        <p>Wigan Subscription v. Cborlton.—Played et Trafford Hotel on Saturday. Wigan Subscription, 194; Chorltou, 199; majority for Choraon, 5. Pendleton Belmont v. Carter's Arms, Sale.—Played at Urrnst on Victoria on Saturday. Pendleton Belmont, 205 ; Carter's Arms, Sale, 227; majority for Carter's Arms, 22.</p>
        <p>Prestwich Conssrvatives v. flarnurhev.—At White- field Subscription Club. Prestwich,l94 ; Harporhey,2l6. Leigh Limited v. Winnineron Park.—At Bowdon Griffin. Leigh Limited, 221 ; Winnington Park, 205. Temple v. Kersal Moor.—At Prestwich Conservative, Kersal, 212 ; Temple, 210. Eccles Cross Revs v. Stretford Angel.—At Harpurhey. Eccles, 216; Angel; 191.</p>
        <p>Ship arlarn) v. Bowdon Griffin.—At Sale Hotel. Griffin, 215 ; Ship, 202. Newton-le-Willows v. Priday.—At Eccles Cross Keys. Newton-le-Willows,. 233; Friday, 179. Leigh Railway v. Lower Broughton Limited.—PlaFed at gendleton Belmont.. Leigh Railway, 250 ; Lower Broughton Limited, 162.</p>
        <p>DRUMS' COMPRTITION.—The members of the War- rington and District Druids' lodges held their second annual main on the "Joiners Arms" Hotel green on Saturday afternoon for the handsome silver challenge cup presented by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart. Ideal weather prevailed and the green was in c,apitsl con- dition. Including the members of the Croft United Lodge, there were 98 competitors representing the following lodges : The " Lord Elliott," " Penketh United." " Pride of , Latchford." " Druids' Camp," "Druids' Hope," " Stonehenge," "Ring William 4th," "Trafalgar," "Queen Victoria," " Gilbert Greenall" and' Mermaid." Some good games were witnessed but from the onset the members of the home lode° kept well to the fore.ln addition to the cup there were 24 other prizes, given by Captain Crosfield. Mr. R. Pierpoint and others. In the closing stages the bowling was very keen and eventually lay between T. Kerfoot, the pro- prietor of the Joiners' Arms Hotel, and j.Hardman,jun., of Warrington, Kerfoot winning amid much cheering a closely contested game and thus gaining for the Croft United Lodge the honour of retaining the challenge cup for twelve months. The other prize-winners were: J. Hardman, jun., W.Goulden, R.Unsworth,D.Fazalterly, E. Allan, J.Lloyd, E. Dumbell, W. Bibby; jun ,J.Beyer, J. Hill, G. ()teen, G. Ashley, H. Bowyer, W. Green, E. Maines, J. Hankey, J. W. Starkie, P. Johnson, sen., J. Hardman, W. Beresford, J. Dickson, R. Mains, H. E. Poole-k. At the conclusion of the bowling D.C.S. 8r0.141 Allen proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Kerfoot, and it was carried with cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES, ETC.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES, ETC.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington continue their career and on Saturday Newton-le-Willows were compelled to bow the knee to them. The match was a League engage- meat and took place at Newton under ideal conditions, the weather being all that could be desired, whilst the wicket was in capital condition. There is no doubt that Warrington mean business this season and a really strong eleven has been got together. I he, prospects are encouraging- and lovers of the summer sport are hoping for great things from the team.</p>
        <p>Warrington had first turn with the bat, and J. Callon and W. Richardson opened operations. Callon hit out merrily, and at fourteen he was smartly caught by Miller at point. He was succeeded by T. Wright, who started promisingly, but the bulk of the scoring was left to RichardsOn, who played sound cricket and ran up a useful fifteen before he was caught by Wright. Another creditable performance was B. Silcock's, ,who compiled 18. Wright was dismissed for 7 and Partridge knockcd.up 11.</p>
        <p>The feature of the innings was a brilliant display by W, H. Podmore who in his ninety minutes' stay at the wicket made the substantial score of 60. His chief hits were eight four's and he defied all efforts of a succession of bowlers to disturb him. Hindley was tried with success, for the batsman played one into the hands of Harrison. There was a long partnership between W. H. Podmore and J. W. Pearson, the latter compiling a well-hit 27 before he was stumped by Wright. J. Callon was also in good 'form and placed 14 to his credit, -while H. Callon made 11 and carried out his bat.</p>
        <p>Newton proved quite unequal to the task of saving the game and their combined efforts realized the small figure of 63. The top scorers were A. Millen (20) and Larice (15). Cresswell and H. Callen bowled with deadly effect, the professional capturing 6 wickets for 23 runs and Callon taking 4 wickets for 38 runs.</p>
        <p>There was more excitement in the match between Warrington 2nd and Newton 2nd, at Arpley, the home eleven winning by 17 runs. The feature of the War- rington innings was a grand exhibition by J. Thompson who placed 50 runs to his credit in an hour. W. R. Hindley (35) batted well for the visitors, but after his dismissal they soon collapsed before the deliveries of Way-well, who with W. Bennett, bowled unchanged, their records being respectively six wicket's for 45 runs and four for 27,</p>
        <p>Other matches in the West Lancashire League resulsed in Prescot first eleven defeating St; Helens</p>
        <p>by the narrOw margin of four and Prescot second accounting for the St. Helens second contingent by 81.</p>
        <p>The follotviug are the League tables to-date :---- FIRST</p>
        <p>Club. P. W. L. D. Pts' St. Helens Recs. 2 ... 2 ... 0' ... 0 ... 4 Prescot Warrington 3 ... 1 ... 1 ... 1 ... 3 Earlestown Newton 2 ... 0. ... 2 ... 0 ... 0 St. Helens 2 ... 0 ... 2 ... 0 ... 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Warrington St. Helens Rees Prescot - 3 ... 2 ... 1 ... 0 ... 4 Earlestown Newton St. Helens</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District League uSurney proceeds apace and on Saturday there were four matches. In a low-scoring game Wilderspool Brewery defeated Latchford Baptists by a margin of eight runs. J. Wray (13 not out) being the most successful batsman,</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills 2nd had Walker's trewery .2nd for their opponents and accounted for them with 38 runs to spare. W. Bell distinguished himself with a tine innings which realized 39. H. Partridge and S. Holden were also in good form and placed 16 and 14 not out respectively to their credit.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Engineers suffered a reverse at the hands of Rylands Bros. who ran up a majority of 27. Duckworth was in excellent form for the winners and knocked up 33, whilst Layfield. contributed 32 to Rylands's total of 84.</p>
        <p>St. James's are carrying all before them and on Saturday they inflicted a severe drubbing on White- cross. The Saints put together the handsome total of 119 and dismissed their opponents for 60. J. Constable gave a brilliHnt display with the bat and knocked up 52, and J. Hallam also played well for ,his 25. .1. Leicester was in capital form for the wire- men and had compiled 25 when he was run out. In the Warrington Junior League St. Algry's 'made a very poor display against Sankey, the whole side being out for 20. Sankey put together 92, Bradburn being the highest scorer with 28.</p>
        <p>Appleton and Stretton had an easy task in disposing of All Saints. The villagers compiled 48, of which P. Whitfield was responsible for 14, but All Saints' distinguished themselves with a great array of ducks, "Extras 5" being the top item. G. Mann took six wickets for one run.</p>
        <p>In the realm of "friendlies," there were several interesting encounters. Secondary School added to their victories at the expense of 80ld-street Wesleyans who do not appear to have found their form yet. The Secondary school rattled up the fine total of 119, Stirrup carrying off the honours with 53, but not one of the Wesleyans reached double figures, the side being dismissed for 13.</p>
        <p>Warrington Municipal Officers' Guild were victorious over Winwick Asylum, Green capturing six wickets for 27 runs and scoring 28.</p>
        <p>Both the G-rappcnhall elevens were successful in their engagements with Altrincham Wesleyans, and Oughtrington Park administed defeat to High Legh. n the latter encounter L. G. L. Farrar batted splendidly for 04.</p>
        <p>Warrington Grammar School met more than their match in Hulme Grammar School who triumphed by 47. Chorlton carried out his bat after making 31 for the winners, while Woodcock made the best display for Warrington with 21.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>Of the three bowling matches played last Saturday in the Warrington Works Competition, that between Rylands' Engineers and Rylands Netting was the most exciting.</p>
        <p>The .Engineers had an advantage of 5 in the,handi- capping and won by 161 to 158. W. Berry (Rylands', Netting) and A. Mather (Rylands' Engineers) pro- vided about the most interesting contest. The two peeled at (5, and then Berry had a run of successes, and reached 12 before Mather gained 2, bringing his total to S. They both plodded on, and when the figures were at 14 and 13 (Berry and Mather respec- tively) excitement ran high. Berry had the satisinc- tion of bowling the winning wood, and won by 15-13 The contest •between J. Mason (Netting) and F. Triggar (Engineers) was very good—the latter leading slightly all the way, and winning by 2 points. There is one other game which is worthy of notice, and that was between \V. Broadhurst (Netting) and J. Law- less (Engineers). The latter had two singles to his credit before the former got any, but Broadhurst reached U whilst Lawless's figures only stood at ,2.. However, the latter revived, and he steadily made his way until he reached 13. Broadhurst had been going along nicely, but only getting one whilst his opponent got two. The figures stood at 13 each, when Broad- hurst claimed the honours by 15 to 13.</p>
        <p>The match between Crosfield's No.l and Greenings' No. was not very keen. The former started with a handicap of five points and the latter 30 points. Crosfield's had by far the best of the game, each of their men reaching the desired number--15—except D. Iligham, who only scored eight; being defeated by T. Quarmby.</p>
        <p>The L. &amp; N.-W. Railway and the Warrington Tanning Company provided a somewhat one-sided match. for the former, although scratch, defeated their contestants by 46 points. The " Tanners "had a handicap of 36 to their advantage, but they were easily outclassed, the figures of the L. &amp; N.-W. men being 15 all the way except a 10 and a 13:</p>
        <p>There were some startling results in the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Club Championship on Saturday, Bull's Head, Belmont, Radcliffe, and Wigan all being knocked out. Bull's Head, last year's champions, were beaten by 11 points by the George on the Nag's Head Green, which is one that may easily upset the calculations of any team. Harold Fielding, one of the noted family, gave the winners a good start by only allowing the captain Albert Weave to score twelve. This was followed by the veteran Tom Paulden winring by A similar number, whilst R. Spencer a new man in the team, defeated Harry Fogg, 21-6. This made the Bull's Head look anything but cheerful, until the younger Weave won with 15 points to his credit. This put them again in the fighting line, but W. Andrews sealed the fate of his team by only registering 10 points against A. Booth.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Cemetery team had the satisfaction of easily accounting for Radcliffe. Tom Bolton put them in a good position early on by beating a strong player in James Isherwood, 21-8. The Cemetery people will be pleased at this recovery of his old form on the part of Bolton. The winners are again a strong team this season and will make a bold bid for honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Swimm!ng.</head>
        <p>Swimm!ng.</p>
        <p>Unlike the commencement of last year's swimming season, the Workington polo team have started well. Many old players have dropped out, some having signed for other teams. NN e hear that C. Ashton has gone to help Hyde Seal and doubtless he will prove an acquisition to them. Although the match with Runcorn on Tuesday night was only a friendly one, the main idea of the committee was to test the players and see in which quarter they are likely to shine. Special mention can be made of the following : Pepper, Ince, Wolstencroft and Dunn. Pepper, if he keeps to his form, as was shown on Tliesday night, should prove a very useful man. The Warrington team soon disposed of their Cheshire .visitors, the score being 8-0:</p>
        <p>Williams, the Runcorn goalkeeper, played a fine game end although he is only a young lad, he seems to have the spirit and should turn out a first-class custodian.</p>
        <p>As in former years squadron races are to take place between different works in the town. On Tuesday one was decided between Warrington and Runcorn. Not much interest was taken in this ah the home team won by a length and a half. R. Allen, the Warringtoc champion, swam for Runcorn, but being the last to enter the water, he had no chance to exercise his powers.</p>
        <p>School boys' handicaps will soon be in full swing. Much interest was taken by the juvenile spectators in the handicap which took place prior to the polo match. Some excellent swimming Was witnessed. The winners were :--1, A. Dean ; 2, F. Pyne ; 3, F. Galvin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
