        <p>5 When history got THE STATE AND things into proper 13efr:t. THE WORKER. spective we doubt that the Governniel/j adoption of the scheme of insurance /lc labour exchanges recently outlined bY It3f CHURCHILL will be singled out as 133 r the most important political event of year after the Budget. The purpose ef labour exchanges is to perfect processes wll/,15 are already in operation. One of the of the old political economy is that labotirreo perfectly fluid. It always, so doctriOio have assumed, goes where there is a derOd for it, like water,rushing instantly into clew, crevice of employment. The only dr°,ll back of this" axiom" is that it has been jo imperfectly true. Time and again thee, demand in one place and supply in an°ie9,s workerless work in one plase and 'Nor' to workers in another; we have left itiog chance for the two to get together, and tool have entailed unnecessary suffering oribor and damage to industry. With 13 00 exchanges we shall do away with friction. The country will be covereu trid a network of bureaux which will be regis both of jobs and of men ; every work ,r through this medium, will know ins'ort whether there is a place for him in anl 'll be of the country, and every employer W 1 r, its able to get men without delay. Lab°</p>
