        <p>The Council has upset WALKING DAY. a pretty kettle of fish and got itself into hot 'Water in attempting to arbitrarily fix the 'date of Walking Day on July sth, instead of on Friday, July 2nd, the customary and `accepted date. Widespread consternation Was created when after the meeting of the al Purposes Committee on Monday the 111.11711 Clerk Published a notice stating in bald K'np.°lllQ Pr,°lll, lsing terms that owing to the ~sjelb-00;;rvsishit, the annual procession of Sunday 4d been postponed to Monday, 'llle 111 Y sth. Postponed by whom ?—was first question uppermost in the Public mind. Enquiry revealed the fact th e Council, sitting in committee, .„.,ah unanimously resolved to change the ':ate of the annual festival, and having () resolved had boldly intimated to all and 4,11dr Y that the day would be Monday the ~j 1 instead of Friday the 2nd. Blank ..:‘zonishment soon gave way to resentment the minds and hearts of the slighted nisters and Sunday school managers and ZirDerintendents who are really responsible te, the organisation of the Walking Day tiotebrations ; and judging from the feeling •;din, expressed in all directions the .1) 'hill of the Town Council will not akcepted. As a body they have exceeded arhe,ir Powers, and we can only express gzernent that they should have thus ltrarily attempted to decide a question )1 'loch primarily Concerned others much more 411:13T than themselves. The proper course taken is so obvious that we can Y imagine a responsible authority e the Council adopting any other eal.,lB' The conference which ought *it'll 'aye been called before now becomes 0113erative and unavoidable if an amicable •-xlei,ilrition to the difficulty is to be founa. We it not discuss the pros and cons here, but .re certainly seems to us that the balance of Wa'2,°,../1 is against changing the date of nu'ing Day at all. The King's visit for a ql‘ter of an hour on Tuesday evening, July 'the heed not necessarily wipe July 2nd off tib, calendar and throw the town into a</p>
        <p>Slum areas, insanitary 12VA,°Ric FOR THE property, and infantile 1-1 COMMITTEE. mortality have almost 11,1% come to be looked .41ist as necessary evils in our large towns. -cite "Las we have always the poor with us, so 'lave these unhealthy and degrading farVitions of daily existence. But the Dr'e7v.s' if seriously considered, ought to exit any lapse into a state of dangerous ten-ldPlacency. Familiarity with these things 21e`s breed a contempt for their hideous- 13Swnioh is often tantamount to unconcern. th: rneeting held on Thursday evening in ; Town Hall under the auspices of the t. '°'l Housing Council should go far to rlate a keener public sand official interest \ofto°llsing and sanitary reform locally. Two pas-ter: of this Association have, during the "wo or three weeks, conducted an 1142.7-stive inquiry into the state of War- 'lll4-,„".°11 slum areas, and their report was as lhalVating as it was voluminous. The 'qso-;„,8 of the community here and 'ths"'" ilere are due to the Council for sea, l_efforts it is making to throw the :tow`,clll,ight into the darkest places in riate-r,"fe• The only thing we can congratu- .Wor:llrselves upon is that Warrington is not -terriZ than similar towns, but the facts are -11Dw dismaying all the same. There are of 100 courts in Warrington—all of ;Iseeln to be dens of foulness, squalor the,, rr "43,111---in which poor people exist or die. Der Children die at the appalling rate of 263 'the d'vu°, and disease is positively invited by `4zle4l:eadful conditions. These plague spots %411,1,11 the first attention, but the Housing tmaps out a wide programme for 11sing reform which should never ost , 'ight of though it may take genera- Carry out. At present the labourer</p>
        <p>The Dean of Westminster's decision in regard to Meredith is not the first of the same kind whereby he has displayed strange judgment in this matter and gone counter to the views of the intellectual world at large. In the opinion of some, at least, an even more remarkable instance than that of Meredith was afforded by the attitude which he adopted in the case of Herbert Spencer, and many will recall the amazing letter which he wrote at the time inquiring what "our great philosopher" (as Darwin called him) had done. * * *</p>
        <p>The Empire is not held together by the waving of flags, but by the union of hearts. —The Earl of Meath. The Ministers of the day are bound to interpret the general will, and not go beyond it.—Mr. Haldane, M.P. Thanks to cheap food, regular wages, and to trade unions, tuberculosis has been diminished to an extent that warrants me in saying, as Minister of Public Health, that twenty-five years hence consumption will be as little known aq. typhus and typhoid are to-day.—Mr. John Burns, M.P. * * The claims of the Warrington Infirmary have lately been pleaded with such effect that £3OO has already been contributed this year in new and increased subscriptions and donations. The recent enlargement has involved such heavy financial responsibilities that the deficit in funds last year amounted to £1,200. A balance of £9OO therefore remains to be raised and we confidently rely upon the generosity of the townspeople to see that the institution is soon freed from embarrassment in the continuance of its noble and beneficent work.</p>
        <p>* * * There seems little or no chance (says the "Daily News ") of the Daylight Saving Bill emerging from the Select Committee this year in time to be considered by the House. Up to the present the proceedings have been slow, the Committee meeting only once a week. It has been suggested that there should be greater expedition after Whitsun- tide, and that the Committee should meet twice weekly, but even if that is found to be possible, in view of the arduous duties which await members. in the House in con- nection with the Budget, the report of the Committee can hardly be read before the end of July, as there are nearly twenty witnesses still awaiting examination.</p>
        <p>* * The guest of the evening at the Liberal Club on Tuesday was Mrs. Crosfield, who was accompanied by her husband on the occasion of the annual prize distribution. The Member in acknowledging a hearty vote of thanks tendered to his wife, took occasion to deliver q, - political speech, and was. delightfully snubbed for his pains. Mrs. Crosfield rose and said naively that if she • had known her husband was going to make such a long speech she would have made one herself at the outset. The little incident was keenly enjoyed, and by no-one more than Mr. Crosfield himself. He will have to remember in future that when occupying the boards he must leave reasonable room for his better half! * * *</p>
        <p>The Member for the Borough made a pleasing announcement at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening. He said he had secured a definite promise from the Lotd Adv,ocate to speak at Warrington in the autumn, and had also practically fixed up Colonel Seely, M.P. In many respects Mr. Crosfield has proved himself to be one of the best representa- tives Warrington has ever had in Parlia- ment, and tot the least of his achievements is the influence which he has obtained with leading members of the Government and the Party.Warrington is nowadays well favoured</p>
        <p>1'</p>
        <p>Personal. Our readers will regret to hear that Alderman Job Evans has been confined to bed for several days owing to illness. On Monday evening his condition was serious, but, fortunately, there has since been a marked change for the better, and he was out for a time on Thursday. On the same evening—Monday—Alderman Piar- point, who is a contemporary of Alderman Evans and a lifelong friend of his, was also seized with sudden illness at his house in St. Annes-on-Sea, and has since been. under close medical dare. He is confined to bed, but, we are glad to say, is going on as well as can be expected. Much sympathy will be extended to the Rev' Herbert Dunnico, of Liverpool, in the -recent loss o. his infant son and the serious illness of Mrs. Dunnicof Councillor Neild's condition is reported to be unchanged. He has for a long time past been confined to his home in Salisbury-stfeet. _ Music in the Park. By the kind permission of Lieut. Colonel J. D. Fairclough and officers the Volunteer Band will play in the Park on Thursday next at 7.15 p.m Councillor Locker, J.P. Ceuncillor J. T. Locker was sworn in as a justice of the peace at the Borough Police Court on Monday. At the time of his appointment Councillor Locker was representing his firm in South Africa and he returned three weeks ago. Forthcoming Wedding. The wedding is announced to take place on June 9th at St. James's Church, . Picerabl!y, of Tom Airey, of Sandfield Hall, Neqton-le-Willows, Lancashire, to Miss Elena Kennard Davia youngest daughter of Mr. E. E. Kennard Davis, of Hyde Park gate. Local Competitors at Southport Festival. At the Southport Musical Festival on Thursday, Master Herbert Geary, Warrington, and Master Arthur L. Edwards, Liverpool, tied for the first prize in the open violin solo (under 12) competition. Lorne House School, Warrington, was fourth in the children's action song (open) competition. Holy Trinity Church 81-centenary. The Mayor has invited the members of the Council and others to accompany him to Divine Service at Holy Trinity Church to-morrow (Sunday),the•Occasion of the - bi-centenary of the Church. The preacher, will be the Lord Bishop of Liverpool. Those desirous of accompanying the Mayor will assemble at the Town Hall at 10.10 a.m. LAWN MoWEßS.—Several good machines on sale in good condition at prices from 12s. 6d.-51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Death of a Former Warrington Schoolmaster. The death is announced of Mr. R. R. Grey, a former headmaster of the Nat;onal School, Church-street, who left Warrington between 40 and 50 years ago, to fill the post of headmaster of the Rochdale Grammar School. Mr. Grey, who pasaed away on Saturday morning, waasucceeded at Warrington by the late Mr. John Dewhurst. He was greatly respected by fill ,who came into contact with him and was always accorded a hearty welcome when he came to the town to take part in the Parish Church. Coming of Age Celebration. A social and dance were 'given at the King's Cafe, Bridge-street, on Friday evening, May 21st, by Mr. and Mrs. Waring, 77, Lovely-lane, to celebrate the coming of age of their eldest son, "'Jim." About 60 guests were'present and a most enjoyable evening was spent, dancing being kept pp until about 2 O'clock. During the interval songs were given by Miss E. Stokes and Mr. H. Boyle. The music was supplied by Mr. G. Lomas. At the close a hearty vote of thanks was given to the host and hostess. Many useful and handsome presents were received. TENNIS, CROQUET,' AND GARDEN FURNITURE.— J. S. GARLAND, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Old English Sports. There will be an excellent holiday attraction at Warrington on Whit Monday, in the shape of the Old English sports in aid of local charities, which will be held on the Wilderspool 'Football Ground. The pro- gramme is one of exceptional interest, and over 300 entries have been received,including all the best athletes of the district. The tug-of-war and squadron races between the various works will be a great feature. A bevy of lasses from the 'Wycliffe and St. Ann's day schools wM perform the Old English May dance, and the band of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment (Territorials) will discourse sweet music. The Mayoress (Mrs. Forshaw) has kindly consented to present the prizes. Miniature Rifle Shooting. In the competition for the Sir Frederick Harrison Shield, open to London and North-Western Railway clubs throughout the system, the Garston and War- rington railway teams have shot their first ties. The conditions are ten shots at twenty-five yards, open sights only, The following are the scores —Garston: Howarth 04, Scott 87, Matthieson 85, Gregson 85, Murray.' .84, Hastier 80, Skett 75, and James 74— total 664. Warrington: Limb 86, Crompton 85, Mott 82, Noble 79, Devortport 77, Allman - 75, Appleby 74, and Lowe 72—total 630. Warrington Band of Hope Union. The quarterly Council meeting of the above wits held in Gregory's Caf‘ on Thursday evening. In addition to. the Council representatives, special invita- tions had been: issued to other temperance workers to attend, and a large and appreciative gathering took place. The chair was occupied by Mrs, James Dodds (president of the Union), supported' by Rev. J. S. Hughes, Mr. T. Dale, Mr. F. Gandy (hon. secretary), Messrs.. Worley and C. R. Fellows (assistant secre- taries) R. Pierce Williams (treasurer), and Amos Clegg (of Manchester), who attended as a deputation 'from the Lancashire and Cheshire.tßand of Hope and Temperance Unions-, in the interests of the Great 'Forward movement. —During a most interesting address, he appealed to all to take up the question in real earnest, and asked' for every individual's united work in this schenle, that the children of our land may be educated with regard to the evils of alcohol. A discussion followed in which Mrs. Dodds, Mr. Gandy, Mr. Dale, Mr. W. Wright, Mr. J. J. Firth and hey. J. S. Hughes took part.—During the evening Mrs. Dodds presented about 40 volumes of Sir Victer Horseley's latest .book " Alcoho and the Human Body ' to,the speakers and officers of the Union. This is part of a very generous act of Sir W. P. Hartley, who has undertaken to bear the cost of forty thousand of these books to be distributed amongst . the speakers and officers throughout the whole area of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union. This gift is made by Sir William that each speaker may be _in possession of the true 'knowledge of alcohol and its effects on the human body. During his address Mr. Clegg pointed out that Sir William P. Hartley had also undertaken to pay the cost of a special day school teacher for three years that the children may be taught the evils of alcohol. Other questions under discussion were the desirability of holding massed meetings of children throughout the winter and the United Temperance Meeting on Temperance Sunday. Mr. T. Gandy also submitted a brief report of the recent May Festival, which showed the event had been most successful. A Most interesting gathering was brought, to a close by resolutions of thanks being passed to Sir W. 1.. Hartley for his generous gift, to Mr. Clegg for his able address, and to Mrs. Dodds for presiding.</p>
