      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>'fact, will be perfectly fluid, not merely in _name but in fact. Hitherto it has been a liquid perhaps, but a viscous one. This oiling of the wheels of the old industrial machine is to be accompanied by 'something in our view even more important the construction of a whole new apparatus for dealing with those who, through no fault of their own, cannot find, or cannot perform, A system of compulsory insurance is to be established, first in five big trades, then in the others. Hitherto, whether a man insured himself against sickness or unemploy- ment depended very largely upon the 'strength of his imagination. Millions of People are congentitially incapable of realis- ing that worklessness or illness may overtake them until the calamity is actually upon them'. Then they pay a bitter penalty for their shortsightedness, and often the State, too, suffers, for Prolonged poverty and low spirits turn efficient and honest men into unemploy- ables or criminals. In future everybody will be protected against these slings and arrows of outrageous fortune, however unimagina- tive or poor he may be. A portion of the Premiums will be deducted from his wages, 'a. Portion will come from his employer, and the rest in the shape of a subvention from the State. That last word recalls us to what under- lies the whole change. The State, from being a somewhat callous stepmother to the illasses, is developing, if not into a fairy god- niother, then at least into a wise and tender- hearted mother. Liberals, Unionists, Social- We may differ profoundly, as to whether 131.°PertY should be socially owned and controlled .but we all agree more and more lilat People should be socially controlled -?nd cared for. The old theory of the Ill.chistrial system and the State's responsi i 3 - . 1143 T towards those who could find no room it, was ihat the former was a sort of ,trapeze, and that the State had done its ;Ignost duty for the worker who fell off it if lt Provided for him an expanse of Poor-lhw barbed-Wire netting a long way below. That hasgone. The new theory is that' the State 'Should see to it that people are not punished 111°re than can be helped for misfortunes 'Which they cannot prevent. If the present 'Productive system, which necessitates a .el:tairi margin of unemployed, is to be, as we believe it ought to be, preserved, then it is 431ir business to see that those unemployed are well looked after. The case of the sick no discussion whatever. With a kindlier social doctrine in the air than has eigned since the Industrial Revolution, it is .Probable that the next generation will see very many of the ugly blemishes on our ocial structure eliminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="5" unit="words">
