      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>with 18s. per week is doomed and damned to 'existence in these courts and alleys because there is no better accommodation at his price. ,The local authorities are faced not only with the obligation of eliminating the slums but with the neccessity for insti- tuting an upward movement of the popula- tion to better and brighter homes, so that each class may make an advance to appre- ciably better things. Our own Health Committee rests somewhat under a cloud,for while not exactly supine, it has never shown a convincing determination to reform the evils that abound. Opportunity now awaits the hand of enterprise. The Committee might earn fame and praise by a bold policy which would carry it over the shifting sands of expediency. With so much to be done, will the members not be up and doing?</p>
        <p>Parliament has PARLIAMENT'S adjourned for a short "BREATHER." Whitsuntide recess, to meet again on Thurs- day, June 3rd, for the remainder of a Session which is plainly destined to be full of strenuous work. Mr. Asquith has announced that the London Elections Bill, in pursuance of a pledge given last week to the London Liberal Members that the measure should be expedited, will be read a second time on the day after reassembling. He has also declared the Government's intention of passing the Town Planning Bill, which had to be dropped owing to want of time last year, through all its stages this Session. There are also on hand the Welsh Disestablishment Bill and the Irish Land Bill, the Labour Exchanges Bill—which has only been introduced—and the Trade Boards Bill, which has been passing through committee, as well as minor items of legislation. All this, of course, is in addition to a Finance Bill which is likely to be opposed—not to say obstructed—at the very least as stubbornly as the historic measure which embodied Sir William Harcourt's Death Duties. The discussion on Mr. Lloyd-George's Death Duties resolution was chiefly remarkable for Mr. Balfour's denunciation of the tendency inherent, as he alleged, in democracy to gratify the aspira- tions of the many poor at the expense of the wealthy few. On the Report stage of the resolutions imposing higher duties upon spirits and tobacco, various amendments were put forward to give a preference to the Colonies and Protection to Ireland, while a vehement attempt was made to convince the House that the whisky-drinking Scotsman and Irishman were being sacrificed to the beer-drinking Englishman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Fi 0 M</head>
        <p>Fi 0 M</p>
        <p>One of the witnesses at the last sitting of the Warrington Petty. Sessions told the truth when he emphatically asserted that "we did not travel thirty secends in a quarter of a mile." He was giving evidence for the defence in a case of the excess of speed of his brother's motor car, and had evidently got a little embarassed. * * *</p>
        <p>Orchids are expensive luxuries and a rare specimen exhibited at the Temple Flower Show, in London, on Wednesday was sold for £BOO. At the same show a " Cypripedium Sultan "—we thought Sultans were a drug in the market now—and a Beryl, were bought by Alderman Bolton, of Warrington, for 130 and 55 guineas respectively. * * *</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <p>with distinguished political speakers, and the thanks of us all are due to Mr. Crosfield for his untiring and successful efforts in this direction.</p>
        <p>King Edward's Derby victory seems to have given great satisfaction to the sport- loving section cif the community, and it is interesting to recall that the late Shah of Persia happened to oe on a visit to England on one occasion when King Edward had a horse running in the Derby, and it is related that when the animal lost the Shah expressed surprise that any horse other than His Majesty's should be allowed to win! On Wednesday, however, Minoru secured for' the King the Blue Riband of the turf for the third time in His Majesty's career, and the event is historically noteworthy by reason of the fact that it is the first time since its establishment in 1750 that the Derby has been won by any reigning Monarch. Opinions differ as to His Majesty's luck during his long connection with horse-racing. Certainly his most successful year was 1900, when hit horses won altogether over £30,000. • n .••••%"..et Neg</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LOiL NEW 3.</head>
        <p>LOiL NEW 3.</p>
        <p>A Child's Strange Diet. coroner Brighouse held an inquiry yesterday, into the peculiar circumstances attending the death of Elizabeth Bird, aged four, daughter of Henry Bird, of 16, Atherton-street, a furnaceman. —On Saturday last the father putted that the child had some ice ; cream in the afternoon, and later in the day had some chipped potatoes. The following day she was taken ill, being feverish and complained of pains in the head, The next day (Monday) she was noticed to be worse Dr. Martin was called in but the child died on his artival Dr. Smith, who had made a post-mortein examination said that the cauQe of death was doable acute pleurisy or pneumonia.—ln reply to the Coroner the doctor said " ice cream and chip potatoes are not a diet I would recommend for children." A verdict of "natural causes ' was returne I. THE KING.S VISIT.—FLAGS, STREAMERS, BUNT- .; 'NG, etc., for sale or hire. Large stock. —I4—ARLA.ND, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. WaPPlllgtOn Cycling Club • Last Saturday's run to Hale was carried out in glorious weather. A large muster of members turned up at headquarters and the captain was enabled to make an early start. The ride on the outward journey was rather warm, and the roads, which were heavily coated with dust none too agreeable. The destination was reached early in the afternoon, and members turned their attention to bowling. A corn-- ' petitiOn was arranged and in it great interest was ,centred during the afternoon. The. wheelers were joined by a motor party of members later on, and tea was taken at the Childe of Hale Hotel. The return journey was made early in the evening and home reached after another enjoyable Saturday afternoon's ride.—The intended start of the Bowling Competi- tition at Sankey on Wednesd,iy evening was unfortunately prevented by the heavy fall of rain. A fair number of bowlers, however, put in an appear- ance, and some bowling was done, but there were no matches in connection with the competition. The Thursday evening's fixture, the run to Glaiebury, had to be abandoned on account of the rain. The club's arrangements for the coming week are :—Whitsuntide. tour, Sandbach and Ellesmere from to-day until Monday; the bowling competition at Sankey on Wednesday; Thursday evening's run to Bucklow Hill. GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., RS Ss., and £l2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—.l. R. WooD, 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt. Teal and Concert at Legh-street. The quarterly tea and concert in connection with Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church was held on Thursday night in the schoolioom and proved very successful, a good company gathering for tea, after which" a concert was proceedea with, presided over 'by 'Miss Dodds, who gave a short address. The firgt. item was a part song by young ladies entitled " Come away fair maidens," which was well sung, as were two other items by the same party, namely, action song, Japanese Fair" and humorous part song "Rats." Two selected songs by Miss Doris Wilkes were sung well for so young an artist, her sweet voice quite captivating the audience. Miss Beard proved herself a true elocutionist in her rendition of the recitations" The Goblins " and "The Newsboy's Debt," each of which was encored- Two humorous duets, " Love and Pride " and " Very Suspicious," by Misses Buglass and Elwell were well received. A new departure at their effort took the shape of an Irish jig, splendidly executed by Annie Edwards, a little girl of about nine years,which hhil to be repeated. Two humorous dialogues "Wanted a 'Lady Reader "and "Two at a time" the latter of which concluded the concert were extremely well done by the young ladies and provoked much merriment. Miss Annie Titchett ably acted as accompanist, great credit is due to the Missesl3uglass, for their successs- ful superintendance of he arrangements. In addition to the names already mentioned the following young 'ladies took part: Misses Cotton, E. Jones, M. Jones, J. Shaw, M. E. Shaw, P. Ball, E. Spruce, 11/Spruce,N. Woodland and J. Woodland. The Rev. J. B. Buglass proposed a vote of thanks to all concerned, particularly to Miss Dodds for her services in the chair. Miss Dodds briefly responded. The meeting was concluded by singing the doxology. THE KING IS CoMING. But the King of Tonics is here already. H. M. Ashton's Life Tonic—the correct strengthener, ls.—H. M. ASHTON, 63, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Advt. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT . CASES, etc. Special lines at 17s. 205., 225., and 265. 'GARLAND, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>LATEST LOCAL ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>'Arrangements for the reception to be accorded to their Majesties on the occasion of their visit to Warrington on Tuesday, July 6th, are being rapidly pushed forward. 1 At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee held at the Town Hall on Monday afternoon, under the presidency of the Mayor, a Reception Committee was appointed consisting of all the aldermen and the senior councillor of each ward, with power to add to their number, We understand it has been practically agreed that stands be erected along St. Elphin's Park, Manchester road, and in Liverpool-road (near St. Luke's Church) for the accommodation of the school children in the respective districts. It is considered very desirable that the children should be kept out, of the centre of the town, as the streets. will be in such a crowded state that they would probably a risk of being trampled upon. It is hoped that their majesties will make a halt before each stand while the scholars sing a verse of the National Anthem. The children of the Blue Coat School with their band will be provided with places on the • St. Elphin's Park stand. There is every probability that a grand stand will be erected in front of the Town Hall, on each side of the steps, where the members of the Council, magis- trates and other leading townspeople will assemble. Another proposal is to erect a stand at Howard-place ou-ihe site of the old police buildings. The Reception Committee held a meeting on Thurs- day when the following sub-committees were selected: —(a) to supervise the decoration of the streets and puildings ; (b) to arrange for the school children's part in the rejoicings ; (a) to attend to the stands and allotment of seats; (a) to deal with the roads and traffic and to make the necessary arrangements with the Territorials and Police; (d) Finance and Execu- tive., The Committee co-opted the following gentlemen to assist the sub-committees in their arrangements : Decorations Sub-Committee Councillor Birtles and Mr. "Segar Owen; School Children Sub-Committee, Mr. R. T. Fairclough and Councillor Locker; Roads and Traffic sub-committee, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart„ Colonel W. Hall Walker, M.P. Colonel Reid Walker, Colonel J. D. Fairclough, and Major Edridge ; Finance and Executive Sub-committee, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., Colonel W. Hall Walker, M.P., Colonel Reid Waiker, Mr, Robert Pierpoint, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, and Mr. C. E. Parker. The sub- committees will all meet in the course of next week. PADGATE SCHOOL CHILDREN AND THE VISIT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians on. Wednesday, Mr". G-. Mather mentioned that at the meeting of the Cottage Homes Committee the previous day there was a conversation with reference to the King's visit to Warrington. According to the published programme, the Royal cars would pass within a short distance of the schools. The com- mittee arranged that the children should assemble in Manchester-road near the Borough boundary and the Clerk was asked to write to Lord Derby asking that the cars ,should slow down in order that the children should be afforded an opportunity of seeing their Majesties. The Board empowered the Cottage Homes Com- mittee to take what action they considered desirable in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PADGATE REQUEST.</head>
        <p>A PADGATE REQUEST.</p>
        <p>A. meeting of ratepayers was held in the Padgate C.E. Schools on Thursday .evening, Mr. Denham occupying the chair. The object of the meeting was to discuss the best means by which the children of the Padgate Industrial Schoolsund the day school's might have an opportunity of seeing the Kin.. on the day of his vitit to Warrington, viz., July 6th.—Mr. G-. Mather said a little had been done in the matter, and he put . forward a suagestion that waggons be arranged alongside the Manchester-road so that the children might be placed upon them, and thus have a good position from which to see their Majesties. He also . suggested that the place where the waggons were should be decorated with flags and bunting. The Chairman pointed out the fact that the Rural District oouncil could not put any 'expense on the rates for this 'outlay, and all the costs of . decorations, &amp;c., Would have to be borne voluntarily. It was decided to write to Lord Derby sending a request that the Royal motors should slow down</p>
        <p>Warrington is fortunate in possessing an enthusiastic corps of the St. John Ambulance Brig ade,and encourag- ine evidence Of the progress of the movement was afforded by the large attendance at the distribution of certificates &amp;c., which took place in the Crosfield Assembly Room on Wednesday evening. At seven o'clock tea was served and this was followed by a social. Captain W. Waring (Chief Superintendent) presided and amongst others present were: Tee Mayor and Mayoress (AI de rman arid Mrs. Forshaw), Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. and Mrs. Crosfield, Mrs. Dodds, Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst, Rev. J. L. T. Darwall, Dr. Adams (chief hon. surgeon), and Miss Adams, Mr. S. Baldry (superintendent), Councillor and : Mrs. Evans, Mr. and the Misses Broadbent, ; Councillor W. B. Roberts, Mrs. Barber, Dr. and Mrs. E. J. Fox, and Mr. E. A. Spann (hon.secretary). , An admirable musical progiamme was sionnitted and appreciatively teeeived. Messrs. Crosfield's orchestra, ' under the conductorship of Mr. Ogden, played the selections " Sweet Briar" and "Emerald Isle" in praise- worthy si yle. Miss Violet Brown, a promising young vocalist, gave artistic interpretations of "Orange blossom " and "When the heart is young' " responding ta an encore with" Home, sweet home." Mr. Reynolds . sang" Glorious Devon" with good effect. The piano- forte solo "La Fontaine" was cleverly executed by Miss Gernall. The humorous element was entrusted to Mr. Victor Roberts who scored a great succsss, his song abeut the suffragettes gc jug with a rare swing. Miss Goruall and Mrs. Greaves discharged the duties of accompanists. During an interval Mrs. Crosfield performed the phasing ceremony of distributing the prizes, certifi- cates and other awards. It wasaannotinced that letters of apelogy for non-attendance bad been received from Mr. and Mrs. J. J. Crostield, Dr. Markel, Mr. and Mrs. W. Peter Rylands, Mr. arid Mrs. Parr. Chief Superintendent ‘VAISING, in a short speech, extended a hearty welcome to Mrs. Crosfield. They all knew her husband. and no words of his (the speaker's) were required in Mr. Crosfield's praise. What he did, he did with his whole heart and soul. Warrington ambulance men revered the neme of Crosfield ; without their assistance there would be no Warrington Corps. the ambulance brigade existed in order to keep men in practice, and to see that • knoe ledge in first aid once acquired was not forgotten and so wasted. It required a great deal of enthusiasen to induce a man to keep in touch with the work continuously, and he was pleased and proud to see such veterans as Sergt. Banning, Sergt. Fell, Private Kirby and others still sticking to ambulance work as enthusiastically as when they first took it up. When they considered that the Corps had no Chancellor of the Exchequer at its back, that all the men and women belonging to the Corps or Nursing Division paid an annual subscription and made them- selves efficient by putting in at least twelve drills a year, and that those in uniform had paid part of the cost of it, tkey would readily see that they were work- ing for the love of the thing and because of their desire to act the good Samaritan in the event of an accident or case of sudden illness coming before their notice. They heard a great deal nowadays about the National Service League or,in the alternative, a scheme of conszription. It might interest them to know that every St. John Ambulance man or woman in that room had agreed to act in home military hospitals if required and that some had joined the R.1T.5.8.R. and had been *rained on board his Majesty's ships or in naval hospital. One of Crosfield's division only a short time ago bad a week's training in the Chester hospital. He (the speaker) was a great believer in the need for training, so that should occasion arise the material that would be required in ,the best interests of the country Would he ready to hand. Some of their men were in the Territorials and in his opinion the Territorial who had a knowledge of first aid was a far better man than one who had not. A man's fighting capacity would not be lessened by his being well up in ambulance work while on the other band he would have it in his power, by the ready aid he would be able to render, to save many lives to fight again. He (the speaker) was told by a man who was on Spion Kop that Captain Birch bled to death, whereas his life would have been saved had there been anyone near who knew kow to stop the bleeding. In conclusion Captain Waring expressed his thanks to the medical men for their lectures, to firms and other gentlemen for assist- ance flnancially,and to the Watch Committee for the use of the Police Station. Mra. CROSFIELD then presented the awards as ollocS: Libels, H. T. Williams (Dallam Forge), A. Hankinson (Dallam Forge), E. Dower, T. Gilt, and E. Fleming (Walker's Brewery), T. Davies (Corporation Highway department), W. D. Fell (Ship Canal), W. A. Banning (Dakin's, Latchford), H. Hampton (Hampton Bros.), T. Broomhall (" Guardian " Office), J. Williamson (Post Office), T. Kirby (Massey's, Latchford) ; medallions, I'. Weston, F. T. Jubb, V. tialford, H. liarrop ("Guardian" Office) • vouchers, Si. Wright, Ayres, W. Webb, D. Clarke, Fox, J. Tomkins, G. W. Freeman(" Guardian" Office); Fa rst aid certifieates,G.Straw (Fletcher,R ussel Is), H. Bottomley (Corporation Electricity Department), Massey, Cheney, Ince,Hawthorne (Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd.). Loftus (Monks, Hall), A. Smith (Hill and Company),E.Hill (Hutch in g's Tannery),Cotteri 11, Barbtr, J. Waring, Hadcroft, Wright ansl J. P. Roscoe (Whitecross Company) ; nursing certificates, Captain Waring (Ship Canal), A. Hankinson (Dallam Forge), T. Weston, Broomhill,Flerrop, Halford, Jubb (" Guardian" Office), Cawley, Wormersley. Whitby, Webb, o)tley, Gleave, Lightfoot, James, Fisher (Crosfield's.) Attendance prizes (presented by Councillor W. B. Roberts, Dr. Markel and Chief Superintendent Waring) were haled to Sergeant Banning (biscuit barrel) Sergeant Fell (egg stand), Mr. Spann (butter dish), Private Clements (carvers), Private Smith (clock). Dr. E. J. Fox was the recipient of some books sob- :scribed for by the members of the nursing class. and Dr. Adams was presented by the corps with a silver- mounted cigarette 130 X. Dr. ADAMS, in moving a vote of thanks to Mrs. Crosfield, emphasised the indebtedness of the Ambulance movement in Warrington to the Crosfield family. He (Dr. Adams) had been connected with the corps for a long time. It was eighteen or nineteen years since he received his first instruction from the then secretary, whose mother and sisters he was delighted to see ' present that evening and who still honoured the cerps with their presence when they had any gatherings. He (Dr. Adams) succeeded to the secretaryship hut when he had more lectures to give he was unable to continue the duties, and Captain George Crosfield took over the office. Since that date the name of Crosfield had been indissolubly associated with the movement. Miss Truda Crosfield had on more than one occasion performed the same gracious office that Mrs. Arthur Crosfield had done for them that night, and it was an additional pleasure that now the latest addition to the family to bear the name of Crosfield had kindly consented and had so generously given away the prizes. He was sure the recipients of the 'awards would value there the more because they had received them at the hands of the wife of their genial, gifted and popular member of Parliament. (Applause). Superintendent BILDRY seconded and the vote was warmly passed. Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, AT.P„ said he had been asked to respond to the vote, but he really thought he was in the wrong place and that it would have been much better if his wife had responded herself. (Laughter and applause). But he supposed that she had taken the hint from a very interesting and graphic song they had just now, from which he gathered that it would not be very long that some of the men were going to have much of a look in, in the way of speaking—(laughter)— and therefore perhaps she thought he had better have an innings on that occasion. (Laughter.) He could not say anything as felicitous and charming as Dr. Adams had said, but he thanked them very sincerely on behalf of his wife. It was a real pleasure to her and to himself to he present,and to join with everybody else in congratulating the members of the St. John Ambu- lance Brigaate on their attention to duty, and upon the spirit that prompted them to do that work, of such inestimable value both in the sad time of war and in the days when peace prevailed. In these days one continu- ally got connected with questions which in the sphere of commerce gave rise to competition, and in the sphere of politics to controversy,and it was a pleasure once in a way to come to a meeting like that, where they all stood on one common platform without any arriere pensee and offer their hearty congratulations and show their respect for those whose work they so greatly and sincerely admired. (Applause). - Hearty votes of thanks to Messrs. Crosfield and the artistes concluded the proceedings..</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission: Sunday School Anniversary. The Sunday school anniverary in connection with Wycliffe Cockhedge Mission was held lait Sunday in the mission. Mr. W. H. Wallingtan trained the children for the singing and the way in which the hymns were rendered was a testimony to the excellent instruction they had received. The organist was Mr. Reginald Wailington. Miss Taylor was the speaker in the morning and she toudhed upon the value of boys' services. She entreated them to use their best efforts in doing good in the servvice of the Master.—ln the afternoon a cantata "The building of the Temple" was given by the choir and scholars. Mi. J. W. Slack was the reader.—The Rev. Thomas Miller WRS the preacher in the evening and spoke of the mother's influence upon the children, taking as s his example Hannah and her son Samuel. Ile pointed out that the mother's life was of vast importance in the training of the child,for what the child was to-day the nation would be to-morrow.—The collections,which were in aid of the Sunday school funds, and were subscribed to by fairly large congregations, amounted to rather more than usual.</p>
        <p>Sir,,—As Secretary of one of the `Nonconformist Sunday schools, I would like to point out that the alteration made by the Town Council regarding the date of our annual Sunday school -festival, will if adhered to, entail a great deal of inconvenience which is altogether unneceggary, and could be avoided.. Many of our schools spend the day out of town, where accommodation for cutting *up the food, &amp;c., is very limited. This cutting up is therefore done on the evening previous, and the food Packed in hampers ready for distribution. If Walking Day is held on Monday, this work • will have to be don 3 either on Sunday (to which we are all strongly opposed of course) or on Saturday, iu which case the food will become dry and unfit for use by Monday. There are other objections, e.g. additional. hire money will probably have to be paid for tents which will be put on Saturday ready for Monday, and railway arrangements, &amp;c., already niade will have to be altered. The change is not wanted by the Sunday schools, and certainly should not have been made in so arbitrary a manner.</p>
        <p>Remembering that the festival is only and altogether one connected with Sunday schools, the Superinten- dents of each might at least have been written to with the object of learning their views. There was surely time for this before the General Purposes Committee meeting.—Yours, &amp;c., 27th May, 1909</p>
        <p>F. T. L.</p>
        <p>[COPY OF LETTER SENT TO TOWN _CLERK.]</p>
        <p>Dear- Sir,—As a Sunday School official T protest against the action of the Town Council in their unwarranted interference in altering the date of Walking Day without first consulting with all the Sunday School officials of the town.</p>
        <p>There is absolutely no necessity to alter the date, inasmuch as the King is not due in Warrington until after 5 o'clock on Tuesday afternoon and the scholars have no part whatever in the proceeding. An hour or two's stoppage of the works on Tuesday afternoon would give everyone an opportunity of seeing His Majesty. Further, Monday is an impossible day for Walking Day from the 'commissariat standpoint, food which would have to be cooked and prepared on the Satur- day would be unfit for consumption on the following Monday, and the health of the children is the first consideration.</p>
        <p>Any alteration of the date at this time will com- pletely upset arrangements which are now well in hand.</p>
        <p>I trust the Sunday school officialtwill make a strong effort to secure the original date and also to vindicate their right to have a voice in the management of their own affairs.</p>
        <p>W. BROWN, Superintendent Wycliffe Sunday school, May fah, 1909.</p>
        <p>Clergy's Objection.</p>
        <p>A meting of clergy to consider the proposed altera- tion of the date of Walking Day was held in the Holy Trinity Parish Room yesterday afternoon, under the presidency of the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis). The meeting.unanimously decided that it was inadvisable to depart from the original date, Friday, July 2nd.and. the Rector was requested to call a public mee‘ting of representatives of all denominations to be held on Wednesday at 8 o'clock in the Nationil Schools, Church-street.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Sunday School Officials' Protest</p>
        <p>- At a. meeting h i eld n the Bold-street Wesleyan School last evening, under the presidency of the Rev. E. B. Keeling, the following resblutien was passed :—" That this meeting of officials of the Wesleyan: Sunday Schools at 80ld-street, Silver-street, Ellesmere-street, and Sankey Bridges, representing upwards of 1,500 scholars, has con- sidered the Town's Clerk's letter of the 25th inst. respecting the alteration in the date of Walking Day and cannot acquiesce in the decision of the Town. Council. It also strongly protests against a change of this description being sprung, upon the schools without their being consulted. In some instances arrangements' hava already been made -Which cannot be altered and the inconvenience which would be involved in the case of each of the schools represented. practically prohibits a change."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
