        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>whilst passing this particular spot, and the children would sing the National Anthem whilst their Majesties were passing. On the motion of Mr. G. Mather, seconded by Mr. W. J. Loxton, the meeting was adjourned to awn' the reply of Lord Derby,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>AMBULANCE WORK.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE WORK.</p>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD PRESENTS PRIZES AND CERTIFICATES.</p>
        <p>DATE CF WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL'S SUDDEN DECISION., Protests From all Quarters</p>
        <p>Considerable surprise and annoyance were created in Warrington when it became known that the Town Council, assembled as a General Purposes Com- mittee on Monday, had decided, in consequence of thet proposed Royal visit to Warrington on July 6th, to postpmie the annual procession of Sunday School Scholars which would this year fall on Friday, the 2nd July, to the following Monday, the sth July. We hear that the question was debated at considerable length, but the Council was unanimous in its decisvp. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), in a circulitlr letter to the local clergy and ministers says "This decision has been arrived at in the general interests of the inhabitants, and the Council think that the change of date will be better for the children, though they regret to hear that in one or two instances it will interfere with arrangements already made for the 2nd July. The Council further wish me to explain that it was considered imperative that a decision should be arrived at immediately, and that there was not sufficient tune to call a meeting for the purpose of ascertaining the views of fhe representatives of the various :Sunday schools. Under the exceptional cir- cumstances the Council trust that you will readily acquiesce in their decision."</p>
        <p>There is, however, little sign of ready acquiescence, and a number of protests from Sunday school workers and others have already been forwarded to the Town Hall. For instance, the committee of the Co-operative Society object on the ground that the alteration would interfere with the Society's proposed trip to London, the arrangements for which have been in hand for some considerable time. Sunday school superintendents and workers, as far as can be gathered, are indignant that the Council should have come .to such a decision without consulting the parties most closely connected with the working of the children's festival. The General Purposes Committee will probably meet again on Tuesday or Wednesday next, and they will then -have an opportunity of con- sidering the many objections raised and, it is hoped, of reverting to the old date, which is undoubtedly the most popular with the majority of the townspeople.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>PROPOSED ALTERATION OF ROUTE.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED ALTERATION OF ROUTE.</p>
        <p>Complaints having been received of the interference with the street traffic, more particularly that passing over Warrington Bridge, the General Purposes Com- mittee on Monday passed a resolution directing the Chief Constable to make other arrangements, it is understood that Parker-street, Wilson-Patten-street„ and Mersey-street, cannot now be used for the pro cession, but an alternative route has not yet been arranged.</p>
        <p>Letters of Protest. [TO TIIE EDITOR. OF TIIE EX.IIIISER.I Sir, The odvertisement in Wednesday's " Guardian "and the Circular from the Town Clerk, addressed to Clergy, Ministers, and Superintendents of Sunday Schools, concerning the alteration of Walking Day consequent upon the King's visit, appears to render a conference desirable of all parties specially interested in Walking Day. Many years ago this matter of Walking Day came under public consideration, and a meeting was held, under the presidency of the Rector, at which the present date of that Festival was finally agreed upon. I submit that the Town Council, doubtless with the best intentions, has exceeded its powers in. upsetting the arrangement, even for so supreme an occasion as the proposed visit of the King. Only by conference with those especially interested should this matter be dealt with.</p>
        <p>I trust that the Rector may be able to see his way to convene such a meeting early in the coming week— unless—which would save all further controversy— the' City Fathers "should decide to leave the cele- bration, and the date, of the Childrens' Annual Day in the hands of the recognised authorities for the, control of that Festival.—Faithfully yours, Wycliffe Church, May 28th, 1909.</p>
        <p>SOHN YONGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Warrington Chamber of Commerce.</head>
        <p>Warrington Chamber of Commerce.</p>
        <p>Ate meeting of the Council held on Thursday, which was very largely attended, the President, Mr. Oswald Lofthouse, in the chair, a long discussion took place as to the proposal made by the Corporation to change the date of Walking Day to Monday, the sth of July. It was generally felt that, in the interests of the large industrial concerns of Warrington and in order to avoid broken weeks, the change was desirable; but, having regard to the many other interests affected, and especi- ally to the Sunday schools, it was eventually decided, on the motion of Mr. Jarman, seconded by Mr. R. T. Fairclough, "That this Council recommends that in view of all the circumstances, Walking Day should be held on the last Friday in June, as usual."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
