      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>WARRINGTON POLICENIAN ATTACKED.— SERIOUS AFFRAY AT BEWSEY. • At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Richard Clancy and Catherine Clancy, 116, Pierpoint-street, Bewsev-road, were charged with maliciously wounding P.C. Thomas henry Faulkner.—The Chief Constable stated that #-shortly before eleven o'clock on Saturday night P.C. 'Faulkner heard a row in a passage at the rear of Pier- point-street. On proceeding there, the male prisoner cime up to him and struck him a violent blow in the eye with his fist. The constable closed with him and whilst they were struggling, the female prisoner struck the officer across the head with a bottle, rendering him insensible. Chief Inspector Williamson arrived on the scene, and the injured constable was conveyed to the Infirmary, where his wound was dressed. He was still detained in the institution, and he (the Chief Con- stable) therefore asked for the case to be remanded until Wednesday.—The application was granted.— At the Borough Mice Cow% on Tuesday Joseph Clancy, Pierpoint-street, was also charged with assault- ing Constable Faulkner on Saturday night.—The Chief Constable again related the facts of the case and said prisoner flew at the constable and seized him by the throat. Prisoner was remanded until the following day. —Mary Ann Clancy was charged with using threats towards Mabel Marsh !n the early hours of Sunday morning.—The Chief Constable stated that the girl Marsh witnessed the assault on Constable Faulkner and with great courage went to his assistance when he was in sore straits. The police interviewed her at one o'clock on Sunday morning and took a state- ment from her. Later on prisoner went to the house and used threats towards the girl, who was in fear that the prisoner would do her bodily harm.—Evidence having been given, prisoner was bound over to keep the peace for six months in the sum of £5 and ordered to. find another surety of £5 or one month's imprison- ment.—The Chairman (Mr. H. Roberts) said the Bench thoroughly appreciated the assistance rendered to the police by the girl Marsh, and he had no doubt that when the magistrates heard the case on Wednesday they would recognize that.—The Cnief Constable said he had not told the Bench the whole of the facts, but intended to ask the magistrates to reward the girl.— On Wednesday morning Richard Clancy, Joseph Clancy, and Catherine Clancy, Pierpoint-street, were again charged with ass tutting Police-Constable Faulkner on Saturday night.—The Chief Constable said it was hoped the officer, who was brutally assaultee, would be able to appear, but the doctor at the Infirmary had refused to allow him out, us be had not completely recovered.—The magistrates remanded • the prisoners until Wednesday next. TWELVE MONTHS FOR FALSE PRETENCES. William Holloway, of Welfield-street, was charged at the Warrington It'olice Court on Wednesday with obtaining by false pretences, from Christopher Bowers, of linutstord-road, 16 tans :newts. of manure valued at £4 lls. ; also with obtaining by false pretences 2 tons Newt. of manure from John Lee, Lion Hotel, and 2 tons 91 cart. from William Henry Burleigh, Patten Arms. He pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable stated that the •circumstances were the same in each cane. The prisoner obtained the manure by stating that he was a repre- sentative of Major Foulkes who was then with his regiment at Aldershot and was having a house built at Sankey. The house, he said, was nearing completion, and he wanted some manure for his garden. It was afterwards found that the story was all a myth. Mr. Mr. Bowers went to the Orford Barracks and was told that the authorities there bed never heard of a Major Foulkes. The manure was afterwards found at a farm at Penketh. The prisoner obtained the manure in a similar way from the Patten Arms and the Lion Hotel. Continuing the Chief Constable stated that, prisoner bad previously been charged with similar offences. - In September last he was sentenced to two months at 'Chester Castle and in the following December to three months. Other charges could be prefered against prisoner, such as cartage of the manure, etc., by John Jackson, contractor. The Chairman (Mr. C. White), said the prisoner had on several occasions received short sentences and the Bench had now decided to impose a long one—namely that of 12 months imprisonment, which would give him time to reflect. They hoped he would come out an honest man. Prisoner left the dock smiling.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court. MoNDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Aldermen Smethurst and Tinnion, Councillors Wilkinson and Evans. Messrs. W. Roberts, H'. Thornton. T. Pember- ton and G;Pilling. FOOTPATH OBSTRUCTION.—Richard Harding, marine store dealer Buttermarket-street, was summoned for causing obstruction in Scotland-road by allowing a number of bales of piper to be left there.—He pleaded guilty.—P.C. Hayes stated that about 10.35 on May 18th he was on duty in Scotland-road when be siw a number of bales of paper on the footpath. On passing two hours later the bales were still there.—Defendant was fined 3s. 6d.—Walter Sutton, house furnisher, Market.. street, was summoned for obstructing the footpath in front of his shop by allowing three mail carts to stand there.—lt was stated that the mail carts were left on the footpath for three hours. Defendant bad been •cantioned, but he persisted in putting them out. When an officer told a young lady in the shop that he would have to report the case, she replied "You are at liberty to do so."—The Chief Constible said thit complaints were so frequent that the police had to take steps to -stop the practice.—He was fined 3s. Gd. A LOAFER'S RlGHTS.—Thomtis McMullen,Shepherd's- row ; Walter Lewis, Rose and Crown-street; and Walter Norbury, Roome-street, were all charged with obstruct- ing Winwick-street, near the Central Station.—P.C. Hayes stated that in consequence of complaints received, he was on duty in Winwick,street about 11 a.m. when he saw the defendants standing at the bottom of the Central Station steps. They remained there twenty minutes, obstructing the, footpath. Witness had previously cautioned them.—The defend- ants stated that they were employed to carry baggage for commercial travellers and were waiting for their arrival.—Deputy Mayor: It is right that some of these men are employed as stated above--Chief Con- stable: Yes, but that does not give them the fight. to obstruct the footpath.—Constable Hayes WWI recalled and said he bad cautioned the defendants no fewer than .50 times in one week.—The defendants were fined 28.6 d. and costs (9s. 6d.) or seven days. A RECORD.—The following were fined for keeping .dogs without licences :—Thomas Ryley, Evelyn-street, Bs. ; Arthur Rosterne, Orford-street, 9s. (in the latter case defendant slipped out at the front door and obtained a licence while Detective Reddington was waiting at the hack) ; Arthur Rogers, C ,therine-street ; John Howard, Clegg-street, ss.—What was described at a record by the Chief Constable was secured by William 111orley, Priestley-street, who VMS summoned for keep- ing his dog without a licence.—Defendant said he had kept the same dog for fifteen years and bad not had to _pay a id.—Tbe case was dismissed. TUESDAY.—Beiore Messrs. H. Roberts (in the chair) J. Rymer Young, C. Jackson Holmes, W. Richardson, and Edwin Woods. Os THE ROOF.—William or Michael Power, aged 76, of no settled address was charged on remand with being found on the enclosed premises of the Warrington Motor Carriage Company on the 17th May for an unlawfhl purpose.—He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner was before the .Court the previous Tuesday, and an adjournment was .asked for in order to inquire lot" his character Thr</p>
        <p>.. • • Q - ".• _ .rt line A I tr. • • • ou kiTs „„, . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>%Eilimmu</head>
        <p>%Eilimmu</p>
        <p>circumstances of the case were, that on Monday night May 17th, P.C. Corbett was on duty in Garveu Place, Saukey'street. On examining the premises of the Motor Carriage Company he saw the the prisoner climbing on the roof of the carage and going in the direction of the Education Offices. The officer immediately climbed on the roof and went in search of the prisoner, who on seeing him, ran to the edge of the roof and jumped on to some waste land adjoining the premises, a distance of 12 feet. The officer gave chase'and with the assistance of Mr. Smith, of the Smith Arms, Legh-street, and Constable Sztunders, he succeeded in arresting prisoner. When arrested he had one boot and one stocking on. On searching the premises, Corbett found the other boot and sticking on the roof. It bad been ascertained that prisoner was an old convict and was at present under police supervision. He had been sentenced to several terms of imprison- ment at Liverpool, Glasgow, snd other towns, for housebreaking, etc. He had also ser red termv in prison abroad.--A sentence of one months' imprisonment was imposed.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor and other magistrates. BETTING AND CHILD NF.GLECT.—John Gordon Bowen and Mary Bowen, 18, Hopwood-street, were charged with neglecting their two children.—Mr. A. Browne prosecuted and said the charge was of neglect- ing their two children aged 12 and 8. The male prisoner was a tanner acd could earn 30s. weekly, but he was given to that wretched habit of betting and whenever he hippened to be a winner he. never would work. He had not worked from the 10th until last Monday. Both prisoners were equally to blame. They had been ejected from numerous houses and in consequence the children Ivid nowhere to sleep, the result being that they had been absolutely neglected. On Friday night last the two children were brought to the office of Inspector Cox in a deplorable condition. They were looking hungry, and they removed to the Workhouse. Two other children of the prisoners,which were previously taken to the Workhouse, had since died. —An adequate sentence of six months was imposed on each prisoner.</p>
        <p>°Uri (ie I • .7` hj t' 1/1,ct.0,1 91/14-i --- .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>St. Paul's Church</head>
        <p>St. Paul's Church</p>
        <p>pecial services in aid of the choir fund were held at this church on Sunday, the vicar (Rev. W. Bracecarnp) preaching in the morning, and the Rev. A. J. Brett, one of the assistant clergy at the Parish Church, in the evening. There was a crowded congregation at the evening service when the music was ot no elaborate and tasteful character. The choir had the assistance of an orchestra composed of members of Mr. Down's band. Mr. B. Wilkes and one of his pupils (Mr. Robert Dumbill) presided at the organ, and Mr. Wilkes also discharged the duties of conductor. The anthem "By Babylon's wave" (Gounod) was expressively sung, and the orchestral introduction was admirably plased. Master Norman W. Brown, of Atherton, sang " Mendelssohn's "Hear my prayer" very sweetly, and Miss Nellie Anderton's interpretation of Gounods "0 Divine Redeemer" was chnracterised by great depth of feeling and artistic expression.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. There was a fair attendance on Sunday last, when a meeting was heldspecially in support of the Anti- Vivisection Society and to hear Mr: R. E. O'Callaghan. of Manchester. who gave an address upon the evils of Vivisection. He dwelt upon the goodwill of God in His creation of man of the higner order, and the dumb animals in their order. God's goodness was not to illtreat, to inflict unnecessary punishment upon the dumb creatures but to allow them to fulfil their own usefulness in life. The strength of the horse, the faithfulness of the dog, and the whole animal world were subvervient to the great service of man and to his great enjoyments. Tbe address was very interesting, and instructive. Mr. W. R. Jenkius presided and spoke upon the teichings of men, who had always before them the great Ideil of love and mercy. The secretary also supported the anti-vivisection society, when he sought the good of all, with pain for none. Mr. A. Healey led the prayer. Mr. W. FL Payton, Mus. Bac., presided at the organ,'and rendered an organ solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Ragged School Mission.</head>
        <p>Ragged School Mission.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with the Ragged School Mission were held on Sunday. The ser- vice in the afternoon was conducted by Mrs._ James</p>
        <p>Dodds' i who gave an adress upon" Naaman the Leper." The children are always delighted to see Mrs. Dodds, and tier helpful and kindly remarks were attentively listened to. In the evening the President, Mr. James Dodds, presided over a good attendance. The service commenced by the singing of the hymn "Pleasant are Thy courts above," followed by prayer by Councillor Skelland. Mrs. R. E. Letheren, who was the speaker at, night, said she had not come specially to speak to the children, but rather to the workers to try and fill them with inspiration so that they might continue to do their little part in helping to build the wall of the New Jerusalem as Nehemiah and his servants laboured in building the walls of Jerusalem. Christian workers met with scoffs and discouragoments, but they could not afford to pay heed to them. Each should endeavour to perform his or her duty faithfully, and as well as Nehemiah and his servants.—The Chairman said all would agree with him when he said that they must all feel that it had been good to be present. He could not wish to spend the night in a better manner, as Mrs. Letheren had so ably dealt with every detail. He felt it a thorough joy to sit and listen to her, and Warring- ton was greatly in need of a few more Christian ladies like her. Special hymns and solos were sung by the children, assisted by members of St. Barnabas' Orchestral Band. Mr. NV. Wright sang "Dying with Jesus."</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Church. Sunday School Anniversary. The Sunday school anniversary in connection with the above was held in the church on Sunday last. Good attendances prevailed at each service.- Dr. .1. Louis Fenn (secret try of tho Lancashire and Yorkshire District of the Evangelisation Society) was the preacher. The children sang special hymns very beauti- fully and were trained by the Misses Liankey. Mr. S. Rickers presided at the organ. Preaching in the morning the Rev. J. Louis FENN took as his text "The Lord God has given me the tongue of the learned, that I should know how to speak a word in season to him that is weary; He wakeneth morning by morning, He wakeneth mine ear to hear as the learned "(Isaiah 50. iv.). He said that they there that morning were scholars in God's school, learning day by day lessons, sometimes slowly, sometimes more rapidly, sometimes having to go over the same ground again and again before they learned the lesson, but all the time being'taught with infinite patience and taught that they might become efficient scholars. The first lesson that God always taught was about sin. But while God spoke of sin and condemned it, it was only that we might learn the second lesson, that of pardon- ing it. He freed them , from the power of sin. Having learned the first lessons in the school of God, we ought to learn the purpose of our salvation. The purpose of our salvation was service. We were not saved for our own peace and happiness alone, we were not brought into fellowship that we might just sit down and fold our arms and enjoy peace. The world needed and the Lord needed servants to-day everywhere. In every department of life the scholar of God must be a servant. We needed to learn the lesson that we had lying upon us the same weight of responsibility that was lying upon the shoulders of our Lord Jesus Christ in His earthy ministry. He contended that every Christ - ald lead men to Christ. We needed faith</p>
        <p>by which we might hold on to Him, and trust by which we might rest in Him all the time. Concluding the rev. gentleman said there never was a greater need for the whole-hearted devotion of our Sunday school teacher's than to-day. The difficulties were greater than ever. Tne workers required better equipment than in the past. The teachers should endeavour to bring the scholars into living touch with tbe living Christ. In the afternoon a scholars' service was held, pre- sided over by Mr. George Wallington. Dr. Fenn gave an address and solos were well rendered by Miss Ada Hamer and Miss May Willett„ the former singing at the evening servie. _</p>
        <p>In tlie evening Dr.Fenn spoke on three passages from Leviticus mentioning knowledge' pleasure, happiness and wealth and urged all to use these for the highest and noblest purpose, the glory of God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 29, 1939.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 29, 1939.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. William Crosfleld The Rev. John Yonge's Tribute.</p>
        <p>An eloquent and sympathetic t—ribute to the memory of the late Mr. William Crosfield was paid by the Rev. John Yonge in Wycliffe Cengregationsl Church last Sunday evening. The rev, gentleman took as hintext "Enoch walked with God: and he was not: for God took him" (Genesis 5-24.) In the course of his sermon he said it seemed to him that William Crosfield, of Liverpool, who passad away last Monday afternoori, as by swiftest, transition from the field of his manifold labour of love to his reward in Heaven, was a true illustration of what it meant fore man to "walk with God" in the life on earth. Unless he had formed an entirely mistaken idea of Mr. Crosfield, the supremely cherished and diligently pursued aim of his life was to he of service. Toe preacher's knowledge and observa- tion of him extended over 40 Years. In the earlier and perhaps the greater part of that time he was engaged in a large and important business, of which on the death of his father in 1881, he became the head. , But long before he reached that position, his leisure , time and strength were devoted to works of philaotbropyand Christian service either in connection with the Congregational Church in Great George- street Liverpool, where he had been brought up from childhood, or with various agencies for philanthropy in Liverpool. For 20 years he was Superintendent of a large Ragged School, which met on Sunday evening, and where he was nearlyal ways to be found working among the waifs and strays, the half fed, half clad denizen of the slum and the gutter. His heart went out to those outcasts of society, for whom comparatively nobody cared. Throughout. his career, even to its last day, and lest hour he was deeply interested in the poorer classes. It was in their behalf, indeed, that last Monday he put forth his latest effort and expended his last breath ! Mr. Crosfield was a man of strong convictions, and even, as some would say, rigid practices. He was a total abstainer throughout his whole life, and took a leading port in temperance work in Liverpool even in days when such work was avoided by many as unpopular. But he was too thorough and too true Christian a man to he deterred from a good work, because it might not be popular. He was also 'a diligent and able worker in the corporate affairs and public interests of the City of Liverpool, for many years being a member, of the City Council, also of the Dock Board and of other principal institutions of the city. lie Ayes also an ardent, active and convinced , politician, being always a steadfast advocate of all measures of progress and reform. Indeed, much earlier than was the case he might have held high office in his , political party in Liverpool, but he ever preferred hard Work to personal prominence. The writer of the sketch which appeared in the "Liverpool Daily Post" accurately said that "though not averse to serving on public bodies, and giving his services for the good of the ratepayers, he shrank from the ordeal of soliciting votes, and was content more or less, that his well-known principles, and his reputation for integrity should be allowed to speak for themselves." It was that characteristic and persistent modesty and the entire lack of self-assertiveness, which probably stood in the way of his earlier success to municipal and national politics. But when, at length, be was elected to the City Council, he served his city with conspicuous success. Many there that night would remember the Parlin-] menu:try election of 1585. When Mr. McMinnies. declined to again enter Parliament, Mr. Crosfield became the Liberal candidate for Warrington. He made a stout, strenuous, and entirely honourable attempt to win the election. Some there were who complained' that, to use their own phrase, "he would not take his gloves off." The meaning of the metaphorical, phrase was probably that Mr. Crostield was "too particular," and that he would have nothing to do wi; h practices in political conflict, which had become only too common and well-known in the past. No one who kaew the man would be surprised to find that he had defln ite, even rigid, limits, to his ideas of righteous and Christian strategies in politicat warfare. The men did not exist who could deflect him, even in the heat and rush of conflict, from his established ideas of whit was right ! Manfully he fought the election with clean hands; and manfully he accepted defeat! ! The Preacher was by his side in the building opposite his Church woen the disastrous result of the poll was brought to him. With manly courage and courtesy, which did not fail him for an instant, he wont to the window of the Club, and, addressing the crowd in the street, in his first words offered his congratulations to his opponent upon his decisive victory. It was one of the great critical moments in a man's life, in which his spirit was most strongly tested. Mr. Crosfield successfully stood the test, both with admirable presence of mind and noble generosity of spirt. Twelve months later he contested Lincoln, being again defeated. But in 1892 hesucceeded in winning that seat, retaining it thoughout that Parliament, but at the following Dissolution he did not. again attempt to enter the House of Commons. From that time onwards he became the willing servant of his native city: or the Church of his youth ; and of the Congrepational denomination to which he belonged. He gave freely both of his time and means to the cause of Foreign Missions, working vigorously on the Board of the London Missionary Society, accompanying Dr. Wardlaw Thompson on a lengthened visit to the Mission churches in the Southern Seas. From its foundation to the present time, Mr. Crosfield was a delighted worker in the P.S.A. for men at Gt. George- street Church, making hiayself the personal friend and helper of large numbers of its adherents.• He was" in" nearly every Christian and philanthropic work in Liverpool. It was not the love or prominence, or of influence or of standing well with people which inspired him. He had not a grab) of that in his whole composi- tion. He was a Christian man, and his conceptien of a Christian teen was that he should follow his Master in seeking to be the servant of men. Of recent. months by serious failure in health, he had to stand aside and to retire for a season from his loved employ. But just latterly in the belief that his health' was being fairly re-established, he again enlisted with delight and zeal in manifold endeavours. It was one of the prized privileges of his (the speaker's) life to be included in his personal friendship. They were con- temporaneous in age. Hence his sudden departure had its own pathetic and powerful appeal to him. It seemed to Say "Be ye also ready, for in an hour when ye think not,the Son of Men corueth ! " William Crosfield did not think last Monday mornik ,that before the sun should set that day his spirit would have winged its flight ! ! And yet probably if ha could have chosen the hour or even the scene of his departure, he would hardly ' have chosen much other, than those which he realised. He died, where for years he had lived, at the post of Christian and Humane Service—uttering as his latest, words, wise counsel concerning the better doing of philanthropic work among the poor,thesufferine and the sad. William Crosfield "walked with God ;" of that he was confident. He was not without his defects, but the greatest of them were defects of manner rather than failures in character. He was apt to be brusque in demeanour; displaying frequently more strength in act than smoothness in word. He was one of the most manly Christians the preacher ever knew, and beneath his sometimes rugged manner there ever beat a kind and loyal Christian heart !</p>
        <p>On the proposition of the Rev. .TorrN YONGE the following resolution was passed: The congregation assembled in Wycliffe Congregational Church, War- rington, on Sunday evening May 23rd, 1009, desires to convey to Mrs. William Crosfield and Miss Crosfield the expression of its deep sympathy in the sudden and heavy bereavement which they have been called upon to sustain. They are specially moved to do so by the fact that Mr. Crosfield, for a large number of years had been one of the trustees of this Church and on various occasions had shewn his interest both in the Church and. in its Pastor. Their prayer is that "the God of all comfort" will richly and strongly console those to whom this great loss brings the nearest and deepest sorrow by the ever-growing revelation of His infinite grace and love. They would also hereby convey a message of most cordial sympathy to the church at Great George-street, Liverpool, with its pastor and deacons, from whose membership and service so beloved and honoured a leader has now passed to his heavenly rest and reward. May both the home and the church receive from above the instaining and consol.ng grace that shall be "Divinely equal to this great visita- tion of sorrow!"</p>
        <p>WHITE CITY, MANCHESTER. The popular fancy was struck hard and has responded most liberally to the building of the great new Pavilion Skating Rink by Mr. J. Calvin Brown Pro- prietor of Manchestf r's favourite amusement resort the White City. In the first ten days that the place was open for the present season more than 1900 skaters had availed themselves of the splendid maple floor and the numerous advantages of skating at the White City. he big seaside resorts have more shows etc. than W ite City, perhaps, hut many are only duplicates of others and the munber of distinct places of entertainment does not equal White City where there are no dupli- cates. " Fretiting the Flames " the huge spectacle has in the week it has been before the public made an impression that will carry it through the season as a veritable triumph of realism never before seen or even attempted in an outdoor show. It simply makes the people gasp in their delighted surprise at its many features of excitement and thrilling moments. This is an entertainment that aside from its merits as a show should be seen by all who ever expect to he of service in case of fire, for it teaches simply and forcibly the very latest and best methods of fire fighting and life saving under the most adverse conditions. It is an object of value that should be taken by every man who can see it. The musical feature for Whit Week will be the famous and favourite Black Watch Band with their wonderful Pipers and •if Mr. Broen had taken a plebescite of the people of the district it is altogether probable that this Niould have been the band to be se:ected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EMPIRE DAY.</head>
        <p>EMPIRE DAY.</p>
        <p>PRETTY SPECTACLE IN THE PARK.</p>
        <p>The bright sunshine of an ideal spring day gave a brilliant setting to Warrington's Empire Day celebra- tions which were this year on a more elaborate scale than on previous anniversaries. The spectacle witnessed in the Park on Monday morning was one long to be remembered. Shortly after ten o'clock school children were arriving from all directions. They carried themselves with conscious pride—for were they not to be the central figures in an impres- sive picture ?—and marched with almost soldier-like precision. By eleven o'clock fully 0,000 of the senior scholars of the town were marshalled on the Recreation Ground. The grouping was admirable,and such excel- lent arrangements had been made hy the Director of Education and the teachers that not the slightest con- fusion occurred. On a platform in the centre. of the field Messrs. Walker's Brewery Band discoursed lively selections while the scholars were assembling, and later accompanied the singing. A hearty cheer heralded the arrival of the Mayor and Mayoress kAlderman and Mrs. ForshaW), Alderman Smethurst (duty-Mayor) and Mrs. Smethurst, Councillor J. and Fulton-Smith (chairman of the Education Committee), who took their places on another platform facing the multitude of children. There was a large gathering of clergy and ministers of various denominations, mem- bers and officials of the Town. Council and Education Committee—in fact the assembly was one of the most representative we have seen in Warrington. Alderman</p>
        <p>spend a few millions on ships ; in this case penny wise would he pound foolish indeed. The Rev. W. BRACECAMP, preaching •at St. Paul's Church, considered it was a great disgrace to a town like Warrington, that they should still be 200 men behind, with regard to the strength of the Territorial Army. What was the reason ?—simply that the young men of to-day did not realize their outy to their King and country as they ought to do. He thought the cure was compulsory military training. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK at Cairo-street Chapel in the morning, said he wanted the Empire Day, movement to be Catholicised, universalised, so as not to breed little Jingoists but great Englishmen and English women. England was the imperial nation among the nations. Her common notion of liberty law ; govern- ment and order were those in accord with the vastest issues of human progress. These placed her as the premier nation, but not that she might " boss " but teach the nations how to rule. To one like himself who believed in the imperialness and imperiousness of his nation the German scare and the panic made him ask " Are our people losing their self-possession ? Is the old spirit of Drake self-restaint, stesdinebs of temper, command of self gone ?" Let them give no rein to racial hatreds but in the name of humanity bind all nations with hoops of steel to their hearts as brothers.</p>
        <p>In the evening, the rev, gentleman referred first of all to the birth of Queen Victoria 90 years ago, to her death eight years ego, and the establishment of the Empire movement six years ago, giving also a short sketch of the meaning of the movement. He said he wanted the children to be taught to be English, which meant good will towards all men, the worthiest and best in character and conduct. So let the children salute the Union Jack, for it was a magnificent emblem. They should also remember that they were not merely English because they spoke the tongue but only as they fulfilled the requirements of being English. So they.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SCENE IN THE. PARK.</head>
        <p>THE SCENE IN THE. PARK.</p>
        <p>Bennett, who is, of course, a strong believer in " Empire Day " celebrations, was away in Lincoln, and telegraphed to the Mayor :—" Very sorry not able to get back till afternoon. My heart is with yen in your celebration. God save the Kinirand Empire." The chief item of the celebration was the singing • of t!..e patriotic hymn "Now pray we for our country. Standing high above the vast assembly, Mr. T. D. Amos, headmaster of the Hamilton-street school, con- ducted the singing, and the sound of so many voices united in the inspiring hymn was most impressive. Considering the difficulty of training 9,000 children to sing in good time and tune, the perfonnanc I was remarkably creditable. The words of the hymn, which. were particularly appropriate to the occasion, are :</p>
        <p>Now pray we for our country. That Blitain long may be The holy and the happy, And the gloriously free ! Who blesseth her is blessed, So peace be in her walls, 'And joy in all her palaces, Her cottages and halls Now pray we that the lustre Of great Victoria's reign, In splendour ever growing, Still a wider fame may gain ; We praise the God who gave us A life so long and pure, Whose sweet and gracious influence For ages shall endure. And pray we that King Edwar,l Our ruler lt,ng may be, And Britnin dear old Britain, Still he famed for liberty ! May Providence in mercy Him nippiest blessings bring, • While Britons sing, with heart and voice, Long live our gracious King ! Next came the prosentation of medals and brooches to the best boys and girls, selected for the honour by the votes of their fellow-scholars. The Mayor handed the medals to the , boys, and the Mayoress the brooches to the girls. The following is a list of the recipients : _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Name of child. School. Brooch. Medal. Sacred Heart Oladys Morrelt John Mu!hare __ . . ___. . _ _</p>
        <p>'l'rinity Smith-street</p>
        <p>Eliztieth Jones Clarence Berry Eliza Johnson</p>
        <p>St. Barnabas' Norah Parkin John Broadhurst St„ James' Annie Foulkes Fred Slater</p>
        <p>Fairfield</p>
        <p>(Upper Girls') Mary Morton Fairfield</p>
        <p>(Girls') Amy Bleasdale Wycliffe Lottie Holland Arthur Rogers St. Mary's Agues O'Keefe John Flanaghon Parochial William Bate Heathside Frances Tetry Reginald Taylor Arpley-street Mabel Timperley Arnold Rutter Beamont Bessie Norcott jobn Andrews</p>
        <p>Hamilton-se Marian Sumner Harold Brownbill Silver-street Isabel Banner Edward Ashton</p>
        <p>St. Ann's (Sen.) Elsie Boaers St. Ann's (Jun.) Ada Knight St. Benedict's Clara Jobnßon</p>
        <p>William Mugham James Bete William Burke</p>
        <p>St. Alban's Bolton</p>
        <p>Alargaret Holland William Harrison Mabel Huelin</p>
        <p>Christ Church - Ernest Geo. Wright Latchford R.C. Agues Dumbill John Clarke</p>
        <p>• The ceremony concluded, the children gave three ringing cheers for the Mayor and Mayoress. ' The MAYOR then addressed the assembly. He IN ished in the first place to extend to all the children a very .cprdial Welcome, and also to congratulate them upon ,having such a fine day. He could further congratulate 'them upon their clean and nice appearance. As they all knew, that was" Empire Day "—the anniversary of the birthday of the late Queen Victoria—which had been set apart to foster the spirit of patriotism and the sense of duty in the young. The institution was only new, but it was growing very rapidly indeed, and was being celebrated all over the British Empire. He hoped that the children would always honour their King and love their country, and that they would grow.up to he .good and useful citizens. (Applause.) I Councillor FULTON-SMITH said they were taking part in a celebration that was being observed wherever the "English language was spoken. The hymn they had just I heard, "Now pray we for our our country," was being 'sung in Canada, Australia, India; and, practically at the same time, the citizens of our Empire, living iteall quarters of the globe, were joining in united thanks to God for the great blessing He bad vouchsafed them ' and in praying fora continuance of his many mercies. He (the speaker) asked the children to attend carefully to the instruction of their teachers, and to remember that in the days to come they would be the men and women who would govern the Empire, and endeavour I'to represent and perpettmte all that was good and noble in the history of Old England. He asked them to keep I themselves fit both in mind and body to uphold our glorious Empire, and concluded by wishing them all a happy and enjoyable day and "God speed." (Cheers). Then the huge, gathering joined in singing the National Anthem with great enthusiasm, and cheers having been given for the King, the children marched back to their respective schools, where they were dis- missed for the day. TELEGRAM TO THE KING.</p>
        <p>The following is a cnpy of a telegam which was sent by the Mayor to the King To His Majesty the King, Buckingham Palace, London. Thirteen thousand Warrington children celebrating Empire Day send you loyal and hearty greetings. TORSHAW, Mayor of Warrington.</p>
        <p>had a big thing on hand to teach their children how to be English, to cultivate a world-sense as against a national-sense, to help them rejoice in the progress of all the nations of the world, as well as in the progress of their own nation, and to teach all, not merely them- selves. and to get the most of the best for all. GREAT SANKEY COUNCIL SCHOOLS. Empire Day was fittingly celebrated at these schools which had been gaily decorated with flags and hunting. Mr. W. Furness had offered this year a silver meal to the best boy and girl respectively, who had been elected by the children of • the upper classes. The election took place on Friday afternoon, and two of the Seventh Standard children, Albert Bonehill and Mabel Garnett, had been chosen. At 9.30 the scholars were arranged in the main room, and there were present among others the Rev. J. R. Jones, Miss Jones, Mr, W. Furness and Mrs. Fortress, file flag was duly saluted and the National Anthem sung. The prize winners were brought up to Mrs. FURNESS, who, with a few well chosen words of congratulation, pinned the prize to the breast of each amid a hearty round of applause. Ttie HEADMASTER presented to Mrs. Furness and Miss Jones bouquets of double white clematis in honour of the occasion. The "Empire song" and "Home, sweet Home," sting by the children, brought a pleasant visit to a close. SANKEY BRIDGES COUNCIL INFANTS' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At this school the celebrations were on an elaborate scale. The parents of the children were invited to be present and about 100 were present. The children were suitably dressed to represent their various parts, viz: Britannia, England, Ireland, Scot. land, Wales, and various Colonies. The songs given were" Land of our birth," march and song," Little soldiers," " Britannla the pride of the ocean," and " Rule Britannia. There was also an Empire Day Pageant, representation of EnglAnd, Ireland, Scotland, and Wales, and formation of the Union Jack. The pro- ceedings concluded with the singing of the National Anthem. Great credit is due to Miss Pugh and the teaching,staff of the school for the excellent, training the scholars had reneived. P.ADGATM C.E. SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon the children attending this school were taken to the Vicarige lawn, by kind per- mission of the Rev. G. W. Dickinson. Here, amid the beautiful surroundings, the flag was saluted and songs were sung. The infants, tinder the direction of Mrs. Harter sang " Now pray we for our country," " A flag song," Three cheers for the red, white and bloc," and " Long live the King," while the senior scholars sang Kipling's "Recessional,', and a squai of senior scholars gavean exhibition of physical exercises, under the superintendence of Miss Walker. The singing of "God save the King" and hearty, cheers tor the King and the Vicar concluded the proceedings. - - SCHOOL CHILDREN'S ATHLETIC FESTIVAL. In the afternoon most of the children wended their way to the Wilderspool Football Ground where the third annual children's athletic festival promoted by the Warrington and District Teachers' Association was held. There would be quite 6,000 spectators present and the sports proved a grAtifying success in every respect. The committee desire to especially thank the following gentlemen for acting es judges :—Alderman Tinnion and Messrs. H. Ashton, J. F. Foster, A. S. Elliott, T. Locker,J. Wore Murray, T. Pemberton, and R. 13. Roberts. Mr, A. Kilburn was an indefatigable secretary, while Mr. I. Bowen the president, rendered invaluable services. Picturesque features of the festival were the Old English and Maypole dances which wore cleverly performed by the girls from Wycliffe and St. Ann's Schools, under the direction of the Misses Orton and Rowland. The Cottage Homes Bands played some capital selections. At the conclusion of the sports, the prizes were pre- sented to the successful competitors by the Mayoress (Mrs. Forshas). 'dr. I. BOWEN said the Mayor was unfortunately unable to be present, but they had the Mayoress with them, and be had much pleasure in asking bet to present the prizes. (Applause). Before the prizes were presented, Mr. J. Mooug MURRAY (director of education) said the Iklayor had received the following message from Earl Meath : " England, with all thy faults, I love thee still." (Applause)., The MAYORESS then presented the prizes in a most plesisiug manner. Mr. BOWEN said that as president of the Festival Committee, be had much pleasure in proposing a vote of thanks to the Mayoress for so kindly presenting the prizes._ . .</p>
        <p>- Mr. KILEURNE (secretary) seconded, and the motion was. heartily carried.</p>
        <p>Three chweera were then given for the Mayor and Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PULPIT REFERENCES.</head>
        <p>PULPIT REFERENCES.</p>
        <p>Empire Day was alluded to by the preachers at several local churches on Sunday. The Rev. T. W. Barber at the Thirish Church, advocated the establish- ment of international courts of arbitration. The demand for this international security became more and more urgent, for modern progress was binding together millions of most diverse interests, and because the aspiration of the mas.es after happiness was rising to an immense and irresistible flood. "The Parliament of man, the Federation of the world," was as indispensable to the citizen as air to the lungs. The Rev. B. PERCIVAL at St. Peter's Church, said he did not wish to be in any pessimistic, but there could be no doubt that preparations were being made to contest our position in the world, and it behoved us to he ready to defend it. We had been strongly warned by men of knowledge and experience, and if we did not , take those danger signals to heart, serious consequences ' migitt follow. He would have every young man join the Territorials, in order that he might be in a position defend h-rarth and finale. He would not hesitate to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. -- " Her Marriage Vow."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. -- " Her Marriage Vow."</p>
        <p>Th:s sensatioral domestic drama has proved a good attraction at the Royal Court Theatre this week. It is smartly IN litten, with plenty of tbrills and touchcs of pathos and humour, while the scenery and stage effects are a special feature. The most ingeniously contrived and realistically engineered sensation is that in which two trains pass each other at a good speed at the Highhead Railway Crowing. The play is performed by a capable company and interest never flags. Mr. J. Forbes Knowles achieveshigh honours as the hero, Ned Benton, and Miss Lilian Herries as Kate Walters, is a chatming heroine and acts with wonderful power and feeling. Mr. Vincent Wardle gives an exceptionally praiseworthy study qf Daffy Dan and Mr. Wilford Bailey is highly amusing as Eben Chubb, the portly merchant, who is always anxious to be " ing home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week we are to have another visit from Mr. Frank Lindo, the well-known act6r-author, who ni11• present an excellent company in his new romantic play " Love, the conquerer." The play deals with the stirring times of William of Orange, and Ur. Lindo will take the part. of Brian Norval, an outlaw and highwayman, known as "The Prince." Miss Marion Wakeford, a charming and talented actress/ will impersonate Misstress Beatrice Mortimer. New and magnificent scenery has been painted for the produc- tion and there will be elabor ate dresses and unifmms. SPECIAL MATINEE—MISS MABEL LOVE IN</p>
        <p>of that charming actress, Miss Mabel Love, ATII°9S .acbieveinents are so well-known and appreciated. OA the occasion of her visit on Thursday, she will apPear in the title role of !` Lady Frederick," the successful comedy by Mr. W. Somerset Maugham, and which will be presented by Miss Emma and Mr. PercY Hutthison's principal London company. The Pro,l- - has had a splendid reception in London artu the provinces, and is, by the way, the only play ever produced at five West End theatres.</p>
