      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>" LADY FREDERICK."</head>
        <p>" LADY FREDERICK."</p>
        <p>Patrons of the Royal Court Theatre have a special treat in store for them next week in the matinee visit</p>
        <p>MISS MABEL LOVE.</p>
        <p>AIR. IL MINSTER.</p>
        <p>Miss Mabel Love will be supported by Mr. Robert Minster, an actor who has already made his name ill many and varied leading parts, creating strong impreg" sions in "Old Heildelberg," "Robin Hood," "The Truth," &amp;c. Another important feature in the pro- duction will be beautiful gowns worn by Miss Mabel Love.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE. A CREDITABLE DISPLAY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE. A CREDITABLE DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>While there was little of the elaborate or picturesque about it, Warrington's eighth annual horse parade which took place on Saturday was the most creditabl? local display of the kind ever seen. As the long line (i'L sleek, well-groomed horses passed along the streets, It was evident to the moat casual observer that the pare is fully achieving its worthy object, which is to induce drivers to take a humane interest in the animals under their care, and to encourage the use of horses suitable and fit for heavy work in the streets. The entries were well up to the good standard of previous 3.eol'st but the most gratifying fact was that tbe quality showed a distinct advance. The cornr raittee had made several new departures Jur the benefit of the small tradesmen and workingmen competitors who hitherto have been handicapped by having to meet large owners with tine teams of horses In order that every one should have an equal chance, the committee took the bold course of abolishing the s3stem of classes and every man whom the judges eon' sidered worthy of a prize received one. The result vrT3 that 32 first prizes, 30 seconds and 17 thirds ;'ere distri- buted and this was especially satisfactory in view of the fact that last year only 14 first prizes %ere giveFr No silver cups were offered on this occasion, and flys left more funds at the disposal of the committee with which to enlarge the prize list. The Mayor (Aldermen - Forsbaw) had kindly granted permission for the judgiug_, to take place in the drives leading to from the Town Ball, and there was a consensus el opinion that this central avenue was much inure, convenient than Arpley Fields. The arrangements 1.101. been most carefully worked out, each section having to muster in a specified street and then proceed in front of the Town Ball. Here the judging was carried out by the following :—The Hon. A. E. Parker, of Norton Curlieu, Warwick ; Captain E. W Griffith,. of Tiresford, Tarporley ; Mr. C. W. Wilson, of Rigmadem Park, Kirby Lonsdale ; and Mr. I‘. Fleming, of North Audley-street, London, W. The • judges carried out their duties in a most painstaking manner, and pave many useful hints to the driver Among those who witnessed the judging were Sir Gilbert Greenall. the President of the committee, Lad?' Oreenall and Mrs. Wynne Griffith; the Mayor ana Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Forshaw), and the Misses ' Foishaw, the Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), Mrs. and the Misses Smethurst, Mr. .W. Peter Hyland. sr Mr. Henry Greenall (who was accompanied by M's Greenall), Councillor J. Fulton-Smith, Mr. W. Fletcher, Mr. E. Draper and Mr. J, M. Greenhalgh. rice-presr dents; Mr. W. Bainbridge, and Mr. George Venn,- mem- bers of the committee ; Mr. A. H. Clive (hon. secre- tary), Councillor Birtles, Councillor Evans, Mr. Sankey, Mr. Mr. T. Raver, the Town Clerk and the Chief Constable. Mr. Robert Pierpoint %yak unable to be present owing to indisposition.</p>
        <p>As the drivers passed-on to take up their position in Crosfield-street, they received their awards at tho bands of the Mayoress. Fortunately. the parade was favoured with glorious weatl)er and thousands of spectators lined the route- The horses were decked with the gay trappings which are always associated with May parades and additional interest was given to the procession by die inclusion of the fire brigades of the borough, Messrs. Greens!), Whitley and Co. and Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons. 'I he engines with their artistic decorations looked spick and spin and praise is due to the brigades for assisting this way. The Borough and Pioneer Bands discoursed martial airs. The procession, marshalled unler the direction of Mr. T. H. Pratt y, left Crosfield-street about two o'clock and proceeded by way of Froghall-lane, Tanners-lane, Winwick-street, Horsemark et-street, Bridge-street, Mersey-street, and Buttermarket-street. dispersing at Market-gate. A band of collectors reaped a good hhrvest on behalf of the Infirmary and District Nursing Association and there were also several lurries for the reception of coins.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon, the President, Officers, and Judges,,psrtook of luncheon at the Lion Hotel. On the proposition of Sir GILBPAT GRERNALL. the Mayor was heartily thanked for the use of the Town Hull, and in reply his WORSHIP paid if be were Mayer again he would have great pleasure in once more plac- ing it at the disposal of the committee. Councillor 3. Fur,ToN SMITH said they had been aim- ing for years to improve the condition of the horses generally and the treatment of them by the drivers, and they had been appealing more to th° drivers than to the owners. Up to this year the owners carried away the trophies, but now the committee bad dispensed with cups and devoted the contributions ag much as possible to the men, so as to encourage them to treat the animals under their Charge all the year round as they ought to be treated. It was very grati- fying that the tradesmen whose business only required ! the service of one horse had come forward with their entries better than they had done for many years Oast. ' It was something to be proud of that the Society were getting at those who were most deserving. It had been remarked that the general appearance of the horses in the town had, during the past few years, shown a" remarkable improvement, not only on the day of Oa parade, but throughout the year. This showed that the lessons taught by the horse parade year after year wer° rpermeating and influencing the drivers. douncillor Fulton-Smith concluded by voicing the indebtedness of the committee to the Mayor and Mayoress and judge He was quite sure the drivers would benefit by 00 ecoarananciasions of Oil Pid UPS. (Applause.) THE PRIZE LIST. Light Horse Turnouts.—First Prizes: J. Davlss (Messrs. Milling and Co.), John Dykes (Mr. E. Roberts), Herbert Dickinson (Mr. J. Babbitt), J. Burrows (%l S. N. Green), J. Parsonage (Mr. j. Cox), J. (Mr. Peter Leigh), and S. Johnson (Mr. Charles Starkey). Second prizes : William Sudlory (Mr. Cunningham), William William Percival (Mr. Raddfirth, Latch- ford Laundry), John Stockton (Mrs. Stockton), spa Fred Taylor Mr. Josh Taylor). Third prizes : Betties (Mr. Redderth, Latchford Laundry). Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.'s Turnouts.—l irs prizes John Harmer, G. James and B. SingleY; Quornby and Gillespie, and J. W.Hankey, Secona prize : C. Holmes and W. Harmer. Third prize : Harmer and J. Arnold, and A. Arnold and Sam Sant., Slate Company's Turnouts.—First prize : Thomason and G. Thomason, Thomas Cook, Thoisn' Hardy, and Robert Grace. Second prizes : JaMe° Wells, S. Stokes, and Edward Deck. Corporation's Turnouts.—First prizes: Alfred Ashteur Joseph Miller, John Hinds, Charles Hankinson, Holder and William Taylor. Second prizes: Joselw Taylor and James Newton. Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons' Turnoute.—Flls,t prizes: S. Mold and W. Palmer, F. Buckley and J,; !Scriaren, T. Harmer and T. Whitfield, M. Gilfedder H. Mason. Second prizes : R. Brockleburst and Dean, J. Condlitie and E. Darby, A. Houghton and a. Smith, J. Lee, W. Williams, T. Dutton and W. Johns°°' Third prizes: R. Bickers and J. Teat. Heavy Miscellaneous Turnouts.—First prizes: JesePt. Monks (Mr. George Howard), Peter Moss (Mr. Percf'i. Cartwright), W. Brat e (Mr. John Chorley), Leslie (Mr. W. A. Leslie), Charles Wilkinso (Co-operative Society), and Sam Harmer (Niessrsni W. and J. Richardson). Second prizes: Bate (Messrs. Pierpoint and Bryant). William Coll 2:;. (Messrs. Mortimer and Co.), Thomas Spender Pit_ Thomas Bleasdale), William Cartwright (Mr. E. 1P wood), Luke Skelland (Mr. E. Isherwood), Barlow (Mr. E. Isherwood), Arthur Davenport (AlesLa Crompton end Shawcross), T. Highana (Co-operat'T Society), J. Fogg (Co-operative Society), Ridgsr% (Messrs. W. and J. Richardson), W Co.)'. Coulson (Mr. Pa. Cooper), William Harrop (Messrs. Buckley and William Whiting (Messrs. Buckley and Co.) endb,4t, Jones (Messrs. Buckley and Co.) Third prizes: 110.,;a5 Barber ('Jr. Edwin Hands), Alan Preston (Mr. , Preston), .T. Mather (Messrs. Mortimer and Co.),WilliV. Broadhurst (Mr. E. Isherwood), J. Titchard and_s_ Arnold (Co-operative Society), J. Kenwright (Co-OP"'it, tive Society), J. Roberts (Co-operative SocietyVve Nelson (Co-operative Society), S. Allen (Co-onera‘W. Society), William Boles (Mr. William Boles), Sbuffiebotham (Messrs. Bucliley and Co.), H. Clll34°P (Messrs. Buckley and Co.)</p>
