      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>TOLSTOI'S LATEST.</head>
        <p>TOLSTOI'S LATEST.</p>
        <p>Count Leo Tolstoi has placed the Russian Government in an embarrassing position. Several persons have been prosecuted for circulating his tract "Thou Shalt Not Kill," and Tolstoi has come forward and demanded that he be put on trial for writing the tract. The authorities, it appears, will have no option but to try him, and this may lead to his deportation to Siberia for lese-maiestie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HELPLESS FOR 13 YEARS.</head>
        <p>HELPLESS FOR 13 YEARS.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Restores Use of Arm and Cures Abscesses and Children's Eczem3</p>
        <p>Mr. William R. Pickles, a master carter, of 31, Wilbraham Place, Scotland Road, Liverpool, who is well-known about the local markets, gave A Liverpool reporter a graphic account of wonderful benefits. obtained from Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>"Thirteen years-ago I was working as a ship's rigger," said Mr. Pickles to the Pressman, " on the Mississippi, at New Orleans, Louisiana, U.S.A. Went to bed one night with a slight pain in the upper part of my right arm. When I awoke next morning my arm was swollen to an enormous size. I went to the City Hospital, but after three operations I was discharged, .as incurable. The British Consul at Orleans then kindly assisted me to get back to Liverpool. • • ".1 had now lost all the. use of my arm. • What Was worse, -abscesses were continually forming on the arm causing me terrible pain. I went first to the Liver- pool Northern Hospital, and _then into • the Leeds Infirmly in December, 1897. I underwent further operations, but after I had been nine months in this infirmary I was told that my arm would have to come off. Abscesses continued to form, and nothing seemed able to heal them or give me ease from the terrible pain. " As Zam Buk had cured me of pilss I began to think that there must be some extraordinary healing power in Zam-Buk, so I decided to give this rare balm a chance on my poor old. arm. From the trial appli- cation I got much relief. The swelling gradually subsided and finally apiece of bone, about an inch long, forced its way through the skin. The abscess then rapidly disappeared. Zazn-Buk healed up the lacerated flesh perfectly, and I have now got back the use of my arm, after -1.1 years' absolute helplessness. " My four children's faces were terribly disfigured by eczema. We tried Zam-Buk again, and all four children were soon completely cured. Is it any wonder that we are so enthusiastic abotlt Zam-Buk?"</p>
        <p>The town-c'erkship of Hereford, vacant by the death of Mr. Joseph Carlessovho held the. office for forty years, has been filled by the appointment of Mr. Arthur bit, aged thirty-four, deputy-town clerk of Blackburn. There were sixty-nine candidates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. &gt; )-Ne,'%^•NiwoVe‘n.-,Neiv-^k.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. &gt; )-Ne,'%^•NiwoVe‘n.-,Neiv-^k.</p>
        <p>THE OLD CORN MARKET.</p>
        <p>A piece of old Warrington which has entirely passed from view is the east side of the Corn Market. The space now known as the Market Place formerly bore that name,and a century and a half ago, before the Market Hall or Fish Sheds had arisen, was a picturesque square. Only one of the timbered buildings—the "Barley Mow"—remains and it is left to the imagination of the Warring- tonian to conjure up the Square in which his ancestors did their business. We cannot but lament the disfigurement of the site and the disappearance of the old buildings, and would gladly give the massive Beef Market andl Fish Shed to have back again the old Court House with its curfew bell and the rows of guaint shops—a fitting centre for an old town, and the Scene of many historic incidents. Of the north and east sides pictures have been preserved, and these will serve as a basis of a brief description. On the north side stood the "Barley Mow," the " Legh Arms "and the" Three Crowns" abreast,for Market-street is a modern thoroughfare and the two last named public-houses were demolished to make way for it. The illustration we are able to give is copied from a painting made by Mr. Edward Gaskell in 1803, and now in the possession of Mr. Vere Davies. Of the seven buildings_ four were public-houses. Starting from the left side of the picture we have first the</p>
        <p>ewe-necked white horse, bearing a suspicious resemblance to the " blue " variety. Few people know that an Esquimaux woman of the name of Arrownix died about 70 years ago in one of the shows in Warrington. I have seen her gravestone on the side of the old churchyard near Church-place." This reference to the visiting Esquimaux will serve tp recall an amusing and true story. A sallow black-haired Warrington woman married an Esquimaux man who was visiting the Fair, and travelled through the country for years afterwards, posing as a true native of the Polar regions. On one occasion the • show was at Preston Guild. The father of Mr. Vavies was there!and to escape from a shower took refuge in the Esquimaux Show. The woman recognized him, and after the performance came to him with a terror-stricken face to say "-For God's sake don't betray Us, for we are doing well, and are very happy." Her appearance seemed to confirm the sta,ments, and Mr. Davies promised to keep the secret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>COBDEN AND BRIGHT.</head>
        <p>COBDEN AND BRIGHT.</p>
        <p>• During the eaPly days of Corn Law agita- tion Richard Cobden and John Bright held a meeting in the Square and spoke from a cart. Mr. Beamont and Mr. Robert Davies were in what was then the Warrington library—now the office of Robert Davies and Co.—and heard the speeches. Mr. Davies declared that scarcely fifty people were present. " But," he added, Things wnre different afterwards. Our old townsman, Mr. Edward West, in his broad-brimmed hat and straight coat collar, introduced Cobden, but the sub- ject of the Corn Laws was at that time far in advance, of public opinion."</p>
        <p>CARICATURE EXTRAORDINARY.</p>
        <p>We have space for one other extract</p>
