      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Edwd. Gaskell, Omit, 1803. VIEW OF THE EASTERN SIDE OF THE CORN-MARKET.</head>
        <p>Edwd. Gaskell, Omit, 1803. VIEW OF THE EASTERN SIDE OF THE CORN-MARKET.</p>
        <p>"Three Legs of Man" which afterwards became the "White Hart." At the time of its demolition it was kept by a Mr. Hindley, who combined the business of butcher with that of publican, and sold to the British public the two articles which were then supposed to be the secret of the nation's greatness—" Beer and Beef." Adjoining stood, the " Unicorn," and next were two dwelling- houses and shop, one of which was occupied by a clogger, and the other by a greengrocer. The building over which the name of "W. •Simcock, Stay Warehouse," is inscribed, was formerly the home of John Gandy, a leather cutter, and sheltered John Wesley on more than one occasion. Next stood the " Globe " Inn, Xnd the timbered house shown on the right was the " Brown Cow." On the South side of the Market Place were streets by which it was approached from Sankey-street, but there was a mid- feather of old brick and timbered houses. The one facing the square was kept by a Mrs. Gandy as a wool shop and stood on the spot now occupied by the steps leading from the shed to the Beef Market. Unfortunately no picture of this side of the Corn Market appears to exist. Near by were the fish stones on which fish was displayed, and a row of venerable warehouses. Inns and shops filled up the west side. A short time before his death, Mr. Robert Davies furnished us with some notes and recollections relating to the Corn Market and we think our readers will find them singularly interesting.</p>
        <p>describing one of the most curious of the amusements of our ancestors. "Perhaps the strangest sight I ever witnessed in the Market-place was a performance called "riding stang." When a man and his wife fell out to the annoyance of their neighbours, and the wife had beaten the husband, it was the custom to obtain a small cart, in which two men, one in a man's night shirt and night cap, and the other in a similar gear, but of a feminine character, went through the quarrel opposite the house of the offenders first, and then rehearsed it in various quarters of the town. There were plenty of willing hands harnessed by ropes to the cart, and, as may be supposed, the most frequent halts were at public-house doors. On the particular occasion now referred to, a besom had been used by the wife, so in the performance, after a violent altercation, the pseudo woman banged the man about the shoulders, until the head of the besom flew off, and hit some head or face in the grinning crowd. Your readers will ask how such things could have been, but they must 'recollect that four parish constables, harmless tradesmen every one of them, and one paid deputy, then kept a population of 18,000 in such order as satis- fied all of us at the time, a fact which speaks well, I think, for the Anglo-Saxon gift of self-government. The same fact also raises other questions and invites comparisons of an interesting nature, the discussion of which I will leave to the local economist."</p>
        <p>A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>FOOD PRICES SEVENTY YEARS AGO,</head>
        <p>FOOD PRICES SEVENTY YEARS AGO,</p>
        <p>"My reminiscences of the Corn Market begin from my earliest childhood, when I used as a treat to go to market with an old servant to buy the household supplies. Butter in those days was never sold in shops —it was either bought in the market, or was hawked round by the farmers' wives to the buyers' houses—and I well remember the price, which was tenpence a pound in summer and 'a shilling in winter, each pound consist- ing of eighteen ounces. Foils were 3s. 6d. a couple, and rabbits is. 6d. and Is. i3d. On the other hand, brown sugar was 7d. to 9d. a lb., loaf sugar 10d. to is., mould candles is., and dips sd. Tea was 65., and coffee 3s. and 3s. 6d. per lb. We also bought butchers' meat on our expedition—best-beef 7d. a lb., mutton 6d.' and pork sd. Bread was any price, according to the harvest. The centre of the Market-place was occupied by a very tall lamp post, surrounded by iron steps, on which, as I have before said, the lazzaroni of the town were in the habit of basking in the sun. On a market day the whole of the paved, uncovered square was hidden by earthenware on straw (over which I once saw an overdriven bull charge, clearing all over before him). hampers of vegetables, and the like, and round the sides of the square country people stood with their baskets. Whatever the weather, hail, snow, frost, or rain, they had to brave it out."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WARRINGTON FAIR.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FAIR.</p>
        <p>"At Fair times, 18th July and 30th November, shows were allowed to occupy quite half the square. I once recollect 17 vans there, nearly every bile provided with a drum, a speaking trumpet, and a pair of cymbals, so that the din was incessant at night. An African village on the rampage could not have beaten it. The 14rgest show was one of strolling players, who used to strut about outside in their tawdry finery before the performance began. "The Murder of Maria in the Red Barn" was the standing dish for some years. I once witnessed it, saw the victim shot whilst on her knees she prayed for her life, her blood flowing in the shape of 'a .red raddle patch on her temple, and saw her ghost disclose her burial place under the floor of the barn to her mother; but only once, for as the actors lived pro tern in the booths, or, at all events, cooked there, the atmosphere was by no means that of the spicy shores of Araby the blest, as sung by Milton."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>THRILLS AND SENSATIONS.</head>
        <p>THRILLS AND SENSATIONS.</p>
        <p>"The illumination- of the shows outside was tow, frizzling, as it were, in oil, in a pair of grocer's scales, lighting up the canvas scenes outside, on which were depicted giants and giantesses about 14 feet high, boa constrictors sbout a hundred feet long and, so on. Cruel disappointments as you dis- covered on entry, like the fatal upas tree, the simoom of the , desert, Robinson Crusoe and his island, and other popular myths of childhood, all killed by the frigid touch of reality. There was a " blue " horse led, sheeted, up through the crowd at every fair to be shown in a booth, but I occasionally met the boards of the said booth travelling on the highroad in a cart drawno by an old</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OUR MISCELLANY.</head>
        <p>OUR MISCELLANY.</p>
        <p>In an old copy of" Notes and Queries "we recently read the following- under the title "The Scales of Justice 'no longer a fable —" On Monday, December 4th, at the War- rington Borough Court, before the Mayor (Joseph Davies, Esq.), H. Bleckly, Esq., and C. Broadbent, Esq., Patrick Flanagan was T Mayor charged with having had an unjust half- pound weight in his possession. he requested the Clerk to see how many quill pens would be required to balance the scales when the just and unjust weights had been placed at either end. They would fine the defendant is. for each pen. The test was made in the Court and the Clerk reported that nine quill pens were required. A fine of 9s. was imposed. We owe it to War- rington, which is a Lancashire town, to say that all the three justices above named are Cheshire men."</p>
        <p>FIFTY YEARS AGO. From a local print of June 1859. . "A public meeting, called in compliance i with a requisition to the Mayor, was held n the Music Hall, to take into consideration the expediency of forming a Rifle Volunteer Corps in Warrington and the neighbourhood. The room was well filled. The Mayor (T. Glazebrook Rylands, Esq.), presided, and the meeting was addressed by Mr. Clare, Mr. Beamont, Mr. Robert Davies, and the Rev. A. A. Bridgman in support of a resolution for the formation of a corps, which was opposed by an amendment proposed by Mr. Edmund Robinson, seconded by Dr. Smith, and supported by Mr. Robert Gaskell and Mr. West. The amendment was lost, and motions proposed and carried to the effect that subscriptions be forthwith entered into, to. assist in defraying any, expenses that might be incurred, that a Committee be formed to carry out the resolution, consist- ing of the memorialists and others, and that the meeting pledge itself to use every exertion to promote the formation of a Rifle Corps, and to induce Volunteers to come forward and enrol themselves. Although the opposition was out-talked and out-voted no unfair play or obstacle 'as put in the way of those willing to train or be trained as Volunteers. Neither were those endeavour- ing to raise a corps discouraged."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>• • • • The effort was successful and a local poet thus broke into loyal verse:</p>
        <p>I've purchased a rifle and taken an oath, To fight for my country and Queen; To charge French or German I'll never be both, I dread not the best man I've seen. In a country like °ilia, where liberty's flag No foeman has seen but unfurled; Must never be shown as a dirty old rag, The scorn and contempt of the world. We've homes to protect with the inmates they hold; -We've thousands of altars to loot; And ships by the million,and banks full of gold, Marauders would fain like to loot. Shall we prove traitors,or.skulkers of cowards? Forbid it ye powers of The sky ! We may not possess all the blood Of the Howards, i3ut we'll fight and we'll never say die!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
