      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MR. WILTA AM BARLOW.</head>
        <p>MR. WILTA AM BARLOW.</p>
        <p>My life has been a quiet and rather uneventful one, spent within the borders of the "good, old town." I was horn on April 17th, 1828,at Howley Quay,where my father Isaac Barlow, was employed by the Old Quay Company. Although in those days a great portion of the Manchester and Liver- pool traffic was dealt with at the Quay, not much pressure of business was experienced, and two vehicles sufficed for all the carting that was necessary. If two flats came to the Quay in one day it was considered an exceedingly busy time. ,When I was quite young we removed to a house in Church- street at the end of General-street and later my father opened a provision shop in the same street, in _premises formerly occupied by a Mr. Gerrard: Next door was an old thatched cottage—the home of my grand- mother, Nancy Barlow, who was one of the best-known inhabitants of the neighbour- hood. As an infant I attended a school kept by an old lady named Sallie Hall, on part of the site now occupied by Messrs. Rylands' works. A further instalment of my educa- tion was received at Richard Taylor's school in Church-street and later I went to a school kept by John Monks in the Seven Stars Yard, Bridge-street. At the age of ten I started work as an errand lad in the empldy of Mr. William Sanderson, grocer, Bridge- street.</p>
        <p>Warrington has undergone a striking transformation since the days of my youth. The main streets were all paved with big boulder stones and Buttermarket-street especially was in a dreadful state. I was attending school while the present Warring- ton Bridge was in course of construction. I can just recall its 'predecessor and well remember the temporary strgeture beingin use during the erection of the new bridge. Church-street was a very quaint thorough- fare of semi-rural appearance, and the were a number of old cottages and tire Church Sunday school where Rylands's works now stand. The favourite place of assembly was the big lamp which was of curious, old-fashioned design and had a large base. Here old cronies used to congregate and tell their tales. Butchers might be seen engaged in making skewers :and another familiar figure was the late Mr. Cross who would sit there finishing and polishing watch glasses, of which he manufactured large numbers. There was another of these big lamps in the old Market Place. Many of the houses in Church-street had large gardens attached and my father also had the use of a field and kept several cows. I remember driving them ur Church-street and down Howley-lane to this grazing land and as floods were frequent it was not always an easy matter to find a safe path. My father was a tenant of the late Colonel Blackburne and this bringsl to my mind an incident during one of the earliest. Parliamentary elec- tions. . Having erected a shippon, he obtained special permission from Colonel Blackburne's steward to make a door in A wall -so as to afford convenient access to it. During the election in question, one of the sub-agents to Colonel'Blackburne came round canvassing but, although in those days the voting was open, my father would not promise his sup- port to either candidate. The sub-agent retaliated by- giving instructions for the newii-provided door to be made up and workmen promptly appeared to carry out his orders. My father was at work when they began their task and when he came home for breakfast he unceremoniously kicked down all they had done. However, on his depar- ture the work was completed. Not to be out:done, my father complained to the steward who at once said it was unfair that any tenants should be interfered with on political grounds, and the door had to be replaced. It is difficult to realise that at that time there were beautiful gardens, with fine fruit, trees, stretching nearly half-way down Howley-lane. All that land has now been built upon. The Rectory was then entered from Howley-lane instead of from Church-street.</p>
        <p>I can recollect the rejoicings which took place in Warrington on the Coronation of Queen Victoria. There was a great trades procession. It is rather curious that the feature which impressed itself most upon my mind was that the craft-emblem displayed by the curriers took the form of a hide carried on poles. When I was a boy. the Church-street fair was a great event. It was held once a year about Whitsuntide and• there were some lively doings. Near the Ring-o-Bells, a greasy pole was erected, surmounted with a kettle, and he who was fortunate enough to climb to the top kept the kettle for a prize. Another spectacle which would be considered extraordinary in these days was that of a number of men seated in a cart,and smoking tobacco in teapots for a wager,in the presence of hundreds of spectators. All kinds of sports, such as wheelbarrow and sack races, were indulged in and men seemed to take a delight in making the youngsters enjoy themselves. The Howley wharf was then a great place of storage for cotton. There were eight cotton mills—Cockhedge, Allen's (Butter- market-street), Wharf Meadow, three on the Latchford side of the Bridge, one on Friars Green and one at Bank Quay. At the houses opposite the gas works the weaving of sacking and sail cloth—then a flourishing industry—was carried on by means of hand looms.</p>
        <p>I can remember when Bold-street as such did not exist. There were gardens and a wheel barrow road right down to what is now Palmyra-square and from that point fields stretched before us.</p>
        <p>To return to my own career. After being with Mr. Sanderson, of Bridge-street, for a considerable time I entered the employ of Messrs. Georg° and William Fell, grocers, Sankey-street, and remained there a number of years. In February 1850 I married Mary Hedgecock, daughter of the late Peter Hedgecock, and this year we celebrated the</p>
        <p>fifty-ninth anniversary of that happy event. I am thankful to say that we have both been blessed with good health and are still able to get about comfortably. My father-in-law used to keep a milk house in Warrington, but when he decided to devote all his attention to farming at Norton, I took the business over and carried it on in Mount- street, off Golborne-street. In a few years, however, I gave it up and returned to Messrs. Fell's. My father had been carrying on a small shop in Sankey-street, and I succeeded him there when he entered the corn trade. Ultimately I entered into partnership with him, our first premises being a warehouse over the old post office (now Mr. Isaac's shop). From thence we moved to a building in Cairo-street, since acquired by the Co-operative Societk. Thirty-four years ago we opened the present shop in Horsemarket-street, taking over the business formerly conducted by Mr. William Fairhurst. My father lived to be eighty- two, and died in the year 1887. Warrington in the old days used to have a fairly large ship-building industry, and I remember the time when boats were constructed at the Bridge Foundry and launched in the Mersey. The expansion of the business resulted in the opening of new works at Bank Quay, where several larger vessels were built. I was present at the launching of the ill-fated Tayieur, a noble ship which came to grief on its first voyage. There was a very high tide, and when the ship took the plunge it caused such a swell that many of the spectators were knee-deep in .water, and some had to be carried to a place of safety. -Warrington was not then a great brewery centre, for all the publicans brewed their own ale, and I used to go for the grains which were used for provender. A favourite excursion from Warrington was to sail along the Old Quay scanal to Runcorn and thence in steamers to Liverpool, and round what was called the "floating line." I have been at Blackbear Bridge at four o'clock in the morning and seen the Manchester packet, crowded with trippers, arrive en route for various pleasure resorts. There were not the same travelling facilities then, and their excursion used to take a night and a day. I have a vivid recollection of the old "nomination days" in connection with the Parliamentary elections. The hustings were erected in front of the old Bridewell and dur- ing the contest there were exciting scenes. The police force at that time numbered two men, but scores of special constables were sworn in. My father used to have a truncheon, bearing the Government stamp, hanging in his house for many years, show- ing that he was ready, if called upon, to fill the role of policeman. The stocks were then in use as a form of punishment for wrong- doers and I have seen men imprisoned in them near the old Bridewell and also near the old Music Hall. When I was very young many stage coaches used to pass through the town. The last coach that was run was a Warrington vehicle, which plied for years to Manchester and other towns and latterly made journeys to Cheshire places, like Lymm and Altrincham. As a boy I took a delight in hanging on the back of the coaches, but I did it once too often, for on one occasion I was enjoying myself-in that way when the unevenness of the ground near Orford-street, in Buttermarket-street, caused the coach to jolt suddenly and the end of my finger was trapped and taken off. In my childhood I attended the Parish Church for some time, but my father threw in his lot with the Wesleyans and for many years we attended the old Bank-street chapel and the Sunday school in Butter- market-street, my father being a teacher. On Sunday mornings he used to get me up at six o'clock in order to be present at the seven o'clock prayer meeting at Bank-street. After breakfast we went to Sunday School, followed by service in the chapel. I have been a worshipper at Bold-street church ever since it was opened.</p>
        <p>I have never taken an active part in public affairs but an organization in which I am keenly interested is the Order of Druids which I joined in 1847. On February 19th 1907 I was presented with a silver rose bowl on the attainment of lily diamond jubilee as a member of the Druids' Hope Lodge. Other cherished possessions of mine are an illuminated address and solid tea and coffee service which I received on February 2nd of this year to mark the completion of fifty years service as treasurer of the Widow and Orphans Fund of the Order of Druids (Warrington district).</p>
        <p>: 4 $</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>DAILY TRIUMPHS.</head>
        <p>DAILY TRIUMPHS.</p>
        <p>Day by day confidence increises. Success merits reward.</p>
        <p>Earlestowu people know a "good thing." They praise it when they find it. "I have much pleasure in recommending Daan's pills." says Mrs. A. Massey, 21, lisydock-street, Earles- town. "1 had been suffering for about eight months with backadhe, which was caught by my catching cold. The backache came on gradually, causing me consider- hie inconvenience, fosr the back, when 'ad, EeSMS to make the whole system wrong. I also had rheumatism in my ankles, sometimes being scarcely able to work. " Doah's pills, I ant glad t 9 say, have given me per- fect ease from the pains in my back, and relieved me very much of the. rhetirnatism. Soon after I began taking • the pills I noticed a difference in myself." The kidneys are called "the filters of life," because their duty is to filter .waste matters and poisons from the blood. If they are weak or diseased, and therefore unable to do their duty, the whole body suffers. You may feel sure that your kidneys are out oflorder if you suffer from any of these symptoms :—Backache, Pains in the Limbs of Joints, Watery Swellings round the ankles or under the eyes, Sleeplessness Nervousness, Headache or Urinary troubles, Doan's Pals act directly on the kidness ; they cure all kidney diseases, such as Rheumatism, Dropsy, Gravel, Neuralgia, and Lumbago. Keep your kidneys well and they will keep you well. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. You are sure to get the right medicine if sou ask distinctly for Doan's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL AND NORTH WALES.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from our advertising columns that the Liverpool and North Wales Steamship Company are announcing various excursions in connection with their sadings to and from North Wales. To-day (Saturday), their palatial steamer La Marguerite will take up the regular daily service, leaving Liverpool for Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor, and Menai Bridge. Extra railings are also announced by the St. Elvies, for Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor, and Menai Bridge. Passengers by th s steamer can avail themselves of an extended sea trip at cheap fares to Menia Bridge. On Whit Sunday, in addition to La Marguerite. the Snowdon leaves Llandudno at speoial cheap fares. On Whit Monday the company have three railings announced, the steamer, St. Tudno, for Llandudno, with connection for Colwyn Bay and Holyhead. The steamer St.Elvies, also leaves foi: Llandudno, Beaumaris, Bangor, Menai Bridge, and Carnarvon, allowing a long stay ashore at each place. The above sailings are in addition to the usual 10.45 a.m. sailing by La Marguerite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MAX- PEMBEZION</head>
        <p>MAX- PEMBEZION</p>
        <p>Author of "The Iron Pirate " "The</p>
        <p>hundred Days," "A Puritan's Wife,"</p>
        <p>etc., etc.</p>
