      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CHAPTER XXX VI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXX VI.</p>
        <p>[Paddy O'Connell writes a brief letter from Jack Straw's Castle at Hainpetead.] Jack Straw's Castle, Hampstead. October 27, 1905.</p>
        <p>Dear Harry,—l've no mind to be writing letters on a Friday, but as we can't blot that same day out of the week, anyway, and there's .good luck to come in this world as well as bad, here goes for a trial of it. I am still fixed upon this wild heath, tlnnigh God knows why. You tell me *neither to come • nor to go, so here _I am for the</p>
        <p>middle course, a; 'the car-driver said when he put me into the canal for fear of spoiling the banks with his wheels. Little Martha I aee every day, and we've had more than one lunch and flintier at the foreign cafés down West, 641 the off-chance that we might do some good to you—though this. is not a mat- ter that should be naffed to her preaching man of a huosband. What a poor thing he is, to be sure—preaching on a text of St. Paul about marriage directly her back is turned, and giving it out to the fleck that celibacy is the blessed state! She'll give him celi- bacy when she gets home ! Faith, I'd like to be there when his cars are boxed.</p>
        <p>Your letter speaks of no good spirit, my boy, and I'm not wondering at it. But you'll be good enough to believe this—that if any harm had happened to Mimi, your wife, the news of it woulo have come home to you be- fore this time. She's well, and she's kept away from you by some villain or the other who would profit by her story later 6n. That's my certain belief, and nothing will shake it. A girl as clever as Mimi the Simpleton is not going to stay in any cage while her wit can squeeze through the bars thereof; and we'll be hearing from her, with any luck, be- fore the year is very much older. So I say to you, cheer up. Hope's a good Friend, even if he does treat us uncivilly sometimes. Many the time, after taking ten in a bunker, have I foresworn the pastime of golf, and resolved, by my father's name, to take to hoeing turnips. But here I am, at a sixteen handicap still, and willing to back my luck against the company should occa- sion offer.</p>
        <p>I would tell you that we put the advertise- ment offering your thousand pounds for news of Mimi in all the papers, and have had per- haps 'a thoueand answers. This Loudon is a funny place, and as many rogues as fools in it. Sometimes I think that half the world's gone mad. Is our dear little girl a wild animal that people should be writing to you as they are writing? Every crank with a bee in his bonnet, every wide-eyed lunatic who thinks his -sister passed somebody like Mimi in the streets is spoiling good paper and pes- tering us. And then the newspapers- them- selves, still at it with -their theories, and the great doctors of learning, and the scholars from the colleges, and the lunatics that have escaped out of Bedlam, all large in print with their stories of what happened, and their advice gratis to the rest of the com- pany. 'Tis a very pandemonium of sugges- tion, and not one idea worth a silver three- penny among the whole of them. Meanwhile, Harry, my boy, will . you be letting Paddy O'Connell know what. he can do for you? 'Ti. no pleasant holiday-time— ten days for eleven guineas—that I'm spend- ing in these parts. Picture your friend walking on the lonely heath, and hunted about by vulgar men in buttons if he so much as drives a golf ball into a perambulator. This is my occupation —and when I'm tired or it, there are the horse-riders to be seen on the tan, and the motor-ears, which the police are fining. As for the horsemen, we ve -no such riding In Ireland, and wonderful it is to .see, espe- cially the elderly gentlemen on the six-and- sixpenny nags, who take a little horse exer- cise for the liver's sake. One of them fell off by the pond yesterday, and I caught his steeplechaser for him. Such a sorry nag never came out of a knacker's yard in :Ire- land ; but the man himself was shivering like a half-drowned dog when he came up. and sovereigns would not. have persuaded him to mount again. "Did ye see that?" he asks me; "did ye see him buck?" "Why," says I, "not exactly. But if you'll get up and make him do it again, I'll tell you what it is." Ho was very angry at this, and wouldn't hear of it. "I'm not a jockey," says he. "Do you suppose I'm • going to ride a buck-jumper? Wait till I get hack—l'll tell Boulder what I think of him!'' - - . .</p>
        <p>"Just clap your hands," says I to this, "and the old horse will run home by him- self. 'Tis a fine afternoon for walking, and good for the spirits. I would be taking the second hour first next time you go out. 'Tis cheaper in the long run." You can see any amount of these fellows on this "blasted heath," Harry, but not much else that I know of. And I am staying here because my old friend asked that same of me ; and, if he wishes it, I'll remain until they wake me and afterwards. As for the little house, the curious folk still come to stare at the place, and Sunday finds them loafing about half the day, just as though there were to be another bad business for their amusement, and a wrong done to them should it not happen. Yesterday on of, them pushed his nose so far into the garden that for a, halfpenny I would have punched it. He turned out to be a French waiter from a little hotel in Soho, and had much intelli- gence of his own; so, I fell to some agree- able talk with him, and was much struck with his remark that the real way to get at the truth would be by offering money to one of the gang to turn King's evidence. To he sure, we'd have to learn the name of the fellow first—but that's to be done, I am per- suaded—and if you would hear the French- men for yourself write .to Monsieur Jean Rabasseur at the Café Bousson, Soho. A man of some intellect who eight be useful to us. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, for the sake ot all that's charit- able, either summon me to Paris or send me back to Ireland. Your letters speak of a poor spirit; and if there is one man in tnis land of Sassenachs who could cheer you up, 'tis that same rogue Paddy O'Connell. So send for me and have done with it. - Martha leaves London to-morrow, and then I'll be lonely indeed. "I must go back to my dear husband," says She; and when I offered to send, for him to London, 'twas just "Heaven forbid, Mr. O'Connell! would you spoil my holiday?" Bo you see, women are much the same all the world over; and, if ever I would marry a wife, I'll look her up and down first, and ask myself some questions. Is she the kind to go in double harness, and h -.w ill she run without blinkers? Is it my money she's after, or the beautiful face I see in the glass? Be sum I'll be hard to convince. "Cis rarely a husband's face the women see in that same mirror; and lucky for the husbands that they have no gift of second sight—all of which goes to the making of that incurable old bachelor—Your friend,</p>
        <p>PADDY.</p>
        <p>P.S.—There was a letter came to-day from the office of the "Daily Bulletin." I'm send- ing it on. You'll see it's marked "urgent," so don't answer it until you get it. Mean- while, 'the others are going to the waste- paper baqket, especially the bills, which have an ugly look, and should not be left lying ;but any house to annoy a man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHAPTER XXXVIL</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXVIL</p>
        <p>[ln which we hear of Henry Gastonard at' the Pavilion Henry Quatro in the town of St. Germain by Paris.] Pavilion Henry Quatre, St. Germain, By Paris. • November sth, 1905.</p>
        <p>Dear Paddy,—l have very great news for you, and I hardly know how to tell it. This must be the excuse for my silence these many days. Oh, my dear old chap. if you knew what it all meant to me ! But I will try to tell you soberly, though God knows how much my impatience tempts me to heroics.</p>
        <p>You have been to St. G ermain in my motor, and will not haTe forgotten it. Don't you renienther that great forest plateau above (ho river; how the old ear groaned to</p>
        <p>climb the heiEhi, and what a - lovely view we had from the very terrace before the win- dows?</p>
        <p>That was in the "witching month of sum- mer." I re,nusraber that we set out after a dinner at Rernotti's, which you thought execrabl9, and a supper, chi!: Maxim, which won upon your fancy. We were a rirtie carrie in the car, and you were not a 'little alarmed kest news of the escapade, and of two young lnd amiable ladies from the Clia- telet should ever reach the secluded shades of Glvmdaloagb. Those, my dear Paddy, were the roses of yesteryear. We gathered them upon the velvet sward of youth, the vine leaves in our hair, and the garlands of eternal hope about our brows. You, I remember, were all for a fortune year uncle was about to leave you, and a ck teau at Bougival-1 for a gold medal at I he salon and a niche in the eternal temple. Let us draw the veil upon such a treacherous jade. Did not old John O'Con- nell leave every shilling to the priests—bad eess to him, and is not the very bust I then worked upon become a corner-stone in the house of my abasement? _</p>
        <p>lam on-co again at St. Germain, Paddy, and the chaA.ged season of the year speaks eloquently for me. Uhat a rare day of autumn, what a chill, bleak night, with a voice to whisper of winter! Here is the very same terrace, magnificent as of yore— that terrace upon a glorious height where- from you look down to the valley of the shining river, away across the desolate plain of poplar, whereupon humanity plays its Liliputian. role upon a mighty field, and be- yond whieli Paris herself is but a blur upon a far hor zon. But it is a silent terrace, Paddy, and the "monde" has deserted it. No gay music of fiddles now; no majesty of womanhood; no rustling silks and floating chiffon, and " Monsieur " to glance to the right of him and to the left of him ere sit. tingiat the table of his guilt. • Even the " tear teuf " is silent, and the very stables cry desolaiion to you. The sun gave and the sun hAs taken away; and for want of that litth sunbeam the chiffoned elves are hidden, and the world of laughter has, fled the woods.</p>
        <p>But I hear you resenting this philosophy and ask -mg for the news. Let an excuse . of prolixity be that of the day and of the hour. It is Sunday, and but two parties at this famous house. I linger here because the events of these latter days forbid me to tear myself • away. Would you have it otherwise as the circumstances go? Was it not on Fri- day that Jules Farman came to me, whis- pered. a word in my ear that quickened my pulse as wine, and even hinted that the end was near? A more reticer t man does not exist—there is no greater pes.iimist in all the service. And he came to me &amp;id told me— lie pernitted me to hope—he encouraged me to believe the best. Do you wonder that behaved like a fool for three good hours, and did not cease so to behave until he threatened to leave me behind and to do the work—if work were to be done—himself?</p>
        <p>There are some private affairs, Paddy, which are better for the wisdom of an old friend's tongue—and this is one of them. I was in a poor way when Farman came to me and had begun to say that the Marquis de Saint Faur would never reveal what he knew of the Count d'Antoine's visit to Lon- don. More than that, I imagined that the purpose of the visi.`, could throw no light upon its dreadful sequel. Here was a French gentleman, of the older fashion, who told me plainly that he kept a secret from me, but begged me to believe that his reti- cence was both wise and honourable. And I must believe him; I must carry the assur- ance that he knew, and that his knowledge must be hidden froni me. '</p>
        <p>- A torture truly—for what • burden is so heavy to a man in doubt as the silence of another who could speak? 1 perceived that the Marquis would never speak; and, driven to the belief that my little wife had not left England at all, was upon the point of keep- ing my promise to you when Farman came with his amazing story, and all the castles of despair were demolished in an instant. •</p>
        <p>_ This was at five o'clock of the afternoon of Friday. There had been a • day of wonderful sunshine for the month of November, and many people in the streets. I lunched with that pleasant fellow, the Chevalier Honore de Villefort at the Madrid, and went with him afterwards to the house of the famous painter, Delmormet, who has a studio for portraits in the Avenue de Malakoff.- A pro- mise to Villefort that we would go up to the Butte together to dine, and then make a tour of the old cafés, was not fulfilled, for I had no sooner come in sight of the Hotel St. Paul than I remarked a large motor-car standing at the door, and leaped to the con- clusion that it had come for. me. In this l was not mistaken,. Jules Farman himself waited in my room, and instantly informed me of the truth. -</p>
        <p>"Madame Gastonard is at Bougival—in an old house near the river," he said.</p>
        <p>I looked at him, but did not answer. The room and all things in it were spinning be- fore me as a gyroscope. The temptation to laugh was almost uncontrollable. "What do. you say, Farman?" "That Madame Gastonard is at Bougival, and that we must go there immediately:" I walked across to the buffet and filled a wine-glass with brandy. It was odd to hear my own heart beating, odd to be lifted in an instant, as it were, upon the wings of light to a very heaven of gratitude and thankS- giving—and yet my prudence saved me. Doubt whispered loudly in my ear. I could laugh for excitement and yet curse the fate which made me doubt. Even Jules Farman was powerless against that native caution which has saved me so 'many days. "Why do you believe this, Farman? " "Monsieur, he said very simply, "the man Jean-le-Mont has told us something." "He has told you something—yes—but what? Speak, man, for God's sake ! Is your heart of marble that you play with me like this? " He seemed astonished. He is officialdom personified. What are men and women to him but names to be docketed, identities to be established, the persons of the drama which can move him to no excitements.</p>
        <p>"It is a long story," be said next, "and we have little time. If you arc ready, we will go to Bougival." I did not answer him immediately. The voice of prudence cried to m 2 to make haste. Good God, that I should delay, I who would have gone through fire and water for Mimi's sake! And he reproached me, 'this smooth- faced servant of bureaucracy who had done Ili§ work so well.</p>
        <p>"I am ready, Farman—let us go," I said— for he delayed now. He pointed to my thin overcoat. "Not without our furs, monsieur; - the night will be cold." The rebuke was just, and I perceived. that he was heavily clad, though in other respects the same -reticent, unobtrusive creature we have always known. When all was ready, he gave a direction to the driver to go to Asnieres—a suburb : o the north-west of Paris —and taking his seat beside me in the ton• neau, we made the journey without a single word spoken between us And what could I say to him? What trea- sure of my inmost thoughts should I lay bare at such an hour? My love? Impossible to speak of that: My gratitude? He was well aware of it. The doubts lingering, the spec fres of incredulity? They *sat by. his side, too, for his very reservations betrayed him.: Enough for me to say, Mimi is at Bougival ; I am going to her; I shall find her to-night ; to-morrow she will awake upon my heart. Were we not flying towards hetl—streets and boulevards devoured us with an appetite of distance insatiable ; houses and shops, cafés and restaurants—so Many stars of light to guide us • intervals of blackness; bridges above and rivers below; trains crashing upon iron girders; trams humming toward the- city—all the panorama of the flight as though shown upon a cloth. And afterwards the open country. Streets emaciated; factories at intervals; red blasts of light against a black sky; the fresher air of fields and parks;- and beyond all, one clear star upon an imagined horizon—the star of cur faith and purpose. I have never been more grateful tq a motor-car in my life, and may never be as grateful again. Here was a machine of steel- whose soul sent out a sympathetic message to my own. "I know, know' know." 'the voice of it seemed to say. A horse bending to the whip could not have answered more readily to the fevered pries of a despairing master. We covered the ground as upon some magic carpet stretched out at my de- sire. When the flight ended, when the voice from the heart of steel was no longer heard, I noticed that we stood before a little café in the narrow street of an inconsiderable town. And I was as one awakened from a nightmare of sleep. Was she here; was this the house? The omens said, no. I glanced at the café and perceived two old friends standing at its doorway. They were the men- dicant Georges Oleander and the merry Che- valier Honore de Villefort7-w horn I had but just left in Paris. _ _</p>
        <p>"We are here to dine with the, Chevalier," Farman whispered to me as we descended; "please to remember that. We are returning at eleven o'clock. I wish no other suggestion to be made. It is very important." I nodded my head to him, and we entered the house together. A zeal, burning to a. point almost beyond endurance, bade me wel- come this respite, as a man may welcome an inn upon a journey to the house of his plea- sures. The cafe, I observed, was one fre- quented by the students ; a shabby little place, with a damp, stained frieze of faded gilt to speak of ancient glories. The occu- pants of it were a dozen students, poets, painters, *lnd the riff-raff of the schools. Dinner had been set for us in a mock cabinet part ;culler —but a sorry imitation of the genuine article. Here, anon, we were joined by Desmond Barrymore, and immediately he had enterd, the patron himself- served up the first course of a dinner whose menu might have been in Chinese, for all I remember of it.</p>
        <p>Imagine my feelings, Paddy, as I sat at this rude table with the old friends who have fought so many battles—and crackat so many bottles—of Bohemia in my company. .Not many months mit), Barrythore and I were fighting for little Mimi's very life in the Lapin Agil on the Butte. I left Paris, and he came acmes the sea to witness my marriage. Again a few weeks roll on, and he is here ts a silent witness uf my despair. The others, good fellows that they were, could but act A mean parr on such a night. Ah, for the old times when no man cared a sou for the morrow, and sufficient for the ,day was the evening thereof. This thought tempted me sometimes. We may well dread a gift of happiness, for shall there not be an aftermath of sorrow, whatever our fortune? It was not hidden from me that Jules Far- man had called upon these good comrades of mine to share his confidence. They were ex- cited, but not eloquent. - Recalling old days of the games, I found them in that mood which overtakes a man when he is about to run or row a race, and believes that he will win it. They talked at rare intervals, drank much wine, evaded my questions, and rarely looked • me in the face. • When dinner was over, we went into the outer room .and joined the Bohemians there. This I understood to be part of the strata- gem, and I made no comment upon it; and, for that matter, the scene was droll enough. The artist—especially the French Artist—is Suburbia is a wild creature, as you know well; nor were these men exceptions. We discovered them in all attitudes, some sprawling at the tables, some billing and cooing in far secluded corners. The man al the piano had a girl upon his knees, and cuddled her while he played. The shadow. graph which they acted presently would have brought the police into any establishment in London. But there it seemed innocent enough. After all, the spirit of the play goes for much. Depict a far from clean sheet drawn across one end of this mean room, and a shadow- play cast upon it. There is a sick man shown, and he it 3 i, ex...teen-as:. Appear a doc- tor, who pulls out the fellow's tongue with a pair of pliers, and rams it in again with a motor tyre lever. Medicine is administered to the moribund creature as a horse is drenched with drugs. The relatives gather round the bed, and begin to divide up the man's property between them. But they are reckoning without their host. The wonder- ful drug acts upon the sick man with amazing and miraculous results. He sits up in bed, wave the throng off, , spurns his wife aside, leaps from his couch, and embraces the pretty nurse ecstatically. This is the whole cure; and those who were the specta- tors have now become the imitators, inspired by the mens sana. The artists embrace all the pretty girls near them. Somebcdy puts out the electric light, and in the darkness we hear squeals and giggles. Thus genius amuses itself—thus, the story of the Youth of to-day, whom Fame will aeclarm at maturity to-Morrow..</p>
        <p>Just as a man upon his way to a nmdez- vous. where the, opportunity of - a -lifetime awaits him, maybe amused by the gamins of the pavement, so did this absurlity of the café engross me. It was something to lean back in my chair and to tell myself that I was a Prisoner there for Mimi's sake, Later,</p>
        <p>, "LONDON OPINION." The bright`and popular weekly paper, "London Opinion." has now been acquired by a new Company, and the Editorship will be undertaken by Mr. Lincoln Springfield. New and entertaining features are being introduced. The "Letters of Algy," the series of bright stories of London Life behind the Scenes. which sent the circulation of the Paper to over two hundred and fifty thousand weekly. are now appearing, besides the wittiest drawings of the leading Black-and- White Artists of the d,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON TO HANKOW.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TO HANKOW.</p>
        <p>WEST TO EAST OVERLAND.</p>
        <p>BY GEORGE REEVES.</p>
        <p>I make no pretence at giving a graphic detailed description of a trip to China "overland," for to cross the hemisphere in 21 days affords one little opportunity to describe that part of the globe, from west to east, therefore your readers must gain what satisfaction they can from a brief survey. I left Warrington on the 15th day of November, 1908, at 8 a.m. for London, arriv- ing at 12 noon. I immediately made all final arrangements for the journey, which consisted in procuring a book of tickets (thanks to Cook's agents) from London to Moscow, 241 Russian Rouble notes and 20- 20 marks German. "I may also add I took £2O sterling, the English sovereign being changeable in most countries. I spent a good 6 hours in London, leaving High Holborn about 8 o'clock at night, for Queen- borcffgh. I took steamer for Flushing, Holland, arrived next morning at 5 o'clock, after a very rough passage. When leaving Queenborough, the onlY interesting sights were the lights of Margate, which soon faded in the distance, as the fast steamer plunged through the strong wind, rain and heavy sea. I was not sea sick, but I can assure anyone I did not indulge in sweet repose. Whilst in my bunk I had a sensation as if held suspended in a coalpit shaft, and being frequently jerked up and down, neither going to the bottom nor coming to the top. I was almost getting used to this kind of thing, when the steward came round, 'telling the</p>
        <p>'-,-, ' ' 1 • ~,,•..,,,„,- 4.,,.,,,-,,,,-,.. , , -,,,, ,„- • : ~-'--4,., • 111 n , ~.,. , .. .....,.., .4 . ' , ,4 ' \ ' - • . ' - ' ' --',V'; --- '..-",- •-• '?" egt: , , -,'•-• •'' -- , ,-, • • .'-' ,•.,--• : .2 '‘...-,'. --' -.- .- . • 1'; i'"' : . , , , •.:':. ",' . '' ' '' ,•'..- -• . -,. ~..-• - : - -.•:, • •• t ' • - ‘. •• .:. " , . ...., • • . . . -...•• . . .. • _ •••</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MY STUDY AND BEDROOM COMBINED IN THE FAR EAST.</head>
        <p>MY STUDY AND BEDROOM COMBINED IN THE FAR EAST.</p>
        <p>passengers to dress, as the•ship would be in port in an hour. I landed ! and with a passing look of sympathy for the mariner (with a cast iron constitution) who had walked the bridge all night, I passed into a final station. I at once enquired of a blue-nosed station official with an alcoholic stare about his face, if this was the train. for Berlin. He replied with an expectant relish, "Ja " (yes.) I produced the magic coin, aud told him to bring my luggage, and I was soon speeding along in a comfortable corridor train on terra firma for the rest of my journey, which is hardly credible considering the distance I had to travel. But if you try conclusions with an hemispherie map, you will find terra firma wins. I now had before me a good run in plenty of daylight, without change of trains to Berlin. The land is very flat, which affords one an opportunity to see the surrounding country, a land of dairy farming and agri- culture, studded with wind-mills of various types, which like the numerous canals the Dutch possess, are made very good use of. I spent most of my time looking through the corridor windows, admiring the ever-changing scene, when an incident happened which savours of romance. I will mention it for the benefit of those who occasionally travel alone, especially those who are not old enough to take care of themselves. I strolled down the corridor, and in so doing, I encountered a young lady. She smiled and displayed a perfect set of pearly teeth, and greeted mo with " guten morgen (" good morning.") I could not muster courage to answer, as my knowledge of German is limited. She tried again to strike the chord of response and said" Sprechen Sic Deutch," ("speak you German. ") I said " Ich weiss noch nicht viele worter " (" I don't know many words "). She then addressed me in English and asked how far I was going. When I replied "China," she offered me some chocolate creams, evidently thinking I needed a little encouragement. I learned she was the daughter of wealthy Germans in 'Hanover. She was studying painting, and frequently visited artistic friends in London. She increased the agony of the long journey before me by reminding me of the beauty spots of England. I invited her opinion on travelling alone. She replied that every year she paid England a visit. without any paternal guidance. I hinted one would naturally expect she would have a companion to travel home with after frequent visits to a place like England. She said the difficulty lay in finding the right one. She invited my opinion about Germany, and ventured to ask if I preferred England to Germany. I replied I had seen pretty girls in both countries. , She then asked if I was joking, and was I married—and the station officials shouted "Hanover," and my fair companion of twenty-five summers showed her pearly teeth again and said "Adieu." I felt a vacancy in the train, but as I am no Mark Twain, and my wife reads the "Examiner," well—</p>
        <p>I arrived in Berlin at five o'clock, only having 35 minutes to change money, register luggage and change trains for Alexandrovo, so the only object of my admiration was the fine station—Frederick Bank. I was soon moving along in a remarkably fast train for Alexandrovo, and in the small hours of the morning I was rudely asked in tones—sound- ing something like Russian—for my pass- port. I realised I had reached the Russian frontier. I was down for customs examina- tion here, so wended my way into a spacious building, with counters all round the interior. After a search amongst an army of officials in gaudy uniform, and armed to the teeth, I discovered my luggage. The passengers' names were called out in Russian, and when I heard someone shout " Ruvee," I shouted " here" ! and I was fortunate in guessing aright, I was fined 10 kopeck's (21d) for having a new knife and fork in my trunk. The consequence of not hearing your name,when it is called out,is very unpleasant. I saw boxes and portmanteaux brutally attacked and broken open, with hammer and chisel, because the owners were not there at the moment, or did not recognise their names in Russian, or could not produce their keys quick enough. I paid my fine, received back my passport, and wondered what the remainder of Russia was like, if this was the first sample of the difficulties to be met with as a traveller through the country. Anyhow I felt thankful I had crept through the first gap all right, into the land of freedom for Despots.</p>
        <p>entrained again an soon had the satiq-</p>
        <p>right in the end,and like the proverbial fairy- tale,all live happy ever afterwards. During an interval of 30 minutes between each playetto the auditors repair to a ball room, finely deporated, the walls being entirely covered - with mirrors. The people parade the floor to the tune of an orchestra admiring their figures through the mirrors as they walk round. Such pomp and vanity I never witnessed in my life before. The performances start at 8 o'clock at night and continue until 2 or 3 o'clock in the morning.</p>
        <p>Mosc-ow can boast a fine art gallery. and I saw pictures from the brush of Russian artists, equal to anything in Eng- land. They also have a fine show of French paintings, many of which have hung in the Paris Salon. I was much interested in vis - ing the Kremlin, within which is the Czar s Palace. Moscow is the ancient capital of Russia, but was burnt down by the citizens. - when invaded by the French troops in 1812. Parts of the ancient outer walls of the castle still remain, which date back to the eleventh century. Within the gates, can be seen many guns taken froni the French during the invasion. Another interesting featur,o, in the Castle grounds is the bell tower, which contained the largest bell in the world, and which fell from the tower more than 100 years ago, a large portion breaking off as it reached the ground. The broken bell still remains where it fell. Climbing the bell tower, which reaches a giddy height, and commands most points,of the conipass a fine panoramic view of the city can be obtained. Moscow has some fine architecture, and many of the buildings are topped with gilded metal domes and artistic finials, different sizes and shapes, which, glistening in the sunlight above the green tiled roofs and richly carved • and painted. basements, lent enchantment to the view. There is plenty of wealth in Russia, judging from the standard of Moscow. Millions of pounds would not buy the wealth of her churches and monasteries. I visited St. Saviour's Temple, a veritable marble hall, columns of marble rising to a tremendous height, supporting architectures of Gothic design. There is a well-laid Mosaic floor of exquisite design, and round the walls of the interior are fixed marble pedestals, with pictures of lie saints, moulded in solid gold, studded with thousands of diamonds, pearls, opals, sapphires and rubies. Truly a hoard of.. fabulous wealth, designed to serve the purposes of ecclesiastics smitten with a, fanatic Ritualism.</p>
        <p>The Russian restaurants are a solution to the drink problem in my opinion. I visited some where very much money must have been spent in making them comfortable and attrac- tive. One of them was the size of Parr Hall, and would comfortably seat 1,500 people. You enter through massive doors, which are opened for you and shut after you. Waiters take off your coat, hat and goloshes. You take your seat at one of the numerous small tables, and order your tea or coffee, and fancy' biscuits, the charge being 10 kopecks (21d3 Tea is mostly drunk, and I am fully converted to the Russian style of tea drinking. The tea is served hot in a tumbler glass, contain- ing a slice of lemon. You sweeten to taste. As a thirst quencher, and a fascinating drink, I never experienced anything like it. The tea is not brewed black like it is in England, but of the colour of pale ale. I was anxious to know how this tea was brewed and.l was given the-following recipe: The tea is brewed in a pot having a receptacle in the top to hold the tea. Boiling water is poured over the tea "slowly," poured frorn the pot, then poured over the tea again: The tea leaves are kept out of the brewed tea entirely. In these restaurants no alcoholic drink is taken at all, and at night time they are crowded with people, who sit and chat over their tea or coffee. The buildings are brilliantly lighted, and the heating arrangements are specially attended to.</p>
        <p>(To be concluded next week.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
