      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>faction of seeing daylight, arriving at - Warsaw at 8 o.c. the same morning. I had trouble here, for I had to change stations necessitating a drive across the city. I hired • a carriage, and arriving at my destination I gave the driver 21 R's (ss. 3d.).He gesticulated, frothed at the mouth, and seized my coat collar. I objected to such indignities, and violently dislodged his hold. I made all effort to obtain my luggage, but the driver saw my move, and seized it first. MY patience was now exhausted, and seeing a large crowd gathering round, I thought it was time to resort to other means, i.e., drawing my revolver,—when a military policeman came forward. I made him under- stand by showing him some rouble notes, tile amount I had given the driver. The police- man was satisfied I had given the right fare, and turned his attention to the driver, threatening him with the butt end of a large service revolver. I escaped from the crowd into the station and a Russian lady who had witnessed the incident, told Inc in English that the driver wanted 5 R's (10s.) I replied: " Perhaps he thought I came from the country, and I was a Hodge." I left Warsaw three hours later for Moscow arriving at one o'clock next day, this being a welcome break in my journey. I stayed 35 hours in Moscow before start- ing on the Trans-Siberian Railway, to cross Siberia. I visited the theatre, art gallery, the Kremlin' (Castle), the monasteries, temples and Russian cafes. The Russian style of theatre performances—if what I saw is general throughout 'Russia—will not suit the English playgoers. It consists of a series of operatic playettes, each lasting about 30 minutes, and of a romantic nature, the lover having a jealous rival. Everything cotnes</p>
      </div>
