        <p>NEWCHURCH PARISH CHURCH.—A apt ?ial service b aid on Sunday afternoon last in the Newchurch re.ttish Church, conducted by the Rev. W. G. Webster 31°1. Zanzibar. He gave a very interesting address on tarien work in Zanzibar, after which a collection was en for the missionary fund which amounted to los. 111)84111 ()PAN OLD CULCHETHITE.—The death took Z:te at his residence, 531, Warrington-road, Culcheth, 'undaY last, of Mr. Henry Thomason. The deceased 1:130 been the trustee for the " St. Peter's Glory" ever dge of the Ancient Order of Foresters at Culclieth th;,sinee its inauguration. He was well known and 40Y respected in the village, having lived there over are• He was a widower, and leaves four sons and cillerdaughters. The funeral took place at the Parish ti eh) Newchurch, on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall. DA CHOOT. TEACHERS' MEETING.—The Sunday Tfachers' Association connected with the Rural ~Ine,ry of Frodsham held its half-yearly meeting at --'snarn on Saturday, Clergy and teachers to a b tlea,„er aPProaohing 250 came from all parts of the .-eq.—At half-past three service was held in the itruih Church, prayers being read by the Vicar, Rev. iur V, • Pigot, and the lesson by the Rev. Canon Blogg, "al dean, and president of the Association. Among iter clergy present was Bet. R. W, Evans, vicar of who is also secretary of the Association. The r was the Rev. W. Bracecamp, vicar of St. a, Warrington, who said there were four qualifica- t4ll,3 of Sunday school teachers, the love of. Christ in own hearts, knowledge of their message, the of- what they preached, and love of the children. anrcl'iter the service, tea was served in the Parish R,oom, woo.the arrangements were most creditable to the local ass;Lelll. The Vicar of Grappenhall, with his able ean-'aurs. was cordially thanked on the proposition of addreo Blogg, while the Rev. W. Bracecamp, for his received a similar compliment on the pro- °Q of Mr. John Litter,' J.P. In the evening a 47,...ant time mas spent in the Rectory grounds, placed at the disposal of the visitors by the ear and Mrs. Pigot.</p>
        <p>Lymm. -N tffANGEß.—William Dixon, son of Mr. T. 40'3;5 milk dealer, had something of a shock on k "IV last in. New Road. Whilst engaged in the 4ceful occupation of delivering milk a shot from an air fired in an adjacent garden scruck hinrin the -dace, f;•Dixon,jun., is to be congratulated on a narrow esc tp il."1 what might have been a very serious accident— Lue loss of an eye. A c .JUCCESS FOR MR. COOKE.—At the Southnort esical Festival on Thursday, Miss Emily Wooley, of "corn, a pupil of Mr. William Cooke, F.R.C.0., Nattist th of L nim Parish Church, gained the first- prize lh e • Zasior bPeinanotorte solo competition, Mr. Cole' idge g the adjudicator. We may add that Miss Wooley won the first prize in the children's ;,"„roforte playing competition at each of the last two —Luti.cal festivals held in Warrington.</p>
        <p>THE MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL. on Thursday next, this popular festival promises to ass all previous attempts, given a fine day. The I,.e°M.naittee have taken every. step necessary to attain . MI-flreltion A grand procession will leave the Cross at bY way of Church Green, Brookfield, Elm Tree-road, 17- lane, Eagle Brow Village to the The May- Queen 17;,are the !ncludecycle parade, ,characters representing eyrungiand and other Colonies." Six of each character as-tlaPPear. This will prove a most attractive feature re„ la spectators will be able to see every character Thresented instead of missing half, as when one of each ,aPPeared. About a dozen decorated lurries are iia7rLivcfee_d, some representing tableeux of Britennia, Prient::::ns9:eireion,g The Seasons, &amp;c. &amp;c. The Irwell a"sslis:t3helaynniruhaeo been engaged.' They will give the afternoon performance and will band with the dancing in the r"ening' as as give a selection. Other attractions maypole dance, fairy dance, golliwog dance, E %stern nee' tarleY dame, by Miss F. Jackson, of Kuutsford, nd the hornpipe and Irish jig, by Miss Dean and tthafxrrhesibt‘eireti,lo:.G,iidlima_nn, of Keutsfo.rd, whilst Mr. F. Brabner, elanchester amateur, will give a roller skating e. pert. e .Aightring, to,n, gymnastic club will give trad °rmance under Mr. 1. Leigh's direction. The ssmen's e round turnouts will meet at the Cross at 1.39 and ee 3ed ~nd the route to the field, where they will be thege' immediately before the procession arrivbs on thns running less ri*k of accidents to the i and characters. The May Queen s Miss Emma Ada Of Statham, whilst the Rose Queen is Mils INlLs°a.,s, last year's Queen. The turnout judge is Mr. tie;"aof Altrincham, and Mr. Pickervance will decide 03'e1e parade winners. Special trains and cheap 4111'484ms have been arranged from Liverpool, sehester and other towns. LYMM URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL Th Monthly meeting of the above Council took place tke hursday evening last. Mr. 0. G. Smith 