      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>FOUNDATION STONE LAID OF NEW CHAPEL</head>
        <p>FOUNDATION STONE LAID OF NEW CHAPEL</p>
        <p>The Primitive Methodists of Broom Edge will in itort time. be able to worship in their own church instead of as at present, and for many years past, in a room rented from the Anglican Church ; for on Saturday last in the presence of a large assembly of people the foundation stones of a new chapel were laid. The chapel;which is being erected near the main road, will, when finished, accommodate nearly 30 persons—quite adequate for the population of the village—and the cost including land will be between ±l5OO and £7OO.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. 13. W. W bitewaypresided and amongst 1 those present were :--The Rev 3. James Travis, A. J. Barratt, G. Parkin, G. Mu hell, and E. Stokes. The Rev. A. Barratt having offered up prayer, the Chairman introduced the Rev. George Mitchell, who, he said, was one of their senior ministers. He had laboured during- the heat of the day, and he (the speaker) knew of his power for good. He would ask Mr. Mitchell to deposit into the foundation of the building a glass jar, which was, as they knew, the usual custom. The jar contained a copy of the "Daily News," a copy of the "Manchester Guardian," a copy of the "Primitive Methodist Leader," a "Circuit Plan;"</p>
        <p>the Holy Ghost. i pronorince this stone :truly laid." Bricks were .bid by the following : Mrs. Talbot, Mr. Blease, Mrs. Garner, Mrs.. Lawson, Master George Daniels, Miss M. Daniels, Miss. Gertie Daniels, Miss Lizzie Daniels and Miss Sumner, all of whom con- tributed .to the funds of the 'Chapel. After the ceremony the Rev. James Travis delivered an address on the -history of Primitive Methodism in the course of which he said they did net claim antiquity,but there was a striking likeness between the pioneers of Christianity, and the pioneers of Primitive Methodism. The pioneers of Christianity were treated with con- tempt by the priests and clst into prison by Magistrates. So were some of the pioneers of Primitive Methodism. The pioneers of Christianity laid down their lives in the cause of the blessed Christ, who laid dowa their lives for thein Some pf the pioneers not only • hazarded their lives, but !aid down their lives in their endeavour to preach .the Gospel and so save the souls and lives of men. Continuing he said he was very concerned that their own people and the public shovld, understand what that great religious and social move- ment,which was know .1 as Primitive Methodism meant. Their movement, which was founded by William</p>
      </div>
