      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LAYI ; THE FOU ND A.TION STONE</head>
        <p>LAYI ; THE FOU ND A.TION STONE</p>
        <p>bearing the honoured names of the ministers, tile local preachers, the Society stewards, and nll the promi- nent people of the circuit. There was also a full list. of the trustees, the name of the architect and builder, the name S of.the stone layers, list of the officers of the Broom- Edge ChurCh, a copy Of the begging circular, a small handbill of the day's proceedings and its programme. The Rev. G. Mitchell said it was 59 years since he joined the Church: • The founders of the denomination were both alive at the time. He had been a local preacher 41 years and had been a minister for 54 years. They were laying the foundation stones of that place that day under the idea of a church there having an important future, and for doing work for which prayer had just been offered by the Rev. A.Barratt. Concluding he said they used to tell hiini when he was a boy that truth lay in a well. Now he believed that truth lay in a jar. (Laughter and applause.) The rev. gentleman amid cheers then deposited the jar in the cavity prepared for it: Stones were laid by the following : Mr. W. Cross who gave £5O, Mr. J. Manley (on behalf of Sir W. P. Hartley) who gave £5O, Mrs Manley 2 who gave 110; Miss Kean who gave £5, Mr. W. Davies who gave £5, Mr. H. Bancroft, sen., who gave £5, Mr. H. Bancroft, jun., who gave £5, and Councillor G. L. Welford who gave £2O. 1 Some of the stone-layers made short speeches, but all uttered, "In the Name of the Father, the Son and</p>
        <p>Clowes and "'Ugh Bourne,when it was startcl put.ne w life into.the.natiOn, and had continued ever since. • By that movement poets were inspired, and William Carpenter wrote several beautiful hymAti. Their Church was born in a revival in the•lBth century, ond was often a means of lifting the burdens of our scaal life'; and it also-prepared us for INiser citizenship and greater responsibility upon which we were to 'enter. Concluding, the speaker hoped that when the Chinch was ready much progress would be made in the work of the Master. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>DUST NUISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE DUST NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor reported that be had received a letter from the County Council stating that they were prepared to pay two-thirds instead of one-third of the cost of laying dust on the main roads. This left the surveyor with £l4 in hand and it was decided that it be expended °I. the tar-spraying and that the calcium chloride be used for dust laying on the main road—section 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>DEFACING THE VILLAGE CROSS,</head>
        <p>DEFACING THE VILLAGE CROSS,</p>
        <p>The Clerk nad a letter from the Secretary of the Lymm Tradesmen's Association stating that a resolu- tion to the following effect had been passed : "That this meeting relates with regret that the Cross is being disfigured by persons carving their initials, etc. on it, and we ask the Urban District Council as costodians of the Cross, to take action to prevent a repetition as this meetinc, believes tlrat II warning issued by them would have the desired effect." The Chairman : Ate we the proper authority to deal with it? . Councillor Groves : If we are the proper authority we ought to put a notice up. The Chairman : The Cross belongs to the lord of the Manor. Councillor Groves: Yet if it belongs to the lord of the Manor I think it is our duty to protect it. Councillor Wolfer ci proposed that the Clerk look into the matter and report. Councillor Groves 'seconded and said that they repaired the stocks some time ago. • The proposition was agreed to. MORTUARY WANTED. The Clerk read a letter dated May 24th, 1909, from the Lyrum Tradesmen's Association, which read as follows: "I am instructed to write and inform you tbat our association greatly supports Mr. Coroner Ridgway in his view as set forth in his letter to your Council, respecting a mortuary for Lymin." And at their last rneeing the followine. resolution was passed: "Th.rt, the secretary write the kUrban D:strict Council and invite them to deal with the question of a mortuary at the earliest possible moment." On the proposition of Councillor Welford seconded by Councillor Davies,the Clerk was instructed to write to the secretary of the Tradesmen's Association to say that the matter was being considered. Councillor Gotobed said that the gentlemen who moved and seconded the resolution did not appreciate to the same extent the need of a mortuary as those, who, like himself, were frequently called upon to serve on coroner's Jones and view bodies under disagreeable circumstances. Councillor Welford said that Mr. Gotobed spoke as though they were actuated from motives that were personal, and altogether 'apart f.om public. Mr. Gotobed : I did not mean it in that spirit. Mr. Welford : I take it in that spirit. Councillor Gotobed said that what he wished to con- vey was that if Mr. Welford had to Serve' on a jury as he had,he would think it objectionable. The matter. then dropped.</p>
        <p>A HEATLEY- FETITION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said a petition had been received asking that the space opposite the Post Office at Heatley Ice enclosed and shrubs planted on it. The matter was referred to the Roads Committee. A FRAUDULENT TEA TRAVELLER.</p>
        <p>HEATLEY WOMEN DUPED: At a special police court on Thursday,before Mr. Alfred Watkin (chairman), and Mr. W. P. Rylands, Mictael Sullivan dase-ibed as a merchant, hailing from Man- chester, was charged with fraudulently obtaining the sums of 2s. 70. from Ann Whitman, Heatley, Zls. from Ada Hill, Grove Avenue,Heatley,and 3s from Elizabeth Simpson, Grove Avenue, Heatley by false pretences. Ann Whitman gave evidence to the effect that on May 24th, the prisoner, went to her house and said he was travelling for the Crown. Tea Company. He asked witness if she -wanted to buy any tea which he produced. Witness said she did not want it. Prisoner said he would sell llb of tea for as. If witness bought it, she would receive a bonus of £1 on July 28th. Witness pave prisoner 2s. 74d. and he left the llb of tea. Prisoner stated to her that a man would go round with the £1 bonus the following Saturday.- Prisoner gave witness a receipt (produced). Witness gafe prisoner the 2s. VA. for the tea on the strength of petting the £1 bonus. He said he had 10lbs of tea to sell. Witness thought the tea was not worth 3s. per lb. Ada Hill, said the prisoner visited her house the same day and asked her if she would like a sovereign. Witness said "Yes" Prisoner then said that he, was going round for the Crown, Tea Company to get some customers. Be had some hills with him of which wit- ness could only see half, which contained the figures, 25., 2s. Gd. and 3s. on it. Witness asked him if he had any bills to ,give away, and he thereupon asked if witness wanted to buy some tea and asked 3s. a lb. for it. .Witness asked him if it was a fraud and prisoner replied "no." He also told her that if she bought the tea she would have El bonus in a - week, and 10s. for half a lb. Witness gave him 3s. and prisoner gave her the lb. of tea, also a receipt (produced). Elizabeth Simpson also eve evidence similar to the above, the prisoner told her that they were giving the bonus away in order to advertise. Witness bought the tea on the strength of obtaining the £1 bonus. The mixture made a rubbishy cup of tea.</p>
        <p>P.C. Dodd spoke to arresting prisoper. After receiving a complaint from Ada Hill, witness went in search of prisoner. He found him just outside Lymm. He had a bicycle with him. Witness drew prisoner's atten- tion to a parcel which was fastened to the machine, and asked him where he was taking it to. Prisoner replied "To Warrington." Witness asked him his name and he replied James Johnson. Witness having Mrs. Hill's receipt for the tea he drew it out of his pocket and showed it to prisoner. On seeing tbe receipt prisoner let his bicycle fall and ran for about 400 to 600 yards before being caught by witness. He took prisoner to the Police station and charged him with the three offences, to which prisoner replied "That's all right." On being searched a number of green pamphlets adver- tising tea were found upon him. The magistrates discussed the matter in private, and on their )eturn the Chairman said they had given prisoner's case the very greatest consideration. They had tried their hardest to keep prisoner from prison, but it was impossible owing to the frauds committed. He (prisoner) did not defraud the men, but the women while their husbands were at work. The women also were very silly for buy the tea, and he hoped it would he a warning to them. Turning to prisoner, Mr. Watkins said "You will be sent to His Majesty's prison at Knutsford for one month's. imprisonment with hard labour.”—The magistrates ordered the Money which the women had lost to be returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>EVENING MEETING.</head>
        <p>EVENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>A well-attended public meeting was held in the evening, when there were. Present Mr. G. L. We7-!ord, the Revs. James Travis, G. Parkin,. E. .Stokes, G. .S. R. Mitchell, R. W. Whiteway., &amp;c. Mr. Whiteway, pastor, of the Lynn Primitive Methodist Church, made a financial siztement, which gave the estimated cost Of the building at between £OOO and £703. The cost of the land (1,000 yards) was £l3O. Towards this there was a sum, in hand of £63. The amount collected by means of the stones and bricks was ±:112 Bs, collected afternoon Bs., at tea 3-.:4 Ss. 6d. ; in the evening the sum collected was :4:3 19s. 5d., making a total in hand from various sources of about A:400. Besides this, it is estimated that about ,200 will be received from the Centenaty Fund, so that between now and September, when the open: ing ceremony will be held; the building will, with the aid of the kind helpers, be practically free from debt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Moore.</head>
        <p>Moore.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.—The Sunday school anniversary sermons in connection with the Moore Wesleyan Church were preached on Sunday last by the Rev. E. J. Gawne,of Liverpool, and formerly of this circuit. Although the morning was very showery, in the afternoon the clouds dispersed, and the sun shone continuously during the remainder of the day. There was a large congregation at the afternoon service, and in the evening these were augmented by a large number of friends from other parts of the circuit who had been drawn by a desire to hear Mr. Gawne once more. The sight of the large congregations must surely have con- vinced Mr. Gawne how welcome was his re-appearance in our midst. At both services special hymns and anthems were sung by the children and choir. The collections for the day amounted to £l9, this sum being a substantial increase on that raised the previous year. The officers asked that the friends would do their best to raise this surn,and wished to thank all for so willingly responding to their appeal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Stockton Heath. PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath. PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above committee was held on Tuesday evening, when Mr. G. Pilling -took the chair and other members present were :—Messrs. J. H. Clarke, J. Whitley, J. Alynekyme, F. Pearson, F. Warburton, T. Baxter, C. J. Brown, H. J. Westbrook, J. McDermid, G. F. Ashton (clerk), and J. Swinton (surveyor). The Clerk presented the report of a meeting of the Stockton Heath Police Station Sub-committee of tte County Council, with regard to the application from the Runcorn Rural District Council, to the effect that the Standing Joint Committee should allocate about 50 square yards of land, forming a part of the garden at the Stockton Heath Police Station 'for the purpose of enabling the District Council to widen and otherwise improve an alleged public highway constituting a back passage connecting Victoria-square and Gaskell-street. Having heard the representatives of the Runcorn Rural District Council and Stockton Heath Parish Council and also inspected the Ceunty property, they regretted that they could not see their way to give up any portion of the land attached to tho present police station but if the Council could give such assurance as might be con- sidered satisfactory, no question would he raised to at least an equal quantity of land being taken from Victoria-square to that dedicated for w idening the back passage, as suggested by ties County architect, and on the District Council' undertaking not to charge any part of the cost of the widening and making of the back passage upon the County in respect of their I frontage of such passage, the matter should be further I considered if and when such proposals were submitted. Mr. Westbrook - The only portion that could be enclosed would dam the entrance to the passages. The Clerk : It piactically means that the Parish will have to pay the cost. arr. Westbrook: I think we should be giving more away than we should get. We should he losing. You will never really have a wide street there. The Chairman said that if they pressed the matter the passage would be closed. It was a private passage. Mr. Westbrook said that with the extia, widening' they were really inviting more danger than they relieved themselves of: _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Alynekyrne : The blunder was made when the property was built.</p>
        <p>The matter as -deferred for further consideration.</p>
        <p>POSTAL FACILITIES. The Clerk read a letter from the Warrington Post- master in response to an application from the Stockton Heath Parish Council, which stated that he had obtained the authority to erect a pillow h2OC at the corner of Ellesmere-road and Roman-road. He asked for a site so that he could have it erected. The Chairman : I move that Mr. Brown and Mr. Westbrook decide the position. This was carried.</p>
        <p>FIRE ENGINE. The Clerk read the report of the Fire Appliances Committee which stated that the tender for a bell of Mr. Hayes, of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., had been accepted at a cost of £1 12s. 6d.,a150 two lamps, repairs o Fire Station, alteration of the position of the bel</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The meeting of the above Board was held in the Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday,Mr. T. Baxter presided. There were also present :—Messrs. J. Spruce. G. Pilling, G. Egerton Warburton, J. H. Davies. J. Whitlow, A. Broome. W. Mandsley, A. Haining, P. Booth, T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, W. N. Morris, C. E. Limiter, jun., Bolshaw, A. R. Norman, J. Antwis, S. Woodward, J. W. Temperley, L. Bolton, E. Egerton, C. Roylan 0: V. Ashton (clerk), H. Warburton (acting r -sistant clerk), T. Cooke, W. R. DePrez and J. Pearson (reliev- ing officers), T. D. Rollinson (mrster). NUMBER OF INMATES. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce reported that there were 167 inmates of the House rs compared with 171 the corr: :ponding week lest year. There was a big influx of vag—nts, there being 116 comparcd with 88 the corresponding week last year. If there was not a decline in the number there would be difficulty as to accommodation. Mr. Spruce said that with regard to Sir John Brunner's offer to supply srectules to the school children, it would be of considerable benefit if they could avail themselves of such an offer. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker,jun., said that some years ago Sir John Brunner made an offer that all elementary school children who needed spectacles should be supplied with them and the cost be charged to him. That wes before the Education Act came into force and he did not 1--low how the matter stood now.</p>
        <p>Mr. Morris moved that the Board provide specticles at their own cost. This was carried.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL,</p>
        <p>_ The fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held in Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday under the presidency of Mr. T. Wright. There were also prerent : Messrs. J. Spruce, G. Pillina, G. Egerton-Warburton, J. W. Davies, J. Whitlow' S. Broom, W. Maudsley, S. Harvey, I'. Booth, J. B. Chadwick, W. M. Morris, C. E. Linaker, inn., J. Bolshaw, T. Antwis, J. Wood- ward, J. W. Timperley, L. Bolton, E. Egerton, C. Roylance. Messrs. W. J. Diggle and J. Swinton (sur- veyors), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health), 3. Farrington (inn- ?ctor of nuisances), G. F. Ashton (clerk) and H. Warburton (acting assistant clerk).</p>
        <p>DUST LAYING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harry F. Bull, county surveyor, wrote that the Main Roads and Buildings Committee were prepared to treat the main roads with calcium chloride or other dust laying material if the Urban, Rural and Parish Councils, parish meetings or other persons interested along the route of such roads contributed one-third of the cost of providing and laying down such dust laying material. 4 .</p>
        <p>, Mr. Linaker, jun., prop,)sed a resolution that the matter be reported to all Parish Councils. With regard to himself, he had the mixture placed upon his roads, and found it was very beneficial to them. He had only been charged two-thirds of the actual cost Mr. Antwis wanted to know if it kept the roads from wearing. Mr. Linaker : The County Council say "Yea." Con- tinuing he stated that Frodsham and Helshy were making an effort to have their roads dressed. it pre- served the roads.</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter said be thought that if the roads were dressed with the mixture it would considerably save the expense of watering the roads. Mr. Linaker, jun., said his roads had only needed dressing three times during lest year. It only cost about 0.0 per mile. Mr. Mawdsley said in his opinion the dressing would considerably wear the roads. After a showF•r of rain the roads were like puddles. They could not be bi.,shed for the dressing would come off. lie thought it harmed the roads.</p>
        <p>It was decided to refer the matter to the Highways Executive Committee.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED OFFENSI VE DITCH.</p>
        <p>The Nuisance Inspector reported that he had examined a ditch which bad formed the subject of a complaint by Mr. W. Sloane. The Manchester Sbip Canal Company had cleaned out the ditch end the nuisance no longer existed. Mr. Woodward said that the ditch had been examined and no nuisance was in existence. On looking round they found another ditch coming from Mr‘Sloane's own land was the cause of the nuisance. (Laughter.) The ditch gave off a very offensive smell and it went right under Mr. Sloane's window. _</p>
        <p>It was decided that thc Inspector report further upon he matter.</p>
        <p>ROADWAY STOPPED.</p>
        <p>The Clerk produced Order of County Quarter Sessions authorising the closing of a roadway near Walton Arches.</p>
        <p>LETTER BOXES.</p>
        <p>The Surveyor, G.P.O. Liverpool, asked permission to fix a letter box to the sign post opposite Yew Tree Farm, Norton, and the Postmaster, Warrington, made a similar request in respect of Ellesmere-road (corner of Roman-roal)Stockton fleath.The application was gran+ id with reference to Norton and with regard to the Stockton Heath application it WAS decided to refer it to the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>iFECTIOUS DISEASES.</head>
        <p>iFECTIOUS DISEASES.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported the following cases of infections diset!ses :—Diphtheria, -Grappenhall, 1 ; scarlet fever, Froosham, 1 ; Stockton Heath, 1 ; 4 cases of measles at Norley School.</p>
      </div>
