      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>Protection and the Boot Trade. -Sir—Tariff Reformers are singularly unfortunate in their choice of industries to illustrate and enforce their arguments. The boot trade has for some time been one of the favourites, and such papers as the "Daily Mail," "Daily Express," and the like, have painted dismal pictures of foreign made lvots dumped into this country shutting the doors ofillsaany of our boot factories, &amp;c. The fact is that the imports of boots and shoes into this country only reached at their highest about 2 per cent of our home manufactures and these imports have been steadily going down for the last five or six years. Instead of free imports • having been a danger to the boot trade they have been its salvation. In the first place we have had free importation of the best American machinery, the McKay and Blake machines have been an immense benefit to the trade effecting a large reduction in the price of footwear to the advantage of everyone who wears boots, and enabling us to considerably increase our exports. The Tariff Reform papers conveyed the impression that unless we had Protection American, boots would very soon cripple our home boot industry, and this absurdity secured a good deal of popular -credence. The truth however is that for the last six years the imports of American boots into this country • have steadily gone down. Why was it that the com- paratively small imports of American boots ever gained a foothold in this country? The reason for this is very simple. The American last is a different shape to the English and for some shapes of feet boots made on American lasts were moze comfortable. The effect of this free importation was that it forced upon the attention of our home manufacturers the fact that there was a demand for this shape and style of boot. They began to import and copy American lasts and American styles of boots, and to-clay we have boots made in Northampton which are sold in all our large centres under thename of an American Company, but what is in point of fact an English Company selling "American "boots made in Northampton. Suppose that six years ago the legislature had listened to the hubbub of the Tariff Reform papers, and put a duty on American machines and American boots; what would have been the result? We should have all the boots used by our people from Gd. to is. per pair . dearer than they* are • to-day, and remember every increase of price involves diminished general con- sumption and. production! We should have those who find the American style of boot more comfortable obliged to wear boots that do not fit, and we hould have our export trade curtailed as the result of Pro- tection, putting up costs of leather, machines, lasts, &amp;c, No better illustration can be given than the history of the boot trade during the lfist few years of the results of the two systems. Free trade stimulates industrial improvement ; protected industries move in leaden feet. Free Trade cries" Advance." Protec- tion says : " Build a wall around, us that we may stand still." Free Trade says to the world, give us of your best, if we can copy it we ivill, if not we will ' exchange our products for yours. , Let other countries build their tariff walls as high as the clouds; as long as we are free buyers other countries must take our , products in exchange.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh May 25th. 1909</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ROLLER SKATING.</head>
        <p>ROLLER SKATING.</p>
        <p>Opening of a New Rink at Warrington. The passion for roller skating which has recently spread,all over the United Kingdom, the Continent, and the United States carried Warrington its train and led to a rink being provided by an enter- -wising company (the Palatine Rinks, Limited) a few months ago, in this town. Warringtonians set themselves with zest to the rt of roller skating and hundreds early became proficient. Their patronage was soon too extensive for the accommodation in Artingstall's Buildings, and the demand for more commodious and suitable premises has resulted in a fine rink being erected by the same company on Wilderspool Causeway. The rink, which was opened yesterday, measures 185 feet by 65 feet, and has cost about £2,000 to be built and equipped ready for use. 'The builders werb Messrs. W. and A. Ashton, and the architect Mr. C. Barnish. The floor is of maple. An unusual feature is that the orchestra is placed on a platform. which juts out from the wall, and thus leaves the whole of the floor available for the skaters. Mr. Barnshaw is the leader of the 'orchestra, and his subordinates are quite efficient in providing the music desired. For those who prefer the part of onlookers there is a gallery provided at a charge of 3d. and by this excellent arrangement they are able to watch the skating with as much interest and comfort as the spectator at a circus. All the requirements for the comfort of the skaters are attended to, there being ladies' and gents' cloak- rooms and retiring rooms. Refreshments and after- noon tea are also prepared and the arrangement of everything is on up-to-date lines. The rink was opened by the Mayor (Alderman W. Forshaw, J.P.), yesterday afternoon in the presence of a large assembly. Supporting him were the Mayoress and Mrs. Smethurst, and Messrs. Alderman Bennett, B. Silcock, H. Ashton and J. Neill. Mr. H. ASHTON, before placing the opening in the hands of the Mayor, said they bad started roller skating in a small way a short time ago, and now they had got a bigger rink. They had givpn the people a proper place in which to enjoy the pastime of roller skating (Applause). The MAYOR said they would all agree with him when he said that skating was a very pleasant and valuable exercise for the people and he thought they would agree with him too when he said that the owners of the concern had provided a very beautiful and spacious room in which that exercise could be enjoyed. (Applause.) He wished the concern every possible success and he hoped it would be free from accident. (Hear, bear.) He had much pleasure in declaring the rink open. (Applause). At this stage Miss H. ASHTON, a little girl of tender years, presented a bouquet to the Mayoress, which was graciously acknowledged. Mr. H. ASHTON then proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor and Mayoress, and alluded to the increasing popularity and healthiness of the skating pastime. The management were anxious to do all in their power to please and he believed they had one of the best equipped rtalts in the country. The speaker concluded with an allusion to the King's visit to Warrington, and expressed the hope that His Majesty would confer some signal honour upon the Mayor—a sentiment which was received with loud applause. Alderman BENNErr seconded the vote and testified to the enthusiastic work of the Mayor and Mayoress. He too, was delighted with all he had seen of roller sating. Mr. B. SILCOCK, secretary of the Palatine Rinks Co., Ltd. supported the motion, which was carried with the Ltd., heartiness. Mr. Charles Hassan, the manager of the new rink, the attendants, and others then gave an exhibition of their dexterity on the rollers, Mr. Hassall's clever feats receiving special and warm recognition. The rink was then thrown open to the guests, and from this point till the hour for closing, the whirr of the little wheels was unceasing, and a large company -vastly enjoyed themselves. The orchestra did excellent service, and the conclusion was unavoidable that music is as essential to rinking as it is to dsncing. ANOTIIER NEW RINK. - -</p>
        <p>The Empire Skating Rinks (Warrington), Ltd., announce that the magnificent permanent building now in course of construction in Museum-street, will open for the skatine season at the end nf August. The rink will have a skating area of 26,000 square feet and a patent noiseless floor, and there will be a cafe, lounger ladies' retiring rooms. and gents smoke room.</p>
        <p>BERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the New Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable is rtrongly recommended.—WHlTE &amp; WRIGHT, Su • rgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw at. upposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. R. ALDRIDGE then read the report which was of greatlengtb. We give the following extracts At the outset of the report a special note says : This report must not be regarded as in any way com- plete. It comprises (1) the result of a series of careful investigations made by an officer of the National Housing Reform Council into the actual housing condi- tions of Warrington, and (2) a series of practical suggestions as to future housing reform action. It is hoped that the publication of this report will help to secure for the Health Committee of the Borough Council the earnest and strong support of the citizens of Warrington in their work of housing betterment. In the report for the year 1907. prepared by the able Medical Officer of Health for the Borough of Warrington _ _</p>
        <p>(Dr. J. Coote Hibbert), the record Of an extremely important investigation of upwards of 400 slum houses is given. This record bears witness to two things (I) the courage of the members of the Health Committee , and their able officials in their desire to know clearly what is wrong, and (2) the fact that a public duty of the most urgent kind devolves on all tbe citizens of Warrington. Two points in the report call for special emphasis, viz :—(a) the existence in alma areas of a Death Rate of 53 per 1,000, and (b) an Infantile Mortality Rate of 263 per 1,000. The figures for the whole Borough, viz :—Death Rate 15.7 per 1000, and Infant Mortality Rate 122 per 1,000, include the figures for these slum houses, and therefore it will hardly be too much to say that the Death Rate in those squalid houses is nearly four times as great as in other houses and that for every two children dying in the ordiniry homes of Warrington five die in these "survivals of unfit" housing conditions.</p>
      </div>
