      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>, . ,':' ',N,,,,,,,_ e N ' k , , * 1 . ' '' t . • • , I ...• . , / , . . ' . . . , . "'.. .... .... ~ ' . '.. ''' f .. . . ' .</head>
        <p>, . ,':' ',N,,,,,,,_ e N ' k , , * 1 . ' '' t . • • , I ...• . , / , . . ' . . . , . "'.. .... .... ~ ' . '.. ''' f .. . . ' .</p>
        <p>HOUSING REFORM.</p>
        <p>STRIKING REPORT ON THE CONDITIONS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Our Slum Areas.</p>
        <p>There was a large and representative attendance at an important conference held in the Town Hall, War- rington, on Thursday evening to consider the Housing conditions of the Borough. A strikinc, report embody- ing the results of a series of. careful investigations made by an official of the National Housing Reform Council into the actual housing conditions nf Warrington was placed before the meeting and an interesting dis- cussion took place. Alderman Bennett presided and many leading townspeople were present. Mr. Henry R. Aldridge (secretary), and Mr. Harold Shawcross C. C. (Rochdale) were present on behalf of the National Housing Reform Council. It was announced that letters of apology for non- attendanceirad been received from Mr. A. H. Crosfield. M.P., Canon Stevenson, Mrs. Crosfield, Miss Truda Crosfield, Mrs. Garnett, and Mr. Henry Hope. Mrs. Garnett, Hall Nook„ Penketh, wrote :—" We have strayed so far from the Eden in which it pleased the Creator to place our first parents, that we have almost forgotten if not despised our birthrights and, mistaking the means for the end, have lost ourselves in the dreary occupation of money-making and the creation of a thousand artificial wants, the gratifitation of which only creates an appetite for more. Hence the growing longing which we see for the fresh smell of the e.trth— to plant a seed, to grow a blade of grass, a flower, ,a tree. 'thank God I trust we have done with felling them, to make room for desolate rows of houses devoid of one preen leaf. Some of us will be copying Sir Francis Drake (was it?) who walked about dropping acorns into the ground in the great faith that future generations might sit under their shadow, but mostly, I fancy, for "the hearts of oak" of which he feared there might be a scarcity in building Dreadnoughts. It is certain that we are not getting one quarter of the • fruitfulness or sweetness out of the land which we might do. We are ignoring the illimitable opportunies which God—so to speak—has squandered upon us and prefer barrenness to plenty. How I sigh for the time when every man shill sit under his own vine and fig tree • —i.e. apple and pear tree—none daring to make him afraid. I bope you you will have a good gathering and some practical results." SOME PHASES OF THE PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>The CHAmm AS said that for many years he had had a horrible sense of disgust when he had thought of the housing conditions of Warrington, but until he accom- panied the Inspector of the National Housing Reform Council (Mr. Brealey) on his recent visit he had not adequately realized the horror of the conditions. They visited some of the worst slums and courts in Warring- ton and it made one heart sick and ashamed. The report gave only a few typical cases out of many, but he was sure they would be enough to make them realize what a great work lay before them to improve - the conditions and make life a little more decent and civilized in our large towns. The great problem was how the labourer receiving 18s. a week was to be well housed. Some of the houses in the town were not fit for pigs to live in—in fact he had seen pigs much better housed. In many areas there were as many as forty houses to the acre, which was simply monstrous, and one of the most urgently needed reforms was an Act of Parliament by which it would be illegal to erect more than a certain number of houses per acre. Amongst the measures of amelioration sug- gested in the report was the rapid clearance of existing unfit houses. The Health Committee were no doubt doing their best, but the existing law was not strong enough. He hoped with all his heart that the clauses on this matter in the Town Planning Bill would become law during the present session, and that fortified with those additional powers,the Health Committee would be able to carry On the work of demolition more rapidly. Referring to the garden suburbs, the Chairman said he must say with sorrow and shame that the workingmen of Warrington had not risen to their opportunity and had not supported the subsidiary Co-operative Tenants' movement to anything like the extent it had been supported in other places. He trusted that a repetition of the atrocities we saw in practically every town in Lancashire would be rendered impossible when the Town Planning Bill became law. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>A DESCRIPTION OF SOME OF THE WORST AREAS.</p>
        <p>In the older and central wards of the Borough tiiere are several narrow streets and back courts and alleys containing insanitary dwellings. During recent years many of these insanitary dwellings have been closed— in some instances by the Health Committee, but chiefly for the purpose of street wideni Despite these clearances there are in Warrington a large number of slum dwellings calling for vigorous civic action. These slum dwellings are of a bad type being shut away in narrow passages and enclosed courts. The courts of Warrington, of which there are upwards of 100, are in a specially bad condition. The people who dwell in these courts are of the poorer working class, mostly labourers. Apart from the fact that many of the houses in these courts are in a dirty, damp and dilapitated condition the surfaces of the courts and</p>
        <p>yards are in a b;ci";i:itie7-In--n-ti.Mer-ou-s-in;t;n-c-e;,-iiii;Wi in the majority of cases, these courts are not paved throughout. The drainage for the carrying off of sur- face water and slop water in a great number of instances is not sufficient. Several visits were made by our investigator to these courts, and it was very seldom that they were found to he free from stagnant slop water. Either the fall to the drain was not sufficient, or tiles were broken or displaced, or the courts not sufficiently paved to allow the surface water and slop water to get away. In many instances all these defects were to be found in the same court. The majority of courts were strewn with refuse and filth. These courts are entered by narrow passages and are unlighted. The pail closets and refuse middens for the surrounding houses are invariably placed in these courts. A large number of the refuse middens are withput doors. The result is refuse and garbage is scattered about. The pails from the closets , are collected during the night, and whether it is owing to the pails not being placed in a correct position or whether it is owing to the pails leaking it is difficult to say, but the fact remains that in a number of instances liquid excreta is to be found either in pools or flowing down the courts and back passages. PARTICULARS OF A FE* SLUM HOUSES VISITED. _</p>
        <p>Eight cases are partictflarised in different Parts of the town, the details being largely similar to the following. No. 1.. house in X. Yard.—Yard approached by narrow passage from main thoroughfare. Four roomed house. No door behind but a small window can be opened. The house is in a bad condition, the plaster and paper rotting from the walls and dirty. Size of living room and front bedroom: Height, 6ft. Bin. ; length, Mt.: depth, lift. Bin. The scullery is an awful place, damp, the brick floor broken up and the damp soil giving out bad stench, dirty and dark. The small scullery window opens out into another yard and is about 2.yards from 5 pail closets. The passage between windows and closets is covered with refuse and filth.</p>
        <p>• The bedrooms are in a--I;ria—coUdition- being damp, dirty and in bad repair. Back bedroom not used. A. small slopstone with water tap is placed betweon door and window. A cupboard for food is placed in corner near the fireplace. No food can be safely kept in scullery—only fit to store coal in. The houses have to be kept up by iron stays. The closets and middens are at the end of yard about 10 yards away—two closets (pail) for three houses. Closets fairly clean. Tenants, man, wife and one young boy. Rent, is. 9d. per week clear. No 8, house in G. Yard.—Two-roomed back to back house. The house is-damp and in bad repair. The roof of living room is in a bad condition, and the floor of same room is black with the moisture rising up. The bare laths are showing in the bedroom. The house faces west, and directly opposite, eight yards away, are the backs of the houses which shut out the yard from the main street. Four pail closets and one refuse midden are directly opposite this house and only two yards away. Size of room: Height, Bft. fin.; length, Oft. 3m.; depth, lift. 3in. Rent: 2s. Gd. per week clear. These courts and houses are the heritage from days when little attention was given to housing and health matters. As the town has grown these areas have been shut in and rendered even more unhealthy. Another bad feature of these houses is the very small windows. A large number of these houses have windows consider- ably less than 3 feet square, including sash.</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS AND ST. JOHN'S.</p>
        <p>These two Wards are taken as typical examples of the best and worst of working class centres in the town,</p>
        <p>There are in Warringon large numbers of skilled artisans whose wages are sufficient to enable them to pay the rent for a well built comfortable cottage. Whilst it may be said that this need is being met by the erection of cottages in accordance with the bye- laws, it. may be poirited out that in one vital essential there is absolute need for the adoption of a vigorous housing policy by the Warrington Borough Council. " From the point of view of the large landowner." a point was made, "the policy of crowding a large num- ber of cottages on a:small area of land is a short-sighted policy. The value of the SAME) area actually covered is, of course, greatly enhanced, but the land not covered is rented at a nominal agricultural value. If you spread your values you get more than when you crowd a particular piece of land. The best method of estate development is that of encouraging the migra- tion to the suburbs by the provision of large gardens and recreation grounds, of careful planning of roads, of arranging wider frontages—in other words, estate development on garden suburb lines." It was hoped that directly the new Town Planning Bill passed into law the Borough Council would appoint. a committee thoroughly to consider the question, of suburb planning. The stimulation of the supply of what might be called model cottages should also be undertaken by the Borough Council. The Garden Suburb Association would, it was anticipated, perform a real public service in this relation. Two estates had already been secured, the .Great Sankey estate and the Grappenhall estate. The migration of skilled artisans to the houses in these suburbs should lesson the pressure on the older accommodation at the centre, and much of this should become available for the labourer. The principal housing difficulty, so far as the labourer was concerned, was without doubt that of low wages, and until wages rose to a proper level the burden of public health administration, and the extent of hospital and Poor Law relief must of necessity be greater for this poorest class than for the skilled artisan class. The general community would be compelled to pay heavily in an indirect way for the lack of better economic con- ditions for this class, and at the same time the efficiency of the labourer must suffer. A movement, therefore, for the improvement both of the wages and efficiency of the labourer should be welcomed by all classes of the community. A four-fold policy was recommended by the writers of the report. The rapid clearance of unfit houses and the thorough repair of houses which could be made reasonably fit was the first suggestion, the others being the arrangement of the migration of the skilled artisan to the suburbs by the provision of means of rapid transit and better suburb planning, the gradual moving up of the tenants of the poorest houses into the next best ones thus rendered vacant, and the erection of dwellings on the same lines as those followed by the Liverpool City Council, but let at rents which should I not involve a loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday May 29, 1909.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday May 29, 1909.</p>
        <p>and comparative statistics given. In 1907 the general death rate in Whitecross was 120 per 1,000 and the infantile death rate 66 per 1,000 births. In St. Johns the general death rate was 19.3 per 1,000 and the infantile death rate 165 per 1,000 births. Speaking of the latter figures the report adds It would seem hopeless to expect a rapid aivance in the health and vigour of children so long as they are born in the houses above described and under the eon- _</p>
        <p>-ilitron-s which prevailed. The foregoing facts and figures will place beyond question the need for careful attention to the housing problem of the labouring section of the community. There is, however, great need for attention to the condition of the houses in which an even poorer class of men are housed, viz., the common lodging-house. There are 33 common lodging- houses within the borough registered to accommodate 685 persons. Unfortunately a number of small houses, many of them ,originally two-roomed back to back houses, have been in addition registered as. common lodging-houses. It is this class of holiee which leaves a great deal to be desirei. One notices that stagnant air giving out a pungent odour. The conveniences for purposes of cleanliness are vary meagre. Whilst the larger houses are for Men, most of the smaller ones are for married couples or single persons. Avery small slopstone sink has to suffice for the purposes of personal cleanliness. This sink in the smaller houses is generally in the public kitchen. Small houses such as many of these are ought not to be registered as common lodging-houses, THE HOUSING OF THE ARTIZAN. As far as the cheapness of houses is concerned it may be at once stated that Warrington will compare favour- ably with many of the other great towns in the United Kingdom. Our investigator gained particulars of the rents actually paid in no less than 67 di fferenc streets, and his report shows that these rents, which are clear rents, vary as follows :-4-roomed house, 4s. 2d. to ss. 6d. per week (4s. 6d. to 4s. Bd. being the average) ; 5-roomed house,, 43. 6d. to ss. 6d. per week (ss. tieing the average) ; 6-roomed house, ss. 3d. to Bs. 3d. per week. In July 1907,there were 13,672 occupied houses. In July 1907 there were 379 occupied for business purposes. In July 1907 there were 418 unoccupied houses. in July 1907 there were 10 unoccupied houses which were used for business purposes. Total 14,188. The number of empty houses is thus less than 3 per cent. Our investi- gator states I am convinced that. the high Infantile Mortality Rates and the Zymotic Death Rates are to a great extent owing to the condition and kind of sanitary conveniences provided and to the condition of the numerous courts, back passages and back streets. He adds:—" I have no hesitation in saying that Warring- ton in its poorer and consequently older districts, is very badly paved and drained. Many of these courts and back passages which are supposed to he paved are only about half paved. It is a common thing to find a yard paved only in part. One part either with tiles or round cobbles, and the other part not paved at all." According to the latest returns there are 022 pail closets, 694 water closets,and 6 privy closets in the borough. It is generally agreed that if there were a general' adoption of the water clrriage system to a considerable degree it would moan a complete fresh sewering and drainage of the town. The major portion of the present drainage was laid about 60 years ago, and would not 17:e adequate to cope with any general adoption of the water carriage system. The question of dealing with this nroblem adequateh is one which must without doubt be fully considered before long by the Warrington Town Council.</p>
        <p>the public did not demand. Let the public demand the abolition of the bad and diseased property in the town and it wonld be carried out. Mr. C. E. PARKER said there was no doubt there had been very great practical difficulties in the way of deal- ing with slum preperty, but some of them would be done away with by the simplification in the Town Planning Bill, and there was no doubt that the authori- ties would have much greater opportunities of dealing with the question. Mr. Cook had referred to the officials and the Health Committee as just doing what the town demanded, hut he (the speaker) thought thy ought to look to the Health Committee to give a lead in the matter. They were in close contact with the conditions which had been described that night and they ought not to wait for the people of Warrington to push them but should lead on their own initiative. He did not like to say anything about the authorities of the Borough because they gave their time, strength and energy so freely and willingly to the welfare of the community, but he had always thought that the War- rington Health Committee was a most wesk body— (laughter)—and in regard to the smoke of the borough they were absolutely indifferent. Of course, there were some departments they attended to very well, but the townspeople looked to the committee to give them a lead and not wait to be pushed forward by anything that was said at public meetides. He hoped the meet- ing would create a great deal of interest in the subject.. The Rev. T. 'MILLER, referring to the question of paving, said he spent two days in going round the whole of the slums of Warrington and the state of things was disgusting. He had taken an Interest in the subject in other towns where he had lived, but he had never known a town where the court yards were so badly paved. Surely the Town Council could do some- thing to improve that and it would stir the owner of slum property up if he had to meet half the expense of paving. The court, yards were so fearfully neglected as to be a discredit to any committee that had the paving and sewering of the town in hand. He thought a housing reform committee could be formed to work with the Beautiful Warrington Society. The CHAIRMAN, in replying to Mr. Furness, said the houses which had been built at Great Senkey were not the last word of the Garden Suburbs Company; they were only the first. If they got the support of the working classes they intended to supply houses of every type. On the motion of the CHAIRMAN, a vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Aldridge, Mr. Shawcross and the officials of the Housing Reform Council for their ser- vices.</p>
        <p>Councillor 3ANIES EVANS (chairman of the Health Committee) said it was euy for gentlemen to stand outside and make remarks of a general character, but it was another matter to face the music and deal with the question. The Health Committee was doing its work slowly, because it had no other alternative. If some of those present could 'ilsuggest methods of to expediting the work, it would be very usefol. During the last year 52 houses had been condemned ; 36 of those had been closei, and 16 of them were undergoing repairs in order to put 'them in what the Health Committee could for the time being look upon as a habitable condition. Improvement was proceeding slowly but he thought that by and by there would be a different condition of doines. Referring to infantile mortality, Councillor Evans said there were other causes besides insanitary conditions, which concerned the mothers themselves. He con- tended that the Health Committee by their worK were slowly but surely bringing to an end the slums in Warrington. - Mr. C. PLINSTON said they did not want explanations of why things were in their present condition. They were there to see if they could not bring influence to bear that would hasten the amelioration of existing conditions. The CHAIRMAN asked for the feeling of the meeting as to the appointment of a Housing Reform Committee. Mr. PARKER : I should appoint the Health Com- mittee. (Laughter.) Councillor EvANs : With power to add to their number?</p>
        <p>Mr. PARKER : If you like. Ultimately it wa3 agreed to leave the matter in abey- ance, as it was considered that the Town Planning Bill would probably revolutionise the whole position. Mr. C. PLINSTON : Of course, you will guarantee that the Bill passes? The CHAIRMAN : I think it will. A vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman on the motion of Mr. A. J. JOLLEY seconded by Mr. W. FURNESS.</p>
        <p>and painful and very often heartbreaking experience which a man went through in tramping ahout the country in search of work—very likely in the wrong direction. The scheme of Labour Exchanges, which' would be provided for not out of the rates but out of the Imperial Exchequer, was surely one of the very best practical pieces of social legislation which the Govern- ment could possibly tackle. Next year they would have a scheme of insurance—insurance ag 'hut p'tysioal breakdown, invalidity, and all the risks of life that men were subjected to before they reached the pension ag-.. Of course, to put the keystone to the arch of social reform, they would also have an attempt to deal with what was perhaps the most difficult and the stddest part of tbe whole problem—the case of the unemployable, the wastrel, the drunkard, the hopelessly inefficient—under a revised scheme of Poor Law adminietration. When one took the work that had been done by the Government during the past three years, and that great democratic advance in the matter of financial reform—the Budget—and also considered the social programme that lay hefore them, it was a really great undertaking for one Government to carry through. They heard prophecies front the other side that tariff reform was going to sweep the decks at the next election, but with such</p>
        <p>REAL HONEST PROGRESSIVE WOR being carried through, and with Free Trade to defend as well, ho told them frankly that he did not believe it. , (Applause). He believed the Government would go back with a swinging majority next time. (Applause.) Tariff reformers were trying to make capital out of the question of unemployment and he would like to deal with one or two obvious and absolute mis-statements. Firh of all there was the statement made by people who were supposed to be in authority on the front Opposi- tion Bench and to have influence in the Tory party, like Mr. Wyndham and • Mr. Chaplin, who went about saying that (Mies could be put on without increasing the cost of materials, articles or food. If that were so, if they could put a duty on corn without raising the price, the agricultural interest of the country would not care twopence about it. They only wanted the duty imposed because it wonld raise the price and the Canadian. farmer only wanted the preference because he was going to get a somewhat better price for his corn in our market, so that on the face of it, it was absolute nonsense to pretend that a duty would not raise the price.(Aroplause.) They knew it would, because they had proof in every single market without exception where the duties were put on. Free Traders based their case on the obvious fact that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>SUG GESTIONS AND RECOMMENDATIONS,</head>
        <p>SUG GESTIONS AND RECOMMENDATIONS,</p>
        <p>THE DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD SHAVVCROSS then addressed the gather- ing. He said there was nothing in the report that need reflect upon Warrington more than upon any other town in Lancashire. Year by year the birth rate was decreasing amongst the better classes—not only the upper classes but all the better working classes were having fewer and fewer children—but in the slums the birth rate kept U. It these conditions continued we we should find in two or three generations that half our population came out of the slums. Mr. Shawcross called particular attention tb the refer- ence in the report to bad paving of yards and courts which he said was 3 very serious thing, especially in the old courts where they could not get proper drainage. He acknowledged that there would be very great difficulties in dealing with the drainage systeni of Warrington, because it was a low lying place, and con- sequently to go in for the water carriage system would be a large undertaking but he would urge the citizens to press that point as much as they could. Mr. Shawcross also referred to the question of the rates— "those terrible rates which were respOnsible for so much misery and illness." What they wanted to see, he continued, was the formation in every town of a Housing Reform Council which need not work in antagonism to the Town Council but which might be a very great deal to help forward a committee Or a medical officer health who was anxious to do a good work. (Applause.) Mr. HE.,TRY ROBERTS said what he should very much like to know was : What was the practical issue to be got from the information which had been conveyed to the meeting? He was not an apologist for the Town Council. He was an old member of the Council, and for eighteen long years he was a member of the Health Committee, and if any man knew what it was to be abso- lutely almost broken-hearted at the slow progress that 'was possible to be made in carrying out the ideas suggested in the report, he was that man. After outlining the unsatisfactory methods available for dealing with slum property, Mr. RoesnTs said there was one grain of comfort to be derived from the paper. Everything suggested which was of a practical nature he would be willing to support an attempt to carry out, but what gratefully surprised him was-that in a population of nearly 72,000 there were not more than 1,300 people living in slums. The Town Planning Bill would not do everything they wanted with regard to the removal of congested areas inside the town. He would be glad if they could, by stirring up public sentiment on the question, give a certain additional support to the intention and policy of the Health Committee by making it possible for them to discard all personal, petty and financial interests and to insist upon it that if there was a single house existing in Warrington which merited the description they had listened to, it should not continue to exist one raw:lent longer than the law would permit. They must not deal with platitudes but with practical issues in a practical way. He was not a man who bad Sympathy with the ratepayer in the ordinary way and he would far rather see an association of the character suggested by the speakers in existence in Warrington and doing a good work than a ratepayers' association, so called, any time. He knew pretty well what a ratepayers' associa- tion meant; it meant as a rule "Keep the rates down no matter whatever else you do?! Mr. W. FURNESS thought the Town Council was largely responsible for the conditions in St. John's Ward. The Town Council as a whole were not doing their duty in reglrd to the health and recreation of the children. St. John's Ward, as far as its sewage arrange- ments were concerned, was one of the worst in the town. HO contended that the class of dwelling which was at present being provided by the Garden Suburbs Company was only suitable for men getting .£2OO a year, and therefore the scheme would not relieve the congested conditions in the town. The movement would be sailing under false colours unless it provided houses for the artisans and the workingmen. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK said it had been quite a intoxication to him to hear such a splendid outburst of civic enthusiasm as had just proceeded from Mr. Roberts. He would like to know whether the munici- pality did all it might do in the improvement of its own property. The Council represented the ptrblic and it was not right to abuse the Council for not doing what</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Crosfield Presents Billiard Prizes.</p>
        <p>THE MEMBER ON THE POLITICAL . SITUATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. and Mrs. Crosfield visited the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening, the occasion being the distribution, of the prizes gained in the two successful billiard handicaps held during the winter. There was a large gathering in the reading room and the Member and his wife were accorded an enthusiastic greeting. Alderman Tinnion presided and amongst others present were Alderman Burton, Councillors Wilkinson, Locker and Halliwell, Messrs. C. E. Parker, T. Locker, W. Bibby, J. Worrall, J. Wilkinson, E. England, F. Lindley, J. Howes, J. Howes. jun., and T. Pemberton (hon. secretary). Mrs. Crostield handed the prizes to the successful competitors as follows :—First handicap ; 1, gold watch,E.Wilson ;2, flower vases,J. Howes,jun; 3,cutlery, H. Eyes ; 4, dressing bag, W. Langley ; 5, striking clock, F'. Mullery ; 6, cruet, R. W. Wright ; 7, carvers, J. J. Pritnha,rd ; 8, biscuit birrel, W.Bibuy ; 0, cigarette case, J. Gorst ; 10, match box, J. Leah; 11, breakfast cruet, H. Shaw. Second handicap: 1, pair bronzes, J. Perry; 2, dressing bag, R. Wilson ; 3, egg stand, J. E. Balmford ; 4, rose bowl, J. J. Pritchard; 5, carvers, J. Howes,juo. ; 6, match box,J.Gorst ; 7,photo frame W. Langley ; 8, photo frame, C. Russell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>VOTE OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOTE OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>• Mr. C. E. PARKER in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Cro.field for presenting the prizes and to Mr. Crosfield for having provided them, said they were all getting very fond of Mrs. Crosfield and she was a very great asset to them in Warrington. (Applause.) It did them good to see her smile, and, they were greatly obliged to her for having Conn from London to make the presentations, which had been performed with the grace they always expected from her. The Club was under continual obligttion to Mr. Crosfield for so generously and kindly providing the wherewithal for those prizes every year. Toe prizes were very appropriate and would be treasured by the recipients for the kindly thoughts they would always suggest in connection with the donor. Mr.WORRA.LL seconded and said that as a representa- tive of the Club he knew how much they were indebted to Mr. Crosfield for the interest he took in its welfare. That was the second time they had had the pleasure of Mrs. Crosfield's presence on those semi-official occ tsions. When she first came amongst them the created one of the best impressions that possibly could have been created and she had carried out her duties that might in an equally appreciable manner. Mr. Crosfield no doubt knew full well the deep affection and sincere regard which every member of the Club entertained for him. (Applause). The CHAIRMAN, in supporting, said that whatever impression Mrs. Crosfield might have made on the male members of the party it was nothing compared with the respect, affection and esteem in which she was held by the ladies. (Applause). He (the speaker) had had something to do with the management of the Club under Mr, C, osfield's presidency, arid he knew that they owed almost everything to the splendid way in which their President had acted on every occasion. He had helped them not only with his purse but with encourage- ment and advice, and he had discoursed to them on political subjects with great acceptance and benefit. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECa.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S SPEECa.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, M.P., who Was warmly applauded on rising to respond, said he always found a great deal of pleasure in those informal gatherings when they met to celebrate the victories that had been won on the bowling green or in their handsome billiard room where they often had a little music as well as a little frank, political talking—and he always thought that was a happy combination on such occasions. (Hear, hear.) To tell them the truth, he was under the impression that his wife would be making a speech to them that evening—(laughter and hear, hear,)— because he believed that when he last visited the club he promised that next time they should hear her, for a little bit of a change. Therefore, he was taken somewhat unawares, and he confessed he found it difficult to mike anything in the way of a speech at the present time, not for lack of anything to talk about, but because there was such an immense amount of, material. He was glad to say that he bad secured a definite promise for the autumn from the Lord Advocate, Mr. Ure. (Applause). Mr. Parker and he (the speaker) had also been in touch with Col. Seeley, and they hoped also to be favoured with a visit from him. (Applause.) With regard to the great work which the Government had in band, he believed every- one would agree that THE BUDGET</p>
        <p>in itself was a tremendous undertaking and, subject possibly to modification here and there in the Com- mittee stage, he unhesitatingly statei that it was one of the greatest pieces of democratic work we had ever seen in this country—(hear, hear)—and he was certain it would be appreciated and valued by the democracy of Britain. The Budget, however, was not the only matter which was occupying attention. They were now deal- ing, not in its entirety, but they were making a very important start with the question of sweated industries. He was that morning on the Committee in London which was inquiring into that complicated and difficult subject. Then they had the really splendid scheme of LABOUR. EXCHANGES.</p>
        <p>which, broadly speaking, bad for its object what John Burns was so fond of describing as the" decasualising" of labour. They wanted to get rid, as far as p3ssible, of casual labour and partial employment, and where the employer was looking for a few bands and so many workmen were looking for jobs, they wanted to bring them in touch with each other without all the costly</p>
        <p>without thinking of the cotton industry of Lancashire, because they knew what an immense proportion of our cotton goods went abroad. He did not doubt that a considerable part of that trade was held by very substantial margins, and there waa no doubt the cotton trade had been a profit- able one for years past. But much of the trade was held by very narrow margins. (Alderman TINNION : "Losses to-day.") He knew it was cut very fine and if in certain .foreign markets, more particularly where we came into competition with America, we got a reduc- tion in the cotton trade and lost 10, 15 or 20 per cent. of our exports in that industry, we would indeed know something of what was one of the greatest curses of modern industry—unemployment. That was a matter I of the gravest national importance, because with diminished output, increased standing charges and higher cost of production, they would have the employer going to the workman and saying "If lam going to keep my trade.l really must make some reduc- tion in your wages." On the other hand the employees would be saying that the cost of living had increased to such an extent that they must have an advance in wages to meet it. Therefore, if</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>DUTIES WOULD RAISE PRICES.</head>
        <p>DUTIES WOULD RAISE PRICES.</p>
        <p>They knew that Mr. Chamberlain's scheme of a 10 per cent. duty all round on manufactured article:. would raise the cpst of agricultural implements and of clothing, boots and the little luxuries enjoyed by the agricultural classes, and even a public spirited man like the late Duke of Devonshire, who would not go in for Protection in order to raise his rent roll, would not agree to agriculture being left out if we had a scheme of Protection like Mr. Chamberlain had proposed. Therefore agriculture was included and the foodstuffs would be advanced in cost. While that con- cerned the country as a whole, it certainly concerned Lancashire in a very special degree and he would simply submit a few facts which ward not answered by the Conservatives when they held their meetings, and wllich could not be answered. The Free Trade issue had a direct bearing on the question of unemployment, but a precisely opposite bearing to what tariff reformers, honestly he dare say, imagined it to have. The cost of everything being increased, they at once reduced the purchasing power of the sovereign and even a comparatively moderate dose of Protection would reduce it to 17s. Gd. That would mean a tremendous falling off in the demand in the home market, which had been reasonably estimated at something like six times the size of our export trade, and consequent lessened production. He never thougtrt of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EXPORT TRADE</head>
        <p>EXPORT TRADE</p>
        <p>THIS PREPOSTEROUS PLAN</p>
        <p>for reversing our fiscal system and introducing that played•out policy of Protection ever succeeded, besides the loss of prosperity, comfort and necessaries amongst the people of this country, we would be faced with the gravest danger of intern ttional strife. Tariff Reformers accused Liberals of doing nothing to deal with the question. but on the contrary he (Mr. Oros&amp;1d) main- tained that they were doing a great deal. Surely, instead of this bogus programme of Protection, any workingman would prefer what the Government pro- posed—an honest, genuine remedy such as labour exchanges and other means by which step by step, they intended if they could not get to the root of the whole thing, to reduce unemployment to a minimum, and to give a better chance to all capable people throughout the country and even to make things a little better for the unfortunate and those who for one reason or another were less capable and efficient. There they bad the two programmes and he believed that during the autumn campaign they would be able to give a good account of themselves both with regard to the work the Government had been doing and the programme of positive, honest, thorough-going work they intended to carry out before this Parliament ended its days. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SPEECH BY MRS. CROSFIELD.</head>
        <p>SPEECH BY MRS. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>In response to the unmistakable desire of the meet- ing, Mrs. CROSFIELD was prevailed upon to say a few words. She remarked that had she known that her husband was going to make such a long speech and tire them out, she would have made one instead. (Laughter.) As it was, she did not see why she should indict a second one upon them. (Renewed laughter.) She could only tell thorn how much pleasurp it bad given her to to present the prizes, and she hoped on another. occailion she would have the pleasure of assisting at their billiard tournaments and bowling maint. (Applause). She would enjoy that very much indeed, because she was something of a sportswoman. She was Particularly fond of tennis, and she had also done a little at billiards—so she would like to come and have a game with them sometimes, if they would have her. (Laughter and applause). She thanked them very heartily for their kind reception. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>THANKING THE CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. HOWES in proposing a vote of thanks to the chairman, said that during the last general election Alderman Tinnion rendered him very valuable services and he was looking forward to relying upon his advice And support when the next contest came. They in Warrington were not only going to defend Free Trade but they were going to show why it was opposed. He did not believe some of the leaders of the Tory Party believed in Protection at all. Free Trade had been such a gigantic success that a late great Tory leader--the greatest Tory leader of modern times—described Protection in language which he (Mr. Howes) could not repeat in the presence of ladies. (Laughter). However, the present generation of Tories were trying to revive it. He did not think Mr. Balfour believed in Protection at all and he thought it was a God-send to his party when the Dreadnought question came to the fore. However, the •Liberal party had spiked his guns in that respect and now he fell back very reluctantly on Protection. What lay behind the present agitition was the dread of the coming fights on great social questions. The present administration was the greatest Government that ever existed and its works were of the most far-reaching description. Mr. Howes concluded: We have a stilendid Government and a splendid cause, a splendid candidate with a splendid wife, and if we cannot win with such weapons as those we don't deserve to win. (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded and the meeting closed.</p>
        <p>FOWLS (couple), 5s to 5s 6d. SOLES, Is Bd. DUCKS (couple), 6s to 7s. LEMON SOLES, 10d. BEEF, 51d to 10d. lb. LOBSTERS, is 6d. MUTTON, 5d to 9id lb. MACKEREL, 4d. PORE, 6d to 9d. HALIBUT, 10d. LAMB, 9d to is HAKE, 6d. VEAL, Gd to 10d. TROUT, Is 4d. WHEAT.-59 6d to 55,9 d per 701bs. OATS.-3s to 3514 per 451bs. WHEAT STRAW.-328 6d per ton. OAT STRAW.-305 per ton. HAY.—Best Clover, £3 2s Gd to £3 17s 6d. per tcn. meadow bay, £2 15s per ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-05-29_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NEW LANCASHIRE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>NEW LANCASHIRE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Local Appointments.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor of the Duchy of Lancaster, on the nomination of the Lord Lieutenant of the County, has appointed the following gentlemen to the Commission of the peace for the County Palatine of Lancaster : Alfred Allen Booth, Allerton •, George Christopher Clayton. Wavertree ; Sir David Gamble, St. He/ens ;' Alfred Tattersall Gibson, Fleetwood; Thomas Glover, MY., St. Helens; John Green, St. Helens; Alfred Grundy, Bolton; Henry Halstead, Haslingden ; James Hamilton, Garswood Old Hall Farm, near Wigan; James Hammill, Eccleston, Prescot ; Joseph Bithell Leech, Hightown ; David Lewis,Birch- field-road, Widnes • William Mitchell, Brookfield House, Golborne liodolph Blackburne Neilson, of .6, Princes Avenue, Liverpool ; Samuel Owens, the Cliff, Appleton, Widnes ; John Parkinson; Poulton-le-Fylde -; Alfred Cecil Pilkington, Rainhill ; Lionel Edward Pilkington, Newton-le•Willows ; William Potts, Poulton-le-Fylde; Henry Roberts,Kingsley,Warringtson; Andrew. John Speedon, Hightown ; Frederick Joseph Thompson, Fleetwood; ...James Wallace, Lytham; William Tyson Wilson, Bolton.'</p>
      </div>
